{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JFe86BB5ivDgXVMaPPFb1z","type":"tool_result","content":"386\t\n387\t**Footsteps (WP 13.22)**: which step a surface tag plays at a gait, sound data a scene's sound map reads sim-side.\n388\t`footstepSound`, `stepGait`, `SURFACE_STEPS`, `STEP_GAITS`; types `StepGait`.\n389\t\n390\tPages only, from engine/index.ts:\n391\t\n392\t**Headless**: the engine without a page, and its inspector (window.__engine in a page).\n393\t`createHeadless`; types `Headless`, `HeadlessOptions`, `Inspector`, `EngineInfo`, `EntitySummary`.\n394\t\n395\t**Materials and procedural textures (WP 2.3)**: materials as data with three.js's classic parameters, seeded tiling texture generators and their helpers, the CPU bake, and the page's material library.\n396\t`defineMaterial`, `getMaterial`, `createMaterialLibrary`, `defineTexture`, `bakeTexture`, `cellHash`, `wrapIndex`, `palette`, `pickTone`, `runningBond`, `tileNoise`; types `MaterialEntry`, `MaterialSpec`, `MaterialLibrary`, `TextureEntry`, `Texel`, `TextureContext`, `TextureSpec`, `TextureLook`, `TextureBake`, `TextureRef`.\n397\t\n398\t**Input (WP 3.3)**: the devices of a page sampled into intents (keys by physical code, mouse with pointer lock and the wheel, gamepads in any slot), action maps and their presets, bindings players change and keep, prompts per device, the virtual device that __engine.input drives, and the scripted gamepad for tests.\n399\t`createInput`, `defineActionMap`, `getActionMap`, `listActionMaps`, `SYSTEM_ACTIONS`, `RESERVED_CODES`, `createBindings`, `codeLabel`, `readKeyboardLayout`, `isCode`, `createVirtualDevice`, `connectVirtualDevice`, `createScriptedGamepad`; types `Input`, `InputOptions`, `CameraLike`, `CameraInputSample`, `ActionMap`, `ActionMapSpec`, `Bindings`, `BindingsOptions`, `BindingsStorage`, `BindingsFile`, `RebindResult`, `DeviceKind`, `VirtualDevice`, `ScriptedGamepad`, `GamepadLike`.\n400\t\n401\t**Aiming and look (WP 13.9)**: the point the intents' aim carries by a map's rule, and the look settings input.*.\n402\t`aimPoint`, `screenRay`, `onFloor`, `AIM_RULES`, `AIM_RANGE`, `STICK_REACH`, `lookOptions`, `scaleLook`, `LOOK_SETTING_FIELDS`, `LOOK_SETTINGS`, `DEGREES_PER_COUNT`; types `AimRule`, `AimPose`, `AimView`, `AimDevices`, `AimRay`, `LookOptions`, `RawLook`, `ScaledLook`.\n403\t\n404\t**Held bindings (WP 13.20)**: a key or a button kept down for an action of its own (Apex's pad reload on X held), and the device a code is of.\n405\t`holdCode`, `heldBase`, `HOLD_PREFIX`, `HOLD_TIME`, `deviceOf`.\n406\t\n407\t**Geometry kit and level meshes (WP 2.4)**: shapes standing on the ground with UVs in metres, the geometry registry (props and levels name geometry by id), their QA measures, and a compiled level drawn as meshes.\n408\t`boxGeometry`, `roundedBoxGeometry`, `limbGeometry`, `rampGeometry`, `frustumGeometry`, `pillarGeometry`, `pillarParts`, `ringGeometry`, `sidesThenTops`, `mergeGeometries`, `defineGeometry`, `getGeometry`, `buildGeometry`, `inspectGeometry`, `createLevelMeshes`, `LEVEL_RECIPES`, `createTerrainMeshes`; types `BoxOptions`, `RoundedBoxOptions`, `LimbOptions`, `RampOptions`, `FrustumOptions`, `PillarOptions`, `PillarPart`, `RingOptions`, `GeometryEntry`, `GeometrySpec`, `GeometryRef`, `GeometryReport`, `LevelMeshes`, `LevelMeshesOptions`, `LevelMeshPart`, `LevelCell`, `LevelRecipe`, `LevelShape`, `TerrainMeshes`, `TerrainMeshesOptions`.\n409\t\n410\t**Sound data (WP 9.2)**: effects and songs as registry entries (voices, layers, variants; tracker-notation tracks), rendered offline by the DSP core into reproducible samples.\n411\t`defineSfx`, `getSfx`, `renderSfx`, `sfxVoices`, `defineSong`, `getSong`, `renderSong`, `songVoices`, `songTiming`; types `Sfx`, `SfxSpec`, `SfxLayer`, `SfxBus`, `SfxRenderOptions`, `Song`, `SongSpec`, `SongTrack`, `SongTiming`, `SongRenderOptions`, `Voice`, `SoundDef`.\n412\t\n413\t**The audio runtime (WP 9.3)**: a page's sound through Web Audio, started by the first gesture: buses with ducking and voice limits, sounds placed in 3D and occluded through the QueryView, room reverb, and music in intensity layers that change on the beat and the bar line.\n414\t`createAudio`, `BUS_VOLUMES`, `ROOMS`, `roomReverb`, `VOICE_LIMITS`; types `Audio`, `AudioOptions`, `AudioState`, `AudioInfo`, `PlayOptions`, `Sound`, `ListenerPose`, `GestureTarget`, `AudioBus`, `MixerInfo`, `MusicChange`, `MusicPosition`, `Quantize`, `RoomName`, `RoomSpec`, `VoiceLimits`.\n415\t\n416\t**Sound for places (WP 13.22)**: a room's reverb out of its size and what it is made of (Sabine's RT60), the walls between a sound and the listener counted, each muffling it more.\n417\t`roomAcoustics`, `ABSORPTION`, `CONTENTS`, `wallsBetween`, `muffling`, `OCCLUSION`; types `RoomMake`.\n418\t\n419\t**The engine in a page (WP 2.7)**: createEngine reads the routes, starts the scene with its view, publishes window.__engine, warms up and runs the frame loop; createHeadless({ view: true }) builds the same view in Node.\n420\t`createEngine`, `parseRoutes`, `routeQuery`; types `Engine`, `EngineOptions`, `Routes`, `CameraInfo`, `PlayResult`, `PlayIntoOptions`, `ViewChange`.\n421\t\n422\t**A page's own presentation (WP 9.5)**: createEngine's onFrame, once per drawn frame, with the run to read and the camera.\n423\ttypes `EngineFrame`, `FrameWorld`, `CameraPose`.\n424\t\n425\t**Touch controls (WP 3.12; built, not tested, ADR-0021)**: a page's touch layout (the corner fan's buttons and the menu button) for createEngine's touchLayout and onMenu, the touch device on input.touch, and the touch codes it writes.\n426\t`defineTouchLayout`, `getTouchLayout`, `touchCode`; types `TouchLayout`, `TouchLayoutSpec`, `TouchButtonSpec`, `TouchDevice`, `StickView`, `TouchSetting`.\n427\t\n428\t**Level meshes v2 and the cutaway (WP 4.4)**: galleries, stairs, the dais, pillars with plinths and capitals and brazier stands as recipe shapes; walls facing an outside camera cut low and pillars before the hero as stumps.\n429\t`createCutaway`, `planCutaway`; types `Cutaway`, `CutawayReport`, `CutTarget`, `LevelCut`, `CutWhen`, `PartCut`.\n430\t\n431\t**Instancing and puppets (WP 3.8)**: batches of instanced copies in pages above r182's 1,000-slot uniform path; puppet bodies as data (one line per part on the humanoid skeleton's bones, mirrored parts, material slots), compiled per build and drawn as one rigid-skinned mesh per character or as instanced parts for crowds; blob shadows.\n432\t`createInstancedBatch`, `defineBody`, `getBody`, `compileBody`, `recolor`, `createPuppet`, `createPuppetBatches`, `createBlobShadows`, `blobScale`; types `InstancedBatch`, `InstancedBatchOptions`, `InstancedBatchStats`, `InstanceColor`, `Body`, `BodySpec`, `BodyPart`, `BodyPoint`, `BodyLength`, `PartOptions`, `CompiledBody`, `BodyPiece`, `PieceShape`, `Puppet`, `PuppetMaterials`, `PuppetBatches`, `BlobShadows`.\n433\t\n434\t**The HTML/CSS overlay (WP 6.5)**: world-anchored labels and damage numbers placed from projected coordinates each frame (three.js's CSS2DRenderer pattern), HUD lines and a crosshair over the canvas; ui.state() for tests.\n435\t`createOverlay`, `DAMAGE_NUMBERS`, `uiState`; types `Overlay`, `OverlayOptions`, `HudOptions`, `HudPlace`, `Label`, `LabelOptions`, `DamageOptions`, `WorldPoint`, `Anchor`, `OverlayCamera`, `UiState`, `UiElementState`.\n436\t\n437\t**The widgets (WP 10.6)**: bars, hearts and boxes, the dialog box and the menu as HTML/CSS over the overlay, reading the page's input (its system layer) and asking the page for sound cues; a gamepad's cursor over any panel. A menu sends the picks that change play as input (`send`), so replays hold them.\n438\t`createBar`, `createHearts`, `createBox`, `createPadCursor`, `createDialog`, `createMenu`.\n439\t\n440\t**The store (WP 10.9)**: what a game keeps across sessions (high scores, progress, its settings) in the page's localStorage under the game's prefix, versioned and checked on load; memory in Node and in the isolated modes.\n441\t`createStore`, `memoryBackend`.\n442\t\n443\t**Lights, shadows, atmosphere and sky (WP 7.1)**: light kinds for a level's light spots, a fixed pool of point lights near the focus with a shadow budget by quality tier, and sky presets (background, dome, backdrop, sun, ambient light, fog) that a sky puts on a scene.\n444\t`defineLight`, `defineSky`, `createSky`; types `LightEntry`, `LightSource`, `SkyEntry`, `SkySpec`, `Sky`.\n445\t\n446\t**Materials v2 (WP 7.2)**: the same material data drawn in four styles (lambert, toon, flat, pbr), switched at run time with every style warmed; the toon look (bands and rim) as uniforms; outline shells for meshes and instanced batches.\n447\t`createStyleSet`, `MATERIAL_STYLES`, `TOON_LOOK`, `createOutline`, `OUTLINE_MODES`; types `MaterialStyle`, `StyleSet`, `StyleSetOptions`, `ToonLook`, `ToonUniforms`, `Outline`, `OutlineMode`, `OutlineOptions`, `OutlineShells`.\n448\t\n449\t**Post-processing (WP 7.4, 13.32)**: one chain every frame draws through, every filter a uniform with an amount, scoped by part; looks as data (a material style, a filter chain, the sky, WP 7.8) that combine looks; objects tagged by part.\n450\t`createPost`, `POST_SETTINGS`, `definePostSettings`, `setPart`, `PARTS`, `defineLook`, `FILTER_FIELDS`, `FILTER_NAMES`, `SCOPES`, `PALETTES`, `STYLIZE_KINDS`; types `Post`, `PostOptions`, `Part`, `PostTag`, `LookEntry`, `LookSpec`, `FilterName`, `Scope`, `FilterChain`, `FilterSpec`, `CombineLayer`, `PostShow`.\n451\t\n452\t**Props drawn (WP 4.5)**: every prop part a batch of the instancing service, posed by its body, the flames dancing on the visual streams, the light spots flickering, for the light pool; a part's geometry and where it stands (its matrix in the prop's frame), for a prop's tests.\n453\t`createPropMeshes`, `FLICKER`, `buildPropPart`, `partMatrix`; types `PropMeshes`, `PropMeshesOptions`, `PropMeshPose`, `PropLight`.\n454\t\n455\t**Crowd rendering and pose LOD (WP 5.4)**: thousands of humanoids, blobs and floaters in a few dozen draw calls over the instancing service, each body seen through bands of detail by its distance (the animator's pose level, capes), culled by the view; the blob and floater bodies as data.\n456\t`createCrowd`, `createCrowdLod`, `CROWD_LOD`, `BLOB_BODY`, `blobBody`, `FLOATER_BODY`, `QUADRUPED_BODY`; types `Crowd`, `CrowdDraw`, `CrowdRigDraw`, `CrowdStats`, `RigBody`, `RigPart`, `RigJoints`, `RigShape`, `CrowdLod`, `CrowdLodOptions`, `CrowdLodCounts`, `CrowdBand`, `CrowdBandSpec`, `BlobFeatures`.\n457\t\n458\t**Performance counters (WP 5.5)**: what `__engine.stats()` returns, means in ms and the renderer's counts, never fps.\n459\ttypes `EngineStats`.\n460\t\n461\t**The texture library (v2, WP 7.3)**: a level's whole floor as a texture generator (define it under a game's id), with moss at the walls' feet, a rune circle on each dais and contact shadows along every block's foot.\n462\t`levelFloorTexture`; types `LevelFloorOptions`, `ContactShadowOptions`, `FloorMossOptions`, `FloorRuneOptions`.\n463\t\n464\t**Clips in pages (WP 8.4)**: three.js's AnimationClip and AnimationMixer, for presentation only (a page plays a baked clip on a puppet with clipJSON and AnimationClip.parse); the sim samples the same tracks through the clip layer. The mannequin: the Quaternius figure the library was measured on, as a body and a build.\n465\t`AnimationClip`, `AnimationMixer`, `defineMannequin`, `mannequinBody`, `MANNEQUIN_SLOTS`; types `MannequinData`.\n466\t\n467\t**Effects (WP 6.4)**: particles as data (emitters: shardfall's presets, the 2D engine's kinds, event effects), drawn with telegraphs on the floor, socket trails, bolts and camera shake by a view's effects; never part of the hash.\n468\t`createEffects`, `FX_BUDGET`, `BOLT`, `defineEmitter`, `getEmitter`, `TRAIL_TINTS`, `ScreenShake`, `SHAKE`; types `Effects`, `EffectsOptions`, `FxEventPayload`, `FxStats`, `Emitter`, `EmitterSpec`, `EmitterLayer`, `ParticleFields`, `FxLight`, `BurstOptions`, `ParticleStats`, `TrailSampleLike`.\n469\t\n470\t**The feel kit (WP 13.4)**: courses, scripted runs of a scene and the numbers measured on them (`node x feel`); the runner and its verdicts; the owner's feel panel and copy as profile.\n471\t`defineCourse`, `getCourse`, `listCourses`, `METRICS`, `runCourse`, `createFeelPanel`, `tunedSpec`, `profileText`; types `Course`, `Measure`, `Metric`, `CourseResult`, `CourseSample`, `MeasureResult`, `FeelPanel`, `FeelRow`, `FeelInspector`.\n472\t\n473\t**Camera modes (WP 2.5, WP 3.9, WP 13.8)**: a code to a camera, each mode's factory, and a game's own.\n474\t`readCameraCode`, `createCamera`, `createShoulderCamera`, `SHOULDER`, `createFollowCamera`, `FOLLOW_RIG`, `createFirstCamera`, `createChaseCamera`, `createViewCamera`, `followCamera`, `defineCamera`, `makePose`, `poseForward`, `poseRight`, `poseUp`, `poseTarget`; types `AnyCamera`, `AnyCameraSpec`, `ShoulderInput`, `ShoulderSpec`, `FollowInput`, `FollowSpec`, `FollowCamera`, `FollowSteps`, `CameraKindSpec`, `Camera`, `CameraInput`.\n475\t\n476\t**The rig a view steps (WP 13.8)**: blends on live switches, the view effects, the view settings cam.*.\n477\t`createCameraRig`, `rigOptions`, `CAMERA_SETTINGS`, `blendCameras`, `BLEND_TIME`, `createCameraEffects`, `applyEffects`, `cameraPlayer`, `apexFov`, `EFFECTS`; types `CameraRig`, `RigOptions`, `RigInput`, `CameraPlayer`, `EffectWeights`, `EffectsFrame`.\n478\t<!-- api-map:end -->\n479\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"dd7ac8f3-5551-4553-91f9-38df56ebd47c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:31.021Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/GAME-GUIDE.md","content":"\n**Footsteps (WP 13.22)**: which step a surface tag plays at a gait, sound data a scene's sound map reads sim-side.\n`footstepSound`, `stepGait`, `SURFACE_STEPS`, `STEP_GAITS`; types `StepGait`.\n\nPages only, from engine/index.ts:\n\n**Headless**: the engine without a page, and its inspector (window.__engine in a page).\n`createHeadless`; types `Headless`, `HeadlessOptions`, `Inspector`, `EngineInfo`, `EntitySummary`.\n\n**Materials and procedural textures (WP 2.3)**: materials as data with three.js's classic parameters, seeded tiling texture generators and their helpers, the CPU bake, and the page's material library.\n`defineMaterial`, `getMaterial`, `createMaterialLibrary`, `defineTexture`, `bakeTexture`, `cellHash`, `wrapIndex`, `palette`, `pickTone`, `runningBond`, `tileNoise`; types `MaterialEntry`, `MaterialSpec`, `MaterialLibrary`, `TextureEntry`, `Texel`, `TextureContext`, `TextureSpec`, `TextureLook`, `TextureBake`, `TextureRef`.\n\n**Input (WP 3.3)**: the devices of a page sampled into intents (keys by physical code, mouse with pointer lock and the wheel, gamepads in any slot), action maps and their presets, bindings players change and keep, prompts per device, the virtual device that __engine.input drives, and the scripted gamepad for tests.\n`createInput`, `defineActionMap`, `getActionMap`, `listActionMaps`, `SYSTEM_ACTIONS`, `RESERVED_CODES`, `createBindings`, `codeLabel`, `readKeyboardLayout`, `isCode`, `createVirtualDevice`, `connectVirtualDevice`, `createScriptedGamepad`; types `Input`, `InputOptions`, `CameraLike`, `CameraInputSample`, `ActionMap`, `ActionMapSpec`, `Bindings`, `BindingsOptions`, `BindingsStorage`, `BindingsFile`, `RebindResult`, `DeviceKind`, `VirtualDevice`, `ScriptedGamepad`, `GamepadLike`.\n\n**Aiming and look (WP 13.9)**: the point the intents' aim carries by a map's rule, and the look settings input.*.\n`aimPoint`, `screenRay`, `onFloor`, `AIM_RULES`, `AIM_RANGE`, `STICK_REACH`, `lookOptions`, `scaleLook`, `LOOK_SETTING_FIELDS`, `LOOK_SETTINGS`, `DEGREES_PER_COUNT`; types `AimRule`, `AimPose`, `AimView`, `AimDevices`, `AimRay`, `LookOptions`, `RawLook`, `ScaledLook`.\n\n**Held bindings (WP 13.20)**: a key or a button kept down for an action of its own (Apex's pad reload on X held), and the device a code is of.\n`holdCode`, `heldBase`, `HOLD_PREFIX`, `HOLD_TIME`, `deviceOf`.\n\n**Geometry kit and level meshes (WP 2.4)**: shapes standing on the ground with UVs in metres, the geometry registry (props and levels name geometry by id), their QA measures, and a compiled level drawn as meshes.\n`boxGeometry`, `roundedBoxGeometry`, `limbGeometry`, `rampGeometry`, `frustumGeometry`, `pillarGeometry`, `pillarParts`, `ringGeometry`, `sidesThenTops`, `mergeGeometries`, `defineGeometry`, `getGeometry`, `buildGeometry`, `inspectGeometry`, `createLevelMeshes`, `LEVEL_RECIPES`, `createTerrainMeshes`; types `BoxOptions`, `RoundedBoxOptions`, `LimbOptions`, `RampOptions`, `FrustumOptions`, `PillarOptions`, `PillarPart`, `RingOptions`, `GeometryEntry`, `GeometrySpec`, `GeometryRef`, `GeometryReport`, `LevelMeshes`, `LevelMeshesOptions`, `LevelMeshPart`, `LevelCell`, `LevelRecipe`, `LevelShape`, `TerrainMeshes`, `TerrainMeshesOptions`.\n\n**Sound data (WP 9.2)**: effects and songs as registry entries (voices, layers, variants; tracker-notation tracks), rendered offline by the DSP core into reproducible samples.\n`defineSfx`, `getSfx`, `renderSfx`, `sfxVoices`, `defineSong`, `getSong`, `renderSong`, `songVoices`, `songTiming`; types `Sfx`, `SfxSpec`, `SfxLayer`, `SfxBus`, `SfxRenderOptions`, `Song`, `SongSpec`, `SongTrack`, `SongTiming`, `SongRenderOptions`, `Voice`, `SoundDef`.\n\n**The audio runtime (WP 9.3)**: a page's sound through Web Audio, started by the first gesture: buses with ducking and voice limits, sounds placed in 3D and occluded through the QueryView, room reverb, and music in intensity layers that change on the beat and the bar line.\n`createAudio`, `BUS_VOLUMES`, `ROOMS`, `roomReverb`, `VOICE_LIMITS`; types `Audio`, `AudioOptions`, `AudioState`, `AudioInfo`, `PlayOptions`, `Sound`, `ListenerPose`, `GestureTarget`, `AudioBus`, `MixerInfo`, `MusicChange`, `MusicPosition`, `Quantize`, `RoomName`, `RoomSpec`, `VoiceLimits`.\n\n**Sound for places (WP 13.22)**: a room's reverb out of its size and what it is made of (Sabine's RT60), the walls between a sound and the listener counted, each muffling it more.\n`roomAcoustics`, `ABSORPTION`, `CONTENTS`, `wallsBetween`, `muffling`, `OCCLUSION`; types `RoomMake`.\n\n**The engine in a page (WP 2.7)**: createEngine reads the routes, starts the scene with its view, publishes window.__engine, warms up and runs the frame loop; createHeadless({ view: true }) builds the same view in Node.\n`createEngine`, `parseRoutes`, `routeQuery`; types `Engine`, `EngineOptions`, `Routes`, `CameraInfo`, `PlayResult`, `PlayIntoOptions`, `ViewChange`.\n\n**A page's own presentation (WP 9.5)**: createEngine's onFrame, once per drawn frame, with the run to read and the camera.\ntypes `EngineFrame`, `FrameWorld`, `CameraPose`.\n\n**Touch controls (WP 3.12; built, not tested, ADR-0021)**: a page's touch layout (the corner fan's buttons and the menu button) for createEngine's touchLayout and onMenu, the touch device on input.touch, and the touch codes it writes.\n`defineTouchLayout`, `getTouchLayout`, `touchCode`; types `TouchLayout`, `TouchLayoutSpec`, `TouchButtonSpec`, `TouchDevice`, `StickView`, `TouchSetting`.\n\n**Level meshes v2 and the cutaway (WP 4.4)**: galleries, stairs, the dais, pillars with plinths and capitals and brazier stands as recipe shapes; walls facing an outside camera cut low and pillars before the hero as stumps.\n`createCutaway`, `planCutaway`; types `Cutaway`, `CutawayReport`, `CutTarget`, `LevelCut`, `CutWhen`, `PartCut`.\n\n**Instancing and puppets (WP 3.8)**: batches of instanced copies in pages above r182's 1,000-slot uniform path; puppet bodies as data (one line per part on the humanoid skeleton's bones, mirrored parts, material slots), compiled per build and drawn as one rigid-skinned mesh per character or as instanced parts for crowds; blob shadows.\n`createInstancedBatch`, `defineBody`, `getBody`, `compileBody`, `recolor`, `createPuppet`, `createPuppetBatches`, `createBlobShadows`, `blobScale`; types `InstancedBatch`, `InstancedBatchOptions`, `InstancedBatchStats`, `InstanceColor`, `Body`, `BodySpec`, `BodyPart`, `BodyPoint`, `BodyLength`, `PartOptions`, `CompiledBody`, `BodyPiece`, `PieceShape`, `Puppet`, `PuppetMaterials`, `PuppetBatches`, `BlobShadows`.\n\n**The HTML/CSS overlay (WP 6.5)**: world-anchored labels and damage numbers placed from projected coordinates each frame (three.js's CSS2DRenderer pattern), HUD lines and a crosshair over the canvas; ui.state() for tests.\n`createOverlay`, `DAMAGE_NUMBERS`, `uiState`; types `Overlay`, `OverlayOptions`, `HudOptions`, `HudPlace`, `Label`, `LabelOptions`, `DamageOptions`, `WorldPoint`, `Anchor`, `OverlayCamera`, `UiState`, `UiElementState`.\n\n**The widgets (WP 10.6)**: bars, hearts and boxes, the dialog box and the menu as HTML/CSS over the overlay, reading the page's input (its system layer) and asking the page for sound cues; a gamepad's cursor over any panel. A menu sends the picks that change play as input (`send`), so replays hold them.\n`createBar`, `createHearts`, `createBox`, `createPadCursor`, `createDialog`, `createMenu`.\n\n**The store (WP 10.9)**: what a game keeps across sessions (high scores, progress, its settings) in the page's localStorage under the game's prefix, versioned and checked on load; memory in Node and in the isolated modes.\n`createStore`, `memoryBackend`.\n\n**Lights, shadows, atmosphere and sky (WP 7.1)**: light kinds for a level's light spots, a fixed pool of point lights near the focus with a shadow budget by quality tier, and sky presets (background, dome, backdrop, sun, ambient light, fog) that a sky puts on a scene.\n`defineLight`, `defineSky`, `createSky`; types `LightEntry`, `LightSource`, `SkyEntry`, `SkySpec`, `Sky`.\n\n**Materials v2 (WP 7.2)**: the same material data drawn in four styles (lambert, toon, flat, pbr), switched at run time with every style warmed; the toon look (bands and rim) as uniforms; outline shells for meshes and instanced batches.\n`createStyleSet`, `MATERIAL_STYLES`, `TOON_LOOK`, `createOutline`, `OUTLINE_MODES`; types `MaterialStyle`, `StyleSet`, `StyleSetOptions`, `ToonLook`, `ToonUniforms`, `Outline`, `OutlineMode`, `OutlineOptions`, `OutlineShells`.\n\n**Post-processing (WP 7.4, 13.32)**: one chain every frame draws through, every filter a uniform with an amount, scoped by part; looks as data (a material style, a filter chain, the sky, WP 7.8) that combine looks; objects tagged by part.\n`createPost`, `POST_SETTINGS`, `definePostSettings`, `setPart`, `PARTS`, `defineLook`, `FILTER_FIELDS`, `FILTER_NAMES`, `SCOPES`, `PALETTES`, `STYLIZE_KINDS`; types `Post`, `PostOptions`, `Part`, `PostTag`, `LookEntry`, `LookSpec`, `FilterName`, `Scope`, `FilterChain`, `FilterSpec`, `CombineLayer`, `PostShow`.\n\n**Props drawn (WP 4.5)**: every prop part a batch of the instancing service, posed by its body, the flames dancing on the visual streams, the light spots flickering, for the light pool; a part's geometry and where it stands (its matrix in the prop's frame), for a prop's tests.\n`createPropMeshes`, `FLICKER`, `buildPropPart`, `partMatrix`; types `PropMeshes`, `PropMeshesOptions`, `PropMeshPose`, `PropLight`.\n\n**Crowd rendering and pose LOD (WP 5.4)**: thousands of humanoids, blobs and floaters in a few dozen draw calls over the instancing service, each body seen through bands of detail by its distance (the animator's pose level, capes), culled by the view; the blob and floater bodies as data.\n`createCrowd`, `createCrowdLod`, `CROWD_LOD`, `BLOB_BODY`, `blobBody`, `FLOATER_BODY`, `QUADRUPED_BODY`; types `Crowd`, `CrowdDraw`, `CrowdRigDraw`, `CrowdStats`, `RigBody`, `RigPart`, `RigJoints`, `RigShape`, `CrowdLod`, `CrowdLodOptions`, `CrowdLodCounts`, `CrowdBand`, `CrowdBandSpec`, `BlobFeatures`.\n\n**Performance counters (WP 5.5)**: what `__engine.stats()` returns, means in ms and the renderer's counts, never fps.\ntypes `EngineStats`.\n\n**The texture library (v2, WP 7.3)**: a level's whole floor as a texture generator (define it under a game's id), with moss at the walls' feet, a rune circle on each dais and contact shadows along every block's foot.\n`levelFloorTexture`; types `LevelFloorOptions`, `ContactShadowOptions`, `FloorMossOptions`, `FloorRuneOptions`.\n\n**Clips in pages (WP 8.4)**: three.js's AnimationClip and AnimationMixer, for presentation only (a page plays a baked clip on a puppet with clipJSON and AnimationClip.parse); the sim samples the same tracks through the clip layer. The mannequin: the Quaternius figure the library was measured on, as a body and a build.\n`AnimationClip`, `AnimationMixer`, `defineMannequin`, `mannequinBody`, `MANNEQUIN_SLOTS`; types `MannequinData`.\n\n**Effects (WP 6.4)**: particles as data (emitters: shardfall's presets, the 2D engine's kinds, event effects), drawn with telegraphs on the floor, socket trails, bolts and camera shake by a view's effects; never part of the hash.\n`createEffects`, `FX_BUDGET`, `BOLT`, `defineEmitter`, `getEmitter`, `TRAIL_TINTS`, `ScreenShake`, `SHAKE`; types `Effects`, `EffectsOptions`, `FxEventPayload`, `FxStats`, `Emitter`, `EmitterSpec`, `EmitterLayer`, `ParticleFields`, `FxLight`, `BurstOptions`, `ParticleStats`, `TrailSampleLike`.\n\n**The feel kit (WP 13.4)**: courses, scripted runs of a scene and the numbers measured on them (`node x feel`); the runner and its verdicts; the owner's feel panel and copy as profile.\n`defineCourse`, `getCourse`, `listCourses`, `METRICS`, `runCourse`, `createFeelPanel`, `tunedSpec`, `profileText`; types `Course`, `Measure`, `Metric`, `CourseResult`, `CourseSample`, `MeasureResult`, `FeelPanel`, `FeelRow`, `FeelInspector`.\n\n**Camera modes (WP 2.5, WP 3.9, WP 13.8)**: a code to a camera, each mode's factory, and a game's own.\n`readCameraCode`, `createCamera`, `createShoulderCamera`, `SHOULDER`, `createFollowCamera`, `FOLLOW_RIG`, `createFirstCamera`, `createChaseCamera`, `createViewCamera`, `followCamera`, `defineCamera`, `makePose`, `poseForward`, `poseRight`, `poseUp`, `poseTarget`; types `AnyCamera`, `AnyCameraSpec`, `ShoulderInput`, `ShoulderSpec`, `FollowInput`, `FollowSpec`, `FollowCamera`, `FollowSteps`, `CameraKindSpec`, `Camera`, `CameraInput`.\n\n**The rig a view steps (WP 13.8)**: blends on live switches, the view effects, the view settings cam.*.\n`createCameraRig`, `rigOptions`, `CAMERA_SETTINGS`, `blendCameras`, `BLEND_TIME`, `createCameraEffects`, `applyEffects`, `cameraPlayer`, `apexFov`, `EFFECTS`; types `CameraRig`, `RigOptions`, `RigInput`, `CameraPlayer`, `EffectWeights`, `EffectsFrame`.\n<!-- api-map:end -->\n","numLines":94,"startLine":386,"totalLines":479}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JFe86BB5ivDgXVMaPPFb1z","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94BvqmjueFQBpevq4z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VQtRJ9viopHGbGnhSEgYwf","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/tools/game/template/AGENTS.md.tmpl"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31590,"cache_read_input_tokens":49447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31590},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"65f4a5fa-cfab-4bbd-9da8-fc73de42b45b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:32.458Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94BcjkJhhN5akYzWx4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VQtRJ9viopHGbGnhSEgYwf":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/tools/game/template/AGENTS.md.tmpl"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VQtRJ9viopHGbGnhSEgYwf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AGENTS.md: {{name}}\n2\t\n3\t{{name}} is a game made with my-3dge, a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js on\n4\tWebGPU, Rapier physics, gameplay reproducible on the CPU and checked without a screen, the UI in HTML/CSS. The engine\n5\tis in this repository, pinned to the commit engine-lock.json names; you make the game.\n6\t\n7\tBefore your first change, read docs/GAME-GUIDE.md once: how a game is built on this engine, the commands that prove\n8\tit works, and where to look things up. Then grow the game from the starter in labs/game/, which already plays, wins\n9\tthrough its bot, and passes its tests.\n10\t\n11\t## Set up (once per machine)\n12\t\n13\t    bash scripts/setup.sh\n14\t\n15\tIt installs Node 24 when missing (Linux x64), Chromium for the browser commands when missing, then `npm ci`. Plain\n16\t`npm ci` is enough for everything except `x shot`, `x film` and `x replay --browser`.\n17\t\n18\t## The loop\n19\t\n20\t1. Edit the game in labs/game/: sim-side code (the rules) in labs/game/scenes/ and labs/game/cast/, the page in\n21\t   labs/game/main.ts.\n22\t2. `npm run check`: types, lint, format, the asset rules, and the engine unchanged.\n23\t3. `npm test`: the game's tests (its replay's golden hashes, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds).\n24\t4. See it without a screen: `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` (the hash and the events), `node x shot labs/game`\n25\t   (what the page draws, as numbers and a text thumbnail), `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim` (the\n26\t   goldens, in Node and Chromium). `node x help` lists every command; each prints its verdict first and writes\n27\t   out/<cmd>/…/report.json.\n28\t5. Commit, and keep labs/game/README.md current: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n29\t\n30\t`npm run dev` serves the game at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ for a person to play.\n31\t\n32\t## Rules\n33\t\n34\t- **The engine is read-only.** engine/, tools/, data/, labs/box/ (the engine's worked example), scripts/setup.sh, the\n35\t  configs and the docs it shipped: engine-lock.json lists every one, and `npm run check` fails when one changes. When\n36\t  the engine is in your way, work round it in the game and write what you needed in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md. Only when no\n37\t  workaround exists, patch the engine file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" there, with why.\n38\t- **Only the public API.** Pages import engine/index.ts; sim-side code imports engine/sim-api.ts (lint enforces it).\n39\t- **Reproducible play.** Sim-side code reads no clock, no `Math.random`, no DOM: randomness comes from named streams\n40\t  (`w.rng('loot')`), time is whole 60 Hz steps. The same intents and seed give the same hashes, in Node and Chromium.\n41\t- **Assets are code or text first**: geometry, textures, materials and sounds as data. A binary file only with the\n42\t  owner's approval, listed in APPROVED-BINARIES.json with their answer (`npm run check` fails one unlisted); fonts are\n43\t  approved, anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them.\n44\t- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines.\n45\t- **Ask the owner** before anything outward or costly: a binary asset, a new npm dependency, spending money,\n46\t  publishing. No answer within 15 minutes: take the safer choice, note it in labs/game/README.md, carry on.\n47\t- Update the engine only when the owner asks: `node x engine --update`.\n48\t\n49\t## Done means\n50\t\n51\t`npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the bot still wins (teach it each new rule); a change that alters play on\n52\tpurpose records the replay's goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) with the reason\n53\tin the commit; labs/game/README.md says what the game is now.\n54\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"a5c25139-7909-467e-ac67-35600dffd6b6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:32.468Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/tools/game/template/AGENTS.md.tmpl","content":"# AGENTS.md: {{name}}\n\n{{name}} is a game made with my-3dge, a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js on\nWebGPU, Rapier physics, gameplay reproducible on the CPU and checked without a screen, the UI in HTML/CSS. The engine\nis in this repository, pinned to the commit engine-lock.json names; you make the game.\n\nBefore your first change, read docs/GAME-GUIDE.md once: how a game is built on this engine, the commands that prove\nit works, and where to look things up. Then grow the game from the starter in labs/game/, which already plays, wins\nthrough its bot, and passes its tests.\n\n## Set up (once per machine)\n\n    bash scripts/setup.sh\n\nIt installs Node 24 when missing (Linux x64), Chromium for the browser commands when missing, then `npm ci`. Plain\n`npm ci` is enough for everything except `x shot`, `x film` and `x replay --browser`.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. Edit the game in labs/game/: sim-side code (the rules) in labs/game/scenes/ and labs/game/cast/, the page in\n   labs/game/main.ts.\n2. `npm run check`: types, lint, format, the asset rules, and the engine unchanged.\n3. `npm test`: the game's tests (its replay's golden hashes, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds).\n4. See it without a screen: `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` (the hash and the events), `node x shot labs/game`\n   (what the page draws, as numbers and a text thumbnail), `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim` (the\n   goldens, in Node and Chromium). `node x help` lists every command; each prints its verdict first and writes\n   out/<cmd>/…/report.json.\n5. Commit, and keep labs/game/README.md current: what the game is, how it plays, the calls you made.\n\n`npm run dev` serves the game at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ for a person to play.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **The engine is read-only.** engine/, tools/, data/, labs/box/ (the engine's worked example), scripts/setup.sh, the\n  configs and the docs it shipped: engine-lock.json lists every one, and `npm run check` fails when one changes. When\n  the engine is in your way, work round it in the game and write what you needed in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md. Only when no\n  workaround exists, patch the engine file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" there, with why.\n- **Only the public API.** Pages import engine/index.ts; sim-side code imports engine/sim-api.ts (lint enforces it).\n- **Reproducible play.** Sim-side code reads no clock, no `Math.random`, no DOM: randomness comes from named streams\n  (`w.rng('loot')`), time is whole 60 Hz steps. The same intents and seed give the same hashes, in Node and Chromium.\n- **Assets are code or text first**: geometry, textures, materials and sounds as data. A binary file only with the\n  owner's approval, listed in APPROVED-BINARIES.json with their answer (`npm run check` fails one unlisted); fonts are\n  approved, anywhere in labs/game/ with their license beside them.\n- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines.\n- **Ask the owner** before anything outward or costly: a binary asset, a new npm dependency, spending money,\n  publishing. No answer within 15 minutes: take the safer choice, note it in labs/game/README.md, carry on.\n- Update the engine only when the owner asks: `node x engine --update`.\n\n## Done means\n\n`npm run check` and `npm test` pass; the bot still wins (teach it each new rule); a change that alters play on\npurpose records the replay's goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) with the reason\nin the commit; labs/game/README.md says what the game is now.\n","numLines":54,"startLine":1,"totalLines":54}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VQtRJ9viopHGbGnhSEgYwf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94BvqmjueFQBpevq4z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QNL63vnGrfoYZrwNomay1A","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/HANDOFF.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31590,"cache_read_input_tokens":49447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31590},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"cc150419-2d0d-4e35-b86f-0be4b8a8593f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:33.610Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94BcjkJhhN5akYzWx4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QNL63vnGrfoYZrwNomay1A":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/HANDOFF.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QNL63vnGrfoYZrwNomay1A","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Handing off my-3dge\n2\t\n3\tFor the owner, and for an agent picking the engine up somewhere new. Where things stand is docs/PROGRESS.md; the\n4\tdecision behind this page is ADR-0024.\n5\t\n6\t## Handing the engine to game agents\n7\t\n8\t**The prompt, lean (the default).** Give every agent, from any lab, the same lines, with the game changed:\n9\t\n10\t```text\n11\tMake <the game> with the my-3dge game engine. First read this whole file (plain Markdown; download it if you can):\n12\thttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/main/lean/README.md\n13\t```\n14\t\n15\tThat is all, for a chat in a browser as much as for a coding agent. lean/README.md is one file, under 20,000 tokens by\n16\tits budget (`LEAN_README_BUDGET_BYTES`, tools/lib/lean.ts; ADR-0024 amendments 1 and 2): the model, the rules worth\n17\tkeeping, game.html (a small game that plays), every look and filter, every built-in id, and every public name by\n18\tarea. The agent writes\n19\tthe game as one HTML page that loads the engine (lean/my3dge.js) from jsDelivr, as a page loads three.js; the page runs\n20\twhen it is opened in a browser with WebGPU. Nothing is installed, built or tested: the engine's repository tests the\n21\tpackage before every merge, so a game does not have to. An agent that cannot open links gets the same file attached\n22\t(lean/README.md).\n23\t\n24\tThe link is the raw file, not its GitHub page (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/blob/main/lean/README.md,\n25\tfor a person): the raw file is the Markdown alone, while the page wraps it in about ten times as much site, and an\n26\tagent's web tool may hand it a summary of a long page instead of every line. The game's head, with its import map,\n27\thas to be copied exactly, so \"download it if you can\" lets an agent with a shell take the file with `curl`.\n28\t\n29\t**The prompt, full workspace.** For a coding agent in a sandbox that should check its work as the engine does\n30\t(headless tests, replays, renders read as numbers):\n31\t\n32\t```text\n33\tMake <the game> with the my-3dge game engine, in its full workspace: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge\n34\tStart with the \"Make a game with my-3dge\" section of its README.\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\tThe agent runs one command in its workspace (an empty repository you made for the game, or a folder): a shallow\n38\tclone of the engine into a temporary folder, then tools/game/new-game.mjs, which writes the game:\n39\t\n40\t- the engine, copied byte for byte and pinned by `engine-lock.json` (the commit and every file's SHA-256);\n41\t- the starter game, labs/game/: a small game that plays, is won by its bot on 10 seeds and passes its tests;\n42\t- the game's own AGENTS.md (CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md import it): about 50 lines on making this game, which every\n43\t  coding agent loads by itself; docs/GAME-GUIDE.md, the engine for game makers, read once (under 15,000 tokens by\n44\t  its budget, `GUIDE_BUDGET_BYTES`);\n45\t- ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, where the agent writes what it needed from the engine and did not get.\n46\t\n47\tThen the agent sets up (`bash scripts/setup.sh`, or `npm ci`), runs `npm test`, and grows the game from the starter,\n48\tchecking its work with `npm run check`, `npm test`, `node x sim` and `node x shot`. The engine is read-only in a game:\n49\t`npm run check` fails when an engine file changes without a declared patch. An agent that cannot reach GitHub but can\n50\ttake a file: the ZIP of main (about 13 MB), https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/archive/refs/heads/main.zip, and\n51\t\"run `node <unzipped folder>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir>`\"; installing still needs npm's registry.\n52\t\n53\t**Many games at once.**\n54\t\n55\t- Lean games need nothing but a browser that reaches jsDelivr: a chat can write one, and you open it. They take the\n56\t  engine at `@main`; a game pins today's engine by putting a commit in place of `main` in its two engine URLs.\n57\t- One repository per game in the full workspace. Agents in cloud sandboxes need somewhere to push, and this repository\n58\t  takes no games: its AGENTS.md sends a game agent to the README.\n59\t- Games share nothing while they run (a workspace carries its own pinned engine, about 20 MB), so ten or a hundred\n60\t  agents work in parallel without touching each other.\n61\t- A workspace agent's sandbox must reach github.com once (the clone) and npm's registry (the install); most coding-agent\n62\t  sandboxes allow both. scripts/setup.sh also downloads, only when missing, Node 24 from nodejs.org and Chromium (Chrome\n63\t  for Testing) from storage.googleapis.com; without them a game runs on Node 22.12 or newer (Vite's floor), all but the\n64\t  browser commands. Where a platform cuts the network while the agent works, put the command in its setup step.\n65\t- Every game made the same day is on the same engine (main, as it was). A game moves to a newer engine only when you\n66\t  ask its agent: `node x engine --update`, then `npm ci`, `npm run check`, `npm test`, and its replays' goldens\n67\t  recorded again where play changed.\n68\t- To improve the engine from many games, give an engine agent in this repository the list of game repositories and\n69\t  ask it to read their ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md files and turn what recurs into work packages.\n70\t\n71\t## How it works, and why this way\n72\t\n73\tWhat a coding agent needs, whoever made it: one obvious first step; instructions that load by themselves and are\n74\tabout its job (AGENTS.md is read by most agents, CLAUDE.md by Claude Code, GEMINI.md by Gemini CLI); a working example\n75\tto change rather than a blank page; checks it runs itself, without a screen; docs to search rather than read whole;\n76\tpinned versions; and nothing that loads by itself and misleads.\n77\t\n78\tAn agent pays for what it reads, not for what is on disk: a repository's size costs nothing, while every line that\n79\tloads each session, and every file opened while finding its way, costs tokens and attention. So a game loads about\n80\t50 lines of rules and reads one guide once; docs/API.md (about 1.2 MB) and the engine's file comments are there to\n81\tgrep.\n82\t\n83\tWhy not the engine's own repository, with the game inside it: a game agent there would load the engine's rules (work\n84\tpackages, the ledger, escalation records), a setup hook that clones the projects the engine was ported from, an\n85\thour-long CI and over 5,000 engine tests, and ten agents pushing games into one repository would collide.\n86\t\n87\tWhy lean is the default (the owner's call of 2026-10-16, after a dozen trials): a library ships as three.js, Rapier\n88\tor Babylon.js do, as code and docs, without its tests and checks; and a model building in a chat answers in minutes,\n89\toften with no sandbox at all, so it cannot run an hour of tests or a toolchain. The engine's repository keeps the\n90\tchecks for itself and runs them on the package before every merge.\n91\t\n92\tWhy not hand-made packages: each would need rebuilding and retesting at every engine change, and would go stale. Here\n93\teach comes from the same checkout by rule, and is tested: the lean package is written by `node x docs --write`, held\n94\tcurrent by `x docs --check` and played in Chromium by tests/e2e/lean.spec.ts; the full workspace is made by one\n95\tcommand and proved after every merge (`node x engine --test`, in the full run); the repository, or its ZIP, is the\n96\tcomplete one.\n97\t\n98\tWhy the engine is copied into each game, not linked: it works offline, with no submodule for a cloud agent to trip\n99\ton, and it stays searchable (agents' search tools skip node_modules). The lock does what a pin would.\n100\t\n101\t## Handing over the engine itself\n102\t\n103\tFor an agent that is to keep building the engine, not a game, give the repository:\n104\t\n105\t```text\n106\tContinue developing the my-3dge game engine: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge\n107\tClone it, run `bash scripts/setup.sh`, then follow its AGENTS.md; docs/PROGRESS.md says where things stand.\n108\t```\n109\t\n110\tAGENTS.md holds the engine's rules and loads by itself in most coding agents; CLAUDE.md (Claude Code) and GEMINI.md\n111\t(Gemini CLI) import it, and Claude Code's SessionStart hook runs the setup. Every step below runs in a fresh clone:\n112\tGitHub's full run does exactly that after each merge.\n113\t\n114\t## Starting in a new environment\n115\t\n116\t```bash\n117\tgit clone https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge && cd my-3dge && bash scripts/setup.sh\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tsetup.sh installs Node 24.22.2, clones the read-only sources at their pinned commits and runs `npm ci`; CLAUDE.md\n121\tsays where Chromium comes from (the cloud container's, or setup.sh's download, ADR-0014 amendment 8). `main` holds\n122\tevery stage, one squashed commit per stage PR (the `integration` branch that kept every lane's commit was deleted on\n123\tGitHub on 2026-10-14; the PRs' pages keep each stage's commits; the owner deleted the `game/*` branches, on purpose,\n124\tADR-0023). The owner's testing budget is ADR-0022, with what the stage runs learned in its amendment 1; ADR-0023 keeps\n125\tlong tests from holding up work: merges wait on the quick gate, and the full run follows on GitHub.\n126\t\n127\t## The box against my-3d2dge's stress-world\n128\t\n129\tWhat a person opens is the playground, `playground.html` (labs/playground/README.md); this compares the Stress Box, an\n130\tengine tool, with stress-world. https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/stress-world (about 2,600 lines, a temporary lab built in\n131\thours) shows most of what the box shows: the hall, the hero's moves, the walker, slime and wisp crowd with flow fields\n132\tand attack tokens, the cel, pixel, bloom and FXAA filters, 5,000 bodies. It shows more on the surface: a full panel\n133\twith presets, live fps and a bottleneck readout, a benchmark button, monster skins, waves, the card characters and a\n134\tWebGL fallback. What my-3dge adds is underneath: audio, the 1,218-clip animation library with reactions, replays with\n135\tthe same golden hashes in Node and Chromium, over 5,000 tests, and a documented public API for game code. That only\n136\tpays off once agents build games on it. The box's menu (labs/box/menu.ts) is the first step toward the panel.\n137\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"4e69325c-3025-4ee1-94a5-99fc1762a933","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:33.618Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/HANDOFF.md","content":"# Handing off my-3dge\n\nFor the owner, and for an agent picking the engine up somewhere new. Where things stand is docs/PROGRESS.md; the\ndecision behind this page is ADR-0024.\n\n## Handing the engine to game agents\n\n**The prompt, lean (the default).** Give every agent, from any lab, the same lines, with the game changed:\n\n```text\nMake <the game> with the my-3dge game engine. First read this whole file (plain Markdown; download it if you can):\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/main/lean/README.md\n```\n\nThat is all, for a chat in a browser as much as for a coding agent. lean/README.md is one file, under 20,000 tokens by\nits budget (`LEAN_README_BUDGET_BYTES`, tools/lib/lean.ts; ADR-0024 amendments 1 and 2): the model, the rules worth\nkeeping, game.html (a small game that plays), every look and filter, every built-in id, and every public name by\narea. The agent writes\nthe game as one HTML page that loads the engine (lean/my3dge.js) from jsDelivr, as a page loads three.js; the page runs\nwhen it is opened in a browser with WebGPU. Nothing is installed, built or tested: the engine's repository tests the\npackage before every merge, so a game does not have to. An agent that cannot open links gets the same file attached\n(lean/README.md).\n\nThe link is the raw file, not its GitHub page (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/blob/main/lean/README.md,\nfor a person): the raw file is the Markdown alone, while the page wraps it in about ten times as much site, and an\nagent's web tool may hand it a summary of a long page instead of every line. The game's head, with its import map,\nhas to be copied exactly, so \"download it if you can\" lets an agent with a shell take the file with `curl`.\n\n**The prompt, full workspace.** For a coding agent in a sandbox that should check its work as the engine does\n(headless tests, replays, renders read as numbers):\n\n```text\nMake <the game> with the my-3dge game engine, in its full workspace: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge\nStart with the \"Make a game with my-3dge\" section of its README.\n```\n\nThe agent runs one command in its workspace (an empty repository you made for the game, or a folder): a shallow\nclone of the engine into a temporary folder, then tools/game/new-game.mjs, which writes the game:\n\n- the engine, copied byte for byte and pinned by `engine-lock.json` (the commit and every file's SHA-256);\n- the starter game, labs/game/: a small game that plays, is won by its bot on 10 seeds and passes its tests;\n- the game's own AGENTS.md (CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md import it): about 50 lines on making this game, which every\n  coding agent loads by itself; docs/GAME-GUIDE.md, the engine for game makers, read once (under 15,000 tokens by\n  its budget, `GUIDE_BUDGET_BYTES`);\n- ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, where the agent writes what it needed from the engine and did not get.\n\nThen the agent sets up (`bash scripts/setup.sh`, or `npm ci`), runs `npm test`, and grows the game from the starter,\nchecking its work with `npm run check`, `npm test`, `node x sim` and `node x shot`. The engine is read-only in a game:\n`npm run check` fails when an engine file changes without a declared patch. An agent that cannot reach GitHub but can\ntake a file: the ZIP of main (about 13 MB), https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/archive/refs/heads/main.zip, and\n\"run `node <unzipped folder>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir>`\"; installing still needs npm's registry.\n\n**Many games at once.**\n\n- Lean games need nothing but a browser that reaches jsDelivr: a chat can write one, and you open it. They take the\n  engine at `@main`; a game pins today's engine by putting a commit in place of `main` in its two engine URLs.\n- One repository per game in the full workspace. Agents in cloud sandboxes need somewhere to push, and this repository\n  takes no games: its AGENTS.md sends a game agent to the README.\n- Games share nothing while they run (a workspace carries its own pinned engine, about 20 MB), so ten or a hundred\n  agents work in parallel without touching each other.\n- A workspace agent's sandbox must reach github.com once (the clone) and npm's registry (the install); most coding-agent\n  sandboxes allow both. scripts/setup.sh also downloads, only when missing, Node 24 from nodejs.org and Chromium (Chrome\n  for Testing) from storage.googleapis.com; without them a game runs on Node 22.12 or newer (Vite's floor), all but the\n  browser commands. Where a platform cuts the network while the agent works, put the command in its setup step.\n- Every game made the same day is on the same engine (main, as it was). A game moves to a newer engine only when you\n  ask its agent: `node x engine --update`, then `npm ci`, `npm run check`, `npm test`, and its replays' goldens\n  recorded again where play changed.\n- To improve the engine from many games, give an engine agent in this repository the list of game repositories and\n  ask it to read their ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md files and turn what recurs into work packages.\n\n## How it works, and why this way\n\nWhat a coding agent needs, whoever made it: one obvious first step; instructions that load by themselves and are\nabout its job (AGENTS.md is read by most agents, CLAUDE.md by Claude Code, GEMINI.md by Gemini CLI); a working example\nto change rather than a blank page; checks it runs itself, without a screen; docs to search rather than read whole;\npinned versions; and nothing that loads by itself and misleads.\n\nAn agent pays for what it reads, not for what is on disk: a repository's size costs nothing, while every line that\nloads each session, and every file opened while finding its way, costs tokens and attention. So a game loads about\n50 lines of rules and reads one guide once; docs/API.md (about 1.2 MB) and the engine's file comments are there to\ngrep.\n\nWhy not the engine's own repository, with the game inside it: a game agent there would load the engine's rules (work\npackages, the ledger, escalation records), a setup hook that clones the projects the engine was ported from, an\nhour-long CI and over 5,000 engine tests, and ten agents pushing games into one repository would collide.\n\nWhy lean is the default (the owner's call of 2026-10-16, after a dozen trials): a library ships as three.js, Rapier\nor Babylon.js do, as code and docs, without its tests and checks; and a model building in a chat answers in minutes,\noften with no sandbox at all, so it cannot run an hour of tests or a toolchain. The engine's repository keeps the\nchecks for itself and runs them on the package before every merge.\n\nWhy not hand-made packages: each would need rebuilding and retesting at every engine change, and would go stale. Here\neach comes from the same checkout by rule, and is tested: the lean package is written by `node x docs --write`, held\ncurrent by `x docs --check` and played in Chromium by tests/e2e/lean.spec.ts; the full workspace is made by one\ncommand and proved after every merge (`node x engine --test`, in the full run); the repository, or its ZIP, is the\ncomplete one.\n\nWhy the engine is copied into each game, not linked: it works offline, with no submodule for a cloud agent to trip\non, and it stays searchable (agents' search tools skip node_modules). The lock does what a pin would.\n\n## Handing over the engine itself\n\nFor an agent that is to keep building the engine, not a game, give the repository:\n\n```text\nContinue developing the my-3dge game engine: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge\nClone it, run `bash scripts/setup.sh`, then follow its AGENTS.md; docs/PROGRESS.md says where things stand.\n```\n\nAGENTS.md holds the engine's rules and loads by itself in most coding agents; CLAUDE.md (Claude Code) and GEMINI.md\n(Gemini CLI) import it, and Claude Code's SessionStart hook runs the setup. Every step below runs in a fresh clone:\nGitHub's full run does exactly that after each merge.\n\n## Starting in a new environment\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge && cd my-3dge && bash scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nsetup.sh installs Node 24.22.2, clones the read-only sources at their pinned commits and runs `npm ci`; CLAUDE.md\nsays where Chromium comes from (the cloud container's, or setup.sh's download, ADR-0014 amendment 8). `main` holds\nevery stage, one squashed commit per stage PR (the `integration` branch that kept every lane's commit was deleted on\nGitHub on 2026-10-14; the PRs' pages keep each stage's commits; the owner deleted the `game/*` branches, on purpose,\nADR-0023). The owner's testing budget is ADR-0022, with what the stage runs learned in its amendment 1; ADR-0023 keeps\nlong tests from holding up work: merges wait on the quick gate, and the full run follows on GitHub.\n\n## The box against my-3d2dge's stress-world\n\nWhat a person opens is the playground, `playground.html` (labs/playground/README.md); this compares the Stress Box, an\nengine tool, with stress-world. https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/stress-world (about 2,600 lines, a temporary lab built in\nhours) shows most of what the box shows: the hall, the hero's moves, the walker, slime and wisp crowd with flow fields\nand attack tokens, the cel, pixel, bloom and FXAA filters, 5,000 bodies. It shows more on the surface: a full panel\nwith presets, live fps and a bottleneck readout, a benchmark button, monster skins, waves, the card characters and a\nWebGL fallback. What my-3dge adds is underneath: audio, the 1,218-clip animation library with reactions, replays with\nthe same golden hashes in Node and Chromium, over 5,000 tests, and a documented public API for game code. That only\npays off once agents build games on it. The box's menu (labs/box/menu.ts) is the first step toward the panel.\n","numLines":137,"startLine":1,"totalLines":137}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QNL63vnGrfoYZrwNomay1A","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94BvqmjueFQBpevq4z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EFkXFJQUV5x82cHeAM5Fq1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/labs/game/main.ts"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31590,"cache_read_input_tokens":49447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31590},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"1b4e0dc9-cb83-4447-8ae2-c0c1e5d7a26f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:34.772Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94BcjkJhhN5akYzWx4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01EFkXFJQUV5x82cHeAM5Fq1":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/labs/game/main.ts"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFkXFJQUV5x82cHeAM5Fq1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/**\n2\t * @file The starter game's page (labs/game/index.html): it starts the engine (`createEngine`, engine/app/engine.ts) on\n3\t * the scene `game:play` (labs/game/scenes/play.ts) with the arena's text registered for the view, offers the bot's\n4\t * replay to `?replay=bot`, and shows the round each drawn frame in the HTML/CSS overlay's HUD: the gems, the clock,\n5\t * the controls, the best round kept across sessions (labs/game/best.ts, through the engine's store) and, when the\n6\t * round ends, how it ended. Enter then starts a new round on the next seed. Escape (or the pad's Start) pauses the\n7\t * round under a menu (`createMenu`: Resume, New round), which keeps its keys out of play while it is open\n8\t * (`createEngine({ modal })`); the store keeps the best round and any keys rebound (`createEngine({ store })`). The\n9\t * camera is the chase camera unless the URL names one (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=orbit`).\n10\t *\n11\t * Everything else a page does (routes, the renderer, the frame loop, the devices, `window.__engine`, the error\n12\t * overlay, the touch controls) is the engine's, so this file is the game's own presentation only. It reads the run\n13\t * and never changes it: the HUD cannot change the hash.\n14\t *\n15\t * Game code: it imports only the engine's public API (engine/index.ts) and the game's own files.\n16\t *\n17\t * Open it at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ (`npm run dev`): `?seed=7&game.gems=20` plays another round,\n18\t * `?replay=bot` the bot's, `?cam=iso` another camera. Headless, `node x shot labs/game` reports what it draws.\n19\t */\n20\t/// <reference types=\"vite/client\" />\n21\timport { createEngine, createMenu, createStore, type Engine, type EngineFrame, type Menu } from '../../engine';\n22\timport { bestOf, recordBest } from './best';\n23\timport { ARENA_TEXT, play, type Round } from './scenes/play';\n24\t\n25\t/** The page's query, with the chase camera when it names none: it follows the player round the arena. */\n26\tconst search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\n27\tif (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase');\n28\t\n29\t/** The game's replays, by name (`?replay=bot`), as text loaded on demand. */\n30\tconst replays = { bot: () => import('./replays/bot.replay.json?raw').then((module) => module.default) };\n31\t\n32\t/** The started engine; null until `createEngine` resolves (its first frames are drawn before that). */\n33\tlet engine: Engine | null = null;\n34\t\n35\t/** Whether the round on screen has ended, so Enter starts the next. */\n36\tlet ended = false;\n37\t\n38\t/** The game's store: its best round across sessions; a replay's page plays in its demo mode, so it sets none. */\n39\tconst store = createStore({ prefix: 'my3dge-game:', mode: search.has('replay') ? 'demo' : 'play' });\n40\t\n41\t/** The fastest won round kept (s), and the run whose clock the page saw running, with the steps it started with. */\n42\tlet best = bestOf(store);\n43\tlet seen: { world: object; steps: number } | null = null;\n44\t\n45\t/** The pause menu while it is open; null while the round plays. */\n46\tlet paused: Menu | null = null;\n47\t\n48\t/** Opens the pause menu over the paused round, or reads it a frame: Resume (or Escape again), New round. */\n49\tfunction pauseMenu(dt: number): void {\n50\t  const [inspector, ui, input] = [engine?.inspector, engine?.ui, engine?.input];\n51\t  if (!inspector || !ui || !input) return;\n52\t  const close = () => {\n53\t    paused?.remove();\n54\t    paused = null;\n55\t    inspector.resume();\n56\t  };\n57\t  if (paused) return void paused.update(dt);\n58\t  if (!input.pressed('pause')) return;\n59\t  inspector.pause();\n60\t  const items = [\n61\t    { label: 'Resume', id: 'resume' },\n62\t    { label: 'New round', id: 'new' },\n63\t  ];\n64\t  paused = createMenu(ui.root, items, { input, id: 'pause', title: 'Paused', onCancel: close, onPick: (_i, item) => {\n65\t    if (typeof item === 'object' && item.id === 'new') inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\n66\t    close();\n67\t  } }); // prettier-ignore\n68\t}\n69\t\n70\t/** Shows the round in the HUD: the gems, the clock, the controls, and how the round ended. */\n71\tfunction showRound({ world }: EngineFrame): void {\n72\t  const ui = engine?.ui;\n73\t  const entity = world.query('round')[0] as unknown as { round: Round } | undefined;\n74\t  if (!ui || !entity) return;\n75\t  const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\n76\t  if (seen?.world !== world) seen = state === 'playing' ? { world, steps: left + world.tick } : null;\n77\t  const was = ended;\n78\t  ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\n79\t  ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color: left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '' });\n80\t  ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash · Esc: pause', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\n81\t  ended = state !== 'playing';\n82\t  if (ended && !was && state === 'won' && seen) best = recordBest(store, (seen.steps - left) / world.hz);\n83\t  ui.hud('best', best === null ? null : `Best ${best.toFixed(1)} s`, { at: 'top-left' });\n84\t  const end = state === 'won' ? 'Every gem! Enter: play again' : 'Time! Enter: play again';\n85\t  ui.hud('end', ended ? end : null, { at: 'top' });\n86\t}\n87\t\n88\tengine = await createEngine({\n89\t  canvas: document.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('#view')!,\n90\t  scenes: [play.id],\n91\t  levels: { 'level:arena': { text: ARENA_TEXT, file: 'labs/game/scenes/play.ts' } },\n92\t  replays,\n93\t  search: `?${search}`,\n94\t  store,\n95\t  modal: () => paused !== null,\n96\t  onFrame: (frame) => {\n97\t    pauseMenu(frame.dt);\n98\t    showRound(frame);\n99\t  },\n100\t});\n101\t\n102\taddEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n103\t  const inspector = engine?.inspector;\n104\t  if (event.code === 'Enter' && ended && inspector) inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\n105\t});\n106\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"8e38e070-2767-45ec-86e4-9219f5e417d2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:34.780Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/labs/game/main.ts","content":"/**\n * @file The starter game's page (labs/game/index.html): it starts the engine (`createEngine`, engine/app/engine.ts) on\n * the scene `game:play` (labs/game/scenes/play.ts) with the arena's text registered for the view, offers the bot's\n * replay to `?replay=bot`, and shows the round each drawn frame in the HTML/CSS overlay's HUD: the gems, the clock,\n * the controls, the best round kept across sessions (labs/game/best.ts, through the engine's store) and, when the\n * round ends, how it ended. Enter then starts a new round on the next seed. Escape (or the pad's Start) pauses the\n * round under a menu (`createMenu`: Resume, New round), which keeps its keys out of play while it is open\n * (`createEngine({ modal })`); the store keeps the best round and any keys rebound (`createEngine({ store })`). The\n * camera is the chase camera unless the URL names one (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=orbit`).\n *\n * Everything else a page does (routes, the renderer, the frame loop, the devices, `window.__engine`, the error\n * overlay, the touch controls) is the engine's, so this file is the game's own presentation only. It reads the run\n * and never changes it: the HUD cannot change the hash.\n *\n * Game code: it imports only the engine's public API (engine/index.ts) and the game's own files.\n *\n * Open it at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ (`npm run dev`): `?seed=7&game.gems=20` plays another round,\n * `?replay=bot` the bot's, `?cam=iso` another camera. Headless, `node x shot labs/game` reports what it draws.\n */\n/// <reference types=\"vite/client\" />\nimport { createEngine, createMenu, createStore, type Engine, type EngineFrame, type Menu } from '../../engine';\nimport { bestOf, recordBest } from './best';\nimport { ARENA_TEXT, play, type Round } from './scenes/play';\n\n/** The page's query, with the chase camera when it names none: it follows the player round the arena. */\nconst search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\nif (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase');\n\n/** The game's replays, by name (`?replay=bot`), as text loaded on demand. */\nconst replays = { bot: () => import('./replays/bot.replay.json?raw').then((module) => module.default) };\n\n/** The started engine; null until `createEngine` resolves (its first frames are drawn before that). */\nlet engine: Engine | null = null;\n\n/** Whether the round on screen has ended, so Enter starts the next. */\nlet ended = false;\n\n/** The game's store: its best round across sessions; a replay's page plays in its demo mode, so it sets none. */\nconst store = createStore({ prefix: 'my3dge-game:', mode: search.has('replay') ? 'demo' : 'play' });\n\n/** The fastest won round kept (s), and the run whose clock the page saw running, with the steps it started with. */\nlet best = bestOf(store);\nlet seen: { world: object; steps: number } | null = null;\n\n/** The pause menu while it is open; null while the round plays. */\nlet paused: Menu | null = null;\n\n/** Opens the pause menu over the paused round, or reads it a frame: Resume (or Escape again), New round. */\nfunction pauseMenu(dt: number): void {\n  const [inspector, ui, input] = [engine?.inspector, engine?.ui, engine?.input];\n  if (!inspector || !ui || !input) return;\n  const close = () => {\n    paused?.remove();\n    paused = null;\n    inspector.resume();\n  };\n  if (paused) return void paused.update(dt);\n  if (!input.pressed('pause')) return;\n  inspector.pause();\n  const items = [\n    { label: 'Resume', id: 'resume' },\n    { label: 'New round', id: 'new' },\n  ];\n  paused = createMenu(ui.root, items, { input, id: 'pause', title: 'Paused', onCancel: close, onPick: (_i, item) => {\n    if (typeof item === 'object' && item.id === 'new') inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\n    close();\n  } }); // prettier-ignore\n}\n\n/** Shows the round in the HUD: the gems, the clock, the controls, and how the round ended. */\nfunction showRound({ world }: EngineFrame): void {\n  const ui = engine?.ui;\n  const entity = world.query('round')[0] as unknown as { round: Round } | undefined;\n  if (!ui || !entity) return;\n  const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\n  if (seen?.world !== world) seen = state === 'playing' ? { world, steps: left + world.tick } : null;\n  const was = ended;\n  ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\n  ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color: left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '' });\n  ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash · Esc: pause', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\n  ended = state !== 'playing';\n  if (ended && !was && state === 'won' && seen) best = recordBest(store, (seen.steps - left) / world.hz);\n  ui.hud('best', best === null ? null : `Best ${best.toFixed(1)} s`, { at: 'top-left' });\n  const end = state === 'won' ? 'Every gem! Enter: play again' : 'Time! Enter: play again';\n  ui.hud('end', ended ? end : null, { at: 'top' });\n}\n\nengine = await createEngine({\n  canvas: document.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('#view')!,\n  scenes: [play.id],\n  levels: { 'level:arena': { text: ARENA_TEXT, file: 'labs/game/scenes/play.ts' } },\n  replays,\n  search: `?${search}`,\n  store,\n  modal: () => paused !== null,\n  onFrame: (frame) => {\n    pauseMenu(frame.dt);\n    showRound(frame);\n  },\n});\n\naddEventListener('keydown', (event) => {\n  const inspector = engine?.inspector;\n  if (event.code === 'Enter' && ended && inspector) inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\n});\n","numLines":106,"startLine":1,"totalLines":106}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EFkXFJQUV5x82cHeAM5Fq1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94BvqmjueFQBpevq4z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GtdCAPmoqXuJzpyT1DZwhq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/labs/game/scenes/play.ts"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31590,"cache_read_input_tokens":49447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31590},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"8b042706-dd81-4f36-a6e1-c313c5560d2f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:35.543Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94BcjkJhhN5akYzWx4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GtdCAPmoqXuJzpyT1DZwhq":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/labs/game/scenes/play.ts"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GtdCAPmoqXuJzpyT1DZwhq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/**\n2\t * @file The starter game's scene `game:play`: every new game begins as this one (tools/game/new-game.mjs copies\n3\t * labs/game/ into it), so it is small, plays, and shows the parts a game is made of. The player, a humanoid on the\n4\t * character controller, runs round the arena (`ARENA_TEXT` below: walls, two pillars, two low walls to jump) collecting\n5\t * the gems the seed scatters, before the clock runs out. All the gems: won; the clock at 0: lost.\n6\t *\n7\t * The parts: a level as text, compiled for its physics bodies; settings (`game.*`, read when the scene starts, so they\n8\t * change play and go in replays); components (`gem`, drawn as a small glowing box because it declares `x` and `z` in\n9\t * metres; `round`, the score and the clock); the player (labs/game/cast/player.ts, an actor that reads the step's\n10\t * intents: `move`, `jump`, `dash`); systems in the phase `rules`, after the actors' feet are read back; a named random\n11\t * stream (`gems`); events (`gem`, `won`, `lost`) that pages, sounds and tests hear. The page is labs/game/main.ts, the\n12\t * bot labs/game/bot.ts.\n13\t *\n14\t * The level's text lives here, not in a .txt file, since sim-side code reads no files; the page registers the same\n15\t * text for the view (labs/game/main.ts).\n16\t *\n17\t * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts, so `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` runs it in Node.\n18\t *\n19\t * @example\n20\t * // node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --steps 600\n21\t * // node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --set game.gems=3 --seed 7\n22\t * ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero')?.position; // [3.5, 0, 2.5]\n23\t * @see labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts\n24\t */\n25\timport {\n26\t  addLevelBodies,\n27\t  compileLevel,\n28\t  defineComponent,\n29\t  defineScene,\n30\t  defineSettings,\n31\t  physicsOf,\n32\t  type ActorComponent,\n33\t  type World,\n34\t} from '../../../engine/sim-api';\n35\timport { spawnPlayer } from '../cast/player';\n36\t\n37\t/** The arena: 18 × 14 m inside walls 3 m high, two 2 × 2 m pillars, two low walls (0.75 m) to jump, the spawn `@`. */\n38\texport const ARENA_TEXT = `---\n39\tdescription: The starter game's arena. 18 × 14 m inside walls 3 m high, two 2 × 2 m pillars, two low walls 0.75 m high that the player jumps, and the player's spawn.\n40\t---\n41\t##################\n42\t#................#\n43\t#..@.............#\n44\t#.......PP.......#\n45\t#.......PP.......#\n46\t#................#\n47\t#....w......w....#\n48\t#....w......w....#\n49\t#................#\n50\t#.......PP.......#\n51\t#.......PP.......#\n52\t#................#\n53\t#................#\n54\t##################\n55\t`;\n56\t\n57\t/** The arena, compiled: its colliders for physics, its tiles for the gems and the bot's paths. */\n58\texport const ARENA = compileLevel(ARENA_TEXT, { file: 'labs/game/scenes/play.ts', name: 'arena' });\n59\t\n60\t/** The game's settings: each is reachable through `x set`, URL parameters (`?game.gems=3`) and `__engine.set`. */\n61\texport const GAME_SETTINGS = defineSettings({\n62\t  'game.gems': {\n63\t    type: 'integer',\n64\t    default: 10,\n65\t    minimum: 1,\n66\t    maximum: 40,\n67\t    when: 'scene',\n68\t    description: 'How many gems the seed scatters.',\n69\t  },\n70\t  'game.seconds': {\n71\t    type: 'number',\n72\t    default: 30,\n73\t    minimum: 5,\n74\t    maximum: 600,\n75\t    unit: 's',\n76\t    when: 'scene',\n77\t    description: 'How long the player has to collect them all.',\n78\t  },\n79\t  'game.speed': {\n80\t    type: 'number',\n81\t    default: 6,\n82\t    minimum: 1,\n83\t    maximum: 20,\n84\t    unit: 'm/s',\n85\t    when: 'scene',\n86\t    description: 'How fast the player runs.',\n87\t  },\n88\t});\n89\t\n90\t/** A gem: where it floats and how it looks (the page draws a component that declares x and z in metres). */\n91\texport const GEM = defineComponent('gem', {\n92\t  description: 'A gem to collect: where it floats, its spin and how it is drawn.',\n93\t  fields: {\n94\t    x: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'East.' },\n95\t    y: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, unit: 'm', description: 'Up: the bottom of its box.' },\n96\t    z: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'South.' },\n97\t    yaw: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'rad', description: 'Its spin.' },\n98\t    size: { type: 'number', default: 0.4, unit: 'm', description: 'The side of its box.' },\n99\t    material: { type: 'string', default: 'material:glow', description: 'What it is drawn with.' },\n100\t  },\n101\t});\n102\t\n103\t/** The round: the score, the clock and how it ended. One entity holds it. */\n104\texport const ROUND = defineComponent('round', {\n105\t  description:\n106\t    'The round: gems collected of the total, the steps left on the clock, and whether it is playing, won or lost.',\n107\t  fields: {\n108\t    gems: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many gems the round scattered.' },\n109\t    collected: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many the player has collected.' },\n110\t    left: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'The sim steps left on the clock (60 a second).' },\n111\t    state: { type: 'string', default: 'playing', description: \"'playing', 'won' or 'lost'.\" },\n112\t  },\n113\t});\n114\t\n115\t/** The round, as the component holds it. */\n116\texport type Round = { gems: number; collected: number; left: number; state: 'playing' | 'won' | 'lost' };\n117\t\n118\t/** The scene's components, typed. */\n119\texport type GameComponents = {\n120\t  gem: { x: number; y: number; z: number; yaw: number; size: number; material: string };\n121\t  round: Round;\n122\t  actor: ActorComponent;\n123\t};\n124\t\n125\t/** How close the player's feet come to a gem to collect it, m (on the ground plane). */\n126\texport const REACH = 0.8;\n127\t\n128\t/** How far from the spawn the gems keep, m, so the first one is never collected without moving. */\n129\tconst CLEAR = 3;\n130\t\n131\t/** Where the player starts: the level's spawn `@`. */\n132\tconst START = ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero')!.position;\n133\t\n134\t/** The floor tiles a gem may float over: open floor (`.`), at least `CLEAR` metres from the spawn, in reading order. */\n135\tfunction gemTiles(): number[] {\n136\t  const tiles: number[] = [];\n137\t  ARENA.tiles.forEach((char, i) => {\n138\t    const x = ((i % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n139\t    const z = (Math.floor(i / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n140\t    if (char === '.' && Math.hypot(x - START[0], z - START[2]) >= CLEAR) tiles.push(i);\n141\t  });\n142\t  return tiles;\n143\t}\n144\t\n145\t/** Scatters `count` gems over distinct floor tiles drawn from the stream `gems`: each seed lays them its own way. */\n146\tfunction scatterGems(w: World<GameComponents>, count: number): void {\n147\t  const tiles = gemTiles();\n148\t  const rng = w.rng('gems');\n149\t  for (let k = 0; k < count && tiles.length > 0; k++) {\n150\t    const [tile] = tiles.splice(rng.int(0, tiles.length - 1), 1);\n151\t    const x = ((tile % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n152\t    const z = (Math.floor(tile / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n153\t    w.spawn({ gem: { x, z, yaw: rng.range(0, Math.PI) } });\n154\t  }\n155\t}\n156\t\n157\t/** Ends the round: the player stops reading intents (and so stands still), and the event says how it ended. */\n158\tfunction endRound(w: World<GameComponents>, round: Round, state: 'won' | 'lost'): void {\n159\t  round.state = state;\n160\t  for (const e of w.query('actor')) {\n161\t    e.actor.player = false;\n162\t    e.actor.intents = {};\n163\t  }\n164\t  w.emit(state, { collected: round.collected, gems: round.gems, left: round.left });\n165\t}\n166\t\n167\t/**\n168\t * Spins the gems, collects those an actor's feet reach, and ends the round when the last one goes. A despawn waits for\n169\t * the end of the step, so a gem taken this step is remembered: two actors never count it twice.\n170\t */\n171\tfunction collect(w: World<GameComponents>): void {\n172\t  const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n173\t  for (const gem of w.query('gem')) gem.gem.yaw += 2 * w.dt;\n174\t  if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n175\t  const taken = new Set<number>();\n176\t  for (const { actor } of w.query('actor')) {\n177\t    for (const gem of w.query('gem')) {\n178\t      if (taken.has(gem.id) || Math.hypot(gem.gem.x - actor.x, gem.gem.z - actor.z) > REACH) continue;\n179\t      taken.add(gem.id);\n180\t      w.despawn(gem.id);\n181\t      round.collected += 1;\n182\t      w.emit('gem', { id: gem.id, x: gem.gem.x, z: gem.gem.z, collected: round.collected });\n183\t    }\n184\t  }\n185\t  if (round.collected >= round.gems) endRound(w, round, 'won');\n186\t}\n187\t\n188\t/** Runs the clock down a step at a time (game time is whole steps, never summed seconds); at 0 the round is lost. */\n189\tfunction clock(w: World<GameComponents>): void {\n190\t  const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n191\t  if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n192\t  round.left = Math.max(0, round.left - 1);\n193\t  if (round.left === 0) endRound(w, round, 'lost');\n194\t}\n195\t\n196\t/** The starter game: collect every gem before the clock runs out. */\n197\texport const play = defineScene<GameComponents>('game:play', {\n198\t  description: 'The starter game: run round the arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out.',\n199\t  level: 'level:arena',\n200\t  physics: true,\n201\t  setup(w) {\n202\t    addLevelBodies(physicsOf(w), ARENA);\n203\t    const gems = w.settings.get<number>('game.gems');\n204\t    w.spawn({ round: { gems, left: Math.round(w.settings.get<number>('game.seconds') * w.hz) } });\n205\t    scatterGems(w, gems);\n206\t    spawnPlayer(w, [START[0], START[1], START[2]], w.settings.get<number>('game.speed'));\n207\t    w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' });\n208\t    w.systems.add('game.clock', clock, { phase: 'rules' });\n209\t  },\n210\t});\n211\t\n212\texport default play;\n213\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"7228c358-5a25-4b0e-a710-8ba10420b3cd","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:35.552Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/labs/game/scenes/play.ts","content":"/**\n * @file The starter game's scene `game:play`: every new game begins as this one (tools/game/new-game.mjs copies\n * labs/game/ into it), so it is small, plays, and shows the parts a game is made of. The player, a humanoid on the\n * character controller, runs round the arena (`ARENA_TEXT` below: walls, two pillars, two low walls to jump) collecting\n * the gems the seed scatters, before the clock runs out. All the gems: won; the clock at 0: lost.\n *\n * The parts: a level as text, compiled for its physics bodies; settings (`game.*`, read when the scene starts, so they\n * change play and go in replays); components (`gem`, drawn as a small glowing box because it declares `x` and `z` in\n * metres; `round`, the score and the clock); the player (labs/game/cast/player.ts, an actor that reads the step's\n * intents: `move`, `jump`, `dash`); systems in the phase `rules`, after the actors' feet are read back; a named random\n * stream (`gems`); events (`gem`, `won`, `lost`) that pages, sounds and tests hear. The page is labs/game/main.ts, the\n * bot labs/game/bot.ts.\n *\n * The level's text lives here, not in a .txt file, since sim-side code reads no files; the page registers the same\n * text for the view (labs/game/main.ts).\n *\n * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts, so `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts` runs it in Node.\n *\n * @example\n * // node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --steps 600\n * // node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --set game.gems=3 --seed 7\n * ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero')?.position; // [3.5, 0, 2.5]\n * @see labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts\n */\nimport {\n  addLevelBodies,\n  compileLevel,\n  defineComponent,\n  defineScene,\n  defineSettings,\n  physicsOf,\n  type ActorComponent,\n  type World,\n} from '../../../engine/sim-api';\nimport { spawnPlayer } from '../cast/player';\n\n/** The arena: 18 × 14 m inside walls 3 m high, two 2 × 2 m pillars, two low walls (0.75 m) to jump, the spawn `@`. */\nexport const ARENA_TEXT = `---\ndescription: The starter game's arena. 18 × 14 m inside walls 3 m high, two 2 × 2 m pillars, two low walls 0.75 m high that the player jumps, and the player's spawn.\n---\n##################\n#................#\n#..@.............#\n#.......PP.......#\n#.......PP.......#\n#................#\n#....w......w....#\n#....w......w....#\n#................#\n#.......PP.......#\n#.......PP.......#\n#................#\n#................#\n##################\n`;\n\n/** The arena, compiled: its colliders for physics, its tiles for the gems and the bot's paths. */\nexport const ARENA = compileLevel(ARENA_TEXT, { file: 'labs/game/scenes/play.ts', name: 'arena' });\n\n/** The game's settings: each is reachable through `x set`, URL parameters (`?game.gems=3`) and `__engine.set`. */\nexport const GAME_SETTINGS = defineSettings({\n  'game.gems': {\n    type: 'integer',\n    default: 10,\n    minimum: 1,\n    maximum: 40,\n    when: 'scene',\n    description: 'How many gems the seed scatters.',\n  },\n  'game.seconds': {\n    type: 'number',\n    default: 30,\n    minimum: 5,\n    maximum: 600,\n    unit: 's',\n    when: 'scene',\n    description: 'How long the player has to collect them all.',\n  },\n  'game.speed': {\n    type: 'number',\n    default: 6,\n    minimum: 1,\n    maximum: 20,\n    unit: 'm/s',\n    when: 'scene',\n    description: 'How fast the player runs.',\n  },\n});\n\n/** A gem: where it floats and how it looks (the page draws a component that declares x and z in metres). */\nexport const GEM = defineComponent('gem', {\n  description: 'A gem to collect: where it floats, its spin and how it is drawn.',\n  fields: {\n    x: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'East.' },\n    y: { type: 'number', default: 0.5, unit: 'm', description: 'Up: the bottom of its box.' },\n    z: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'm', description: 'South.' },\n    yaw: { type: 'number', default: 0, unit: 'rad', description: 'Its spin.' },\n    size: { type: 'number', default: 0.4, unit: 'm', description: 'The side of its box.' },\n    material: { type: 'string', default: 'material:glow', description: 'What it is drawn with.' },\n  },\n});\n\n/** The round: the score, the clock and how it ended. One entity holds it. */\nexport const ROUND = defineComponent('round', {\n  description:\n    'The round: gems collected of the total, the steps left on the clock, and whether it is playing, won or lost.',\n  fields: {\n    gems: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many gems the round scattered.' },\n    collected: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'How many the player has collected.' },\n    left: { type: 'integer', default: 0, description: 'The sim steps left on the clock (60 a second).' },\n    state: { type: 'string', default: 'playing', description: \"'playing', 'won' or 'lost'.\" },\n  },\n});\n\n/** The round, as the component holds it. */\nexport type Round = { gems: number; collected: number; left: number; state: 'playing' | 'won' | 'lost' };\n\n/** The scene's components, typed. */\nexport type GameComponents = {\n  gem: { x: number; y: number; z: number; yaw: number; size: number; material: string };\n  round: Round;\n  actor: ActorComponent;\n};\n\n/** How close the player's feet come to a gem to collect it, m (on the ground plane). */\nexport const REACH = 0.8;\n\n/** How far from the spawn the gems keep, m, so the first one is never collected without moving. */\nconst CLEAR = 3;\n\n/** Where the player starts: the level's spawn `@`. */\nconst START = ARENA.spawns.find((spawn) => spawn.name === 'hero')!.position;\n\n/** The floor tiles a gem may float over: open floor (`.`), at least `CLEAR` metres from the spawn, in reading order. */\nfunction gemTiles(): number[] {\n  const tiles: number[] = [];\n  ARENA.tiles.forEach((char, i) => {\n    const x = ((i % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n    const z = (Math.floor(i / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n    if (char === '.' && Math.hypot(x - START[0], z - START[2]) >= CLEAR) tiles.push(i);\n  });\n  return tiles;\n}\n\n/** Scatters `count` gems over distinct floor tiles drawn from the stream `gems`: each seed lays them its own way. */\nfunction scatterGems(w: World<GameComponents>, count: number): void {\n  const tiles = gemTiles();\n  const rng = w.rng('gems');\n  for (let k = 0; k < count && tiles.length > 0; k++) {\n    const [tile] = tiles.splice(rng.int(0, tiles.length - 1), 1);\n    const x = ((tile % ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n    const z = (Math.floor(tile / ARENA.cols) + 0.5) * ARENA.tile;\n    w.spawn({ gem: { x, z, yaw: rng.range(0, Math.PI) } });\n  }\n}\n\n/** Ends the round: the player stops reading intents (and so stands still), and the event says how it ended. */\nfunction endRound(w: World<GameComponents>, round: Round, state: 'won' | 'lost'): void {\n  round.state = state;\n  for (const e of w.query('actor')) {\n    e.actor.player = false;\n    e.actor.intents = {};\n  }\n  w.emit(state, { collected: round.collected, gems: round.gems, left: round.left });\n}\n\n/**\n * Spins the gems, collects those an actor's feet reach, and ends the round when the last one goes. A despawn waits for\n * the end of the step, so a gem taken this step is remembered: two actors never count it twice.\n */\nfunction collect(w: World<GameComponents>): void {\n  const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n  for (const gem of w.query('gem')) gem.gem.yaw += 2 * w.dt;\n  if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n  const taken = new Set<number>();\n  for (const { actor } of w.query('actor')) {\n    for (const gem of w.query('gem')) {\n      if (taken.has(gem.id) || Math.hypot(gem.gem.x - actor.x, gem.gem.z - actor.z) > REACH) continue;\n      taken.add(gem.id);\n      w.despawn(gem.id);\n      round.collected += 1;\n      w.emit('gem', { id: gem.id, x: gem.gem.x, z: gem.gem.z, collected: round.collected });\n    }\n  }\n  if (round.collected >= round.gems) endRound(w, round, 'won');\n}\n\n/** Runs the clock down a step at a time (game time is whole steps, never summed seconds); at 0 the round is lost. */\nfunction clock(w: World<GameComponents>): void {\n  const [{ round }] = w.query('round');\n  if (round.state !== 'playing') return;\n  round.left = Math.max(0, round.left - 1);\n  if (round.left === 0) endRound(w, round, 'lost');\n}\n\n/** The starter game: collect every gem before the clock runs out. */\nexport const play = defineScene<GameComponents>('game:play', {\n  description: 'The starter game: run round the arena collecting the gems before the clock runs out.',\n  level: 'level:arena',\n  physics: true,\n  setup(w) {\n    addLevelBodies(physicsOf(w), ARENA);\n    const gems = w.settings.get<number>('game.gems');\n    w.spawn({ round: { gems, left: Math.round(w.settings.get<number>('game.seconds') * w.hz) } });\n    scatterGems(w, gems);\n    spawnPlayer(w, [START[0], START[1], START[2]], w.settings.get<number>('game.speed'));\n    w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' });\n    w.systems.add('game.clock', clock, { phase: 'rules' });\n  },\n});\n\nexport default play;\n","numLines":213,"startLine":1,"totalLines":213}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GtdCAPmoqXuJzpyT1DZwhq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj94BvqmjueFQBpevq4z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EZXFe9B17P28kwoJb7pTZt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/PROGRESS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31590,"cache_read_input_tokens":49447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31590},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"7cee0276-d795-48a2-a524-c16383a24df1","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:36.892Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj94BcjkJhhN5akYzWx4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01EZXFe9B17P28kwoJb7pTZt":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/PROGRESS.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EZXFe9B17P28kwoJb7pTZt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Progress\n2\t\n3\tRead this first: where things stand, what is next and the open issues, on one screen (`node x docs --check` fails it\n4\tpast 40 lines). The integrator rewrites it at each gate; every WP's status and notes are in the ledger, PLAN.md §14.\n5\t\n6\t## Next: nothing scheduled; the owner's playtest of the tech demo\n7\t\n8\t- Gate G13 (PR #34): Phase 13 is done, with every WP it fired: the front end (WP 13.25), the governor (7.5),\n9\t  GPU-driven crowds' stage 1 (11.4), templates and skills (12.1), the usability evals and mutation testing (12.2),\n10\t  `x mcp` (7.6), the tour with its attract mode and a film per look (13.26). What follows waits: upgrades U-2 to U-7\n11\t  from their dates (`node x deps --qualify`), on-demand WPs for their triggers (§9.1), Phase H for a game to ship.\n12\t- The owner's, all in the playground's Owner checks (start menu; WP 13.28), each in plain words with Go there: the\n13\t  playtest (docs/PLAYGROUND.md §9.3), the looks to judge (the clean default, clean and cel at full resolution, the\n14\t  accent weak from isometric, the cel look's dark night there, its near-black brick), the sound and a pad, and ESC-0002\n15\t  to ESC-0006's calls (the Quaternius GLBs, model access for the live evals, the crowd's CPU render, the mutants outside\n16\t  the sample), answered from Copy my answers. Their go-ahead (2026-10-22): a bot may play with infinite health and ammo.\n17\t\n18\t## Where things stand (2026-11-05)\n19\t\n20\t- Phases 0–9 and 13 are done (G10–G13: PR #22 to #34). The playground (`npm run dev`) is one map, the block, behind a\n21\t  splash and a start menu (WP 13.33), shut doors walls to its cameras (13.34), no z-fighting (13.35–13.37), the audit's\n22\t  gaps closed (13.38); the player moves as Apex in five cameras (doors, pickups, melee, guns, ragdolls, neighbours, a\n23\t  dog, sound, day and night, GPU rain; clean, cel and pixel looks); Watch the tour plays it all. Agents drive it through\n24\t  `node x`, `__engine` or `x mcp`. Games: lean/. Shipped as playground.html. Faster starts (ADR-0008 amendments 11, 12;\n25\t  Owner: a move still re-keys lit pipelines); Style Lab's filters in Tune the look.\n26\t\n27\t## What waits, and the open issues\n28\t\n29\t- Amendment 22 proposes triangle-mesh terrain. QA baselines: the timelines' knockdown slide, WP 8.8's. Owner:\n30\t  top-down's pixel look panned across a whole pixel changes 7–10 pixels of a soft shadow band (amendment 35). Clip\n31\t  contacts: hands read as holds in walks, idles and more (WP 8.8's row); no foot lock reads a clip's foot contacts yet.\n32\t  Not verifiable here: speakers, touch (ADR-0021), pads, real GPUs (`stats().gpuMs`: the rain's 1 ms; load times), the\n33\t  pixel look's glide, how the filters read, a desktop MCP client. A key pressed in a warm-up is lost (amendment 48).\n34\t- Mutation (WP 12.2, ESC-0006): the run's tenth catches 94.40%, the rest 74.3%; rotate its seed and redraw each gate.\n35\t- Near the size cap (390–397 lines), split on the next change: sim/world.ts, anim/{quadruped,clip/validate}.ts,\n36\t  tools/cmd/replay.ts, app/engine.ts; four tests at 399–400.\n37\t- The quick gate is over its 5 min (CI_BUDGET), its long pole the playground's soak and site tests; under load\n38\t  `npm test` can fail on RPC timeouts; audio.spec's spatial test hung once in an offline render on GitHub (e6f38ba).\n39\t- The tour's page goldens come from its spec (amendment 41). Random play lost the shooter's chest in 4–5% of frames\n40\t  (before WP 13.27). A shot's ID pass builds a pipeline again after each lambert–toon style switch (frames never do).\n41\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"962f1d12-4a88-4b6b-9b5d-b066db86e83a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:36.907Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/my-3dge/docs/PROGRESS.md","content":"# Progress\n\nRead this first: where things stand, what is next and the open issues, on one screen (`node x docs --check` fails it\npast 40 lines). The integrator rewrites it at each gate; every WP's status and notes are in the ledger, PLAN.md §14.\n\n## Next: nothing scheduled; the owner's playtest of the tech demo\n\n- Gate G13 (PR #34): Phase 13 is done, with every WP it fired: the front end (WP 13.25), the governor (7.5),\n  GPU-driven crowds' stage 1 (11.4), templates and skills (12.1), the usability evals and mutation testing (12.2),\n  `x mcp` (7.6), the tour with its attract mode and a film per look (13.26). What follows waits: upgrades U-2 to U-7\n  from their dates (`node x deps --qualify`), on-demand WPs for their triggers (§9.1), Phase H for a game to ship.\n- The owner's, all in the playground's Owner checks (start menu; WP 13.28), each in plain words with Go there: the\n  playtest (docs/PLAYGROUND.md §9.3), the looks to judge (the clean default, clean and cel at full resolution, the\n  accent weak from isometric, the cel look's dark night there, its near-black brick), the sound and a pad, and ESC-0002\n  to ESC-0006's calls (the Quaternius GLBs, model access for the live evals, the crowd's CPU render, the mutants outside\n  the sample), answered from Copy my answers. Their go-ahead (2026-10-22): a bot may play with infinite health and ammo.\n\n## Where things stand (2026-11-05)\n\n- Phases 0–9 and 13 are done (G10–G13: PR #22 to #34). The playground (`npm run dev`) is one map, the block, behind a\n  splash and a start menu (WP 13.33), shut doors walls to its cameras (13.34), no z-fighting (13.35–13.37), the audit's\n  gaps closed (13.38); the player moves as Apex in five cameras (doors, pickups, melee, guns, ragdolls, neighbours, a\n  dog, sound, day and night, GPU rain; clean, cel and pixel looks); Watch the tour plays it all. Agents drive it through\n  `node x`, `__engine` or `x mcp`. Games: lean/. Shipped as playground.html. Faster starts (ADR-0008 amendments 11, 12;\n  Owner: a move still re-keys lit pipelines); Style Lab's filters in Tune the look.\n\n## What waits, and the open issues\n\n- Amendment 22 proposes triangle-mesh terrain. QA baselines: the timelines' knockdown slide, WP 8.8's. Owner:\n  top-down's pixel look panned across a whole pixel changes 7–10 pixels of a soft shadow band (amendment 35). Clip\n  contacts: hands read as holds in walks, idles and more (WP 8.8's row); no foot lock reads a clip's foot contacts yet.\n  Not verifiable here: speakers, touch (ADR-0021), pads, real GPUs (`stats().gpuMs`: the rain's 1 ms; load times), the\n  pixel look's glide, how the filters read, a desktop MCP client. A key pressed in a warm-up is lost (amendment 48).\n- Mutation (WP 12.2, ESC-0006): the run's tenth catches 94.40%, the rest 74.3%; rotate its seed and redraw each gate.\n- Near the size cap (390–397 lines), split on the next change: sim/world.ts, anim/{quadruped,clip/validate}.ts,\n  tools/cmd/replay.ts, app/engine.ts; four tests at 399–400.\n- The quick gate is over its 5 min (CI_BUDGET), its long pole the playground's soak and site tests; under load\n  `npm test` can fail on RPC timeouts; audio.spec's spatial test hung once in an offline render on GitHub (e6f38ba).\n- The tour's page goldens come from its spec (amendment 41). Random play lost the shooter's chest in 4–5% of frames\n  (before WP 13.27). A shot's ID pass builds a pipeline again after each lambert–toon style switch (frames never do).\n","numLines":41,"startLine":1,"totalLines":41}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EZXFe9B17P28kwoJb7pTZt","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
