{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0117dyEPryEkwL5ZDWhHFLn3","type":"tool_result","content":"4:or project folders with their own code and assets; the hub finds them, dresses\n73:A bare trailing number is left alone, so `snake-2.html` stays its own game\n83:games/Out-Run-SUPER-RUNOUT-The-coast-is-calling-2026-09-11-model-unknown.html\n88:`model-unknown` is used until the model is known (`GPT-6-Astra-Pro`). A\n97:`…-2026-10-02-model-unknown.html` — is a newer build of that game: one tile,\n108:the package states otherwise; missing details remain unknown. Exact duplicates\n120:games/claude/tetris.html           is its own tile, and a Collection rail appears\n123:games/orbit/game.js                one game whose page loads its own files.\n132:top-level folder names the collection. A project can point at its own favicon\n196:in the Couch, with its own screenshots and URL for comparison. Its genre and\n208:are preserved. Games copied by hand must supply their own correct import map;\n222:Pushes don't deploy, to keep Vercel's usage down (`\"git\": {\n262:folds the bar down to just the ✕ (tap again to bring it back); holding it\n267:If a game misbehaves in a frame, *Open on its own page* in its Details drawer\n334:  lobby with an invite link), *Got an invite?* (paste the link, the code, or\n360:its own colours, and its name, blurb, facts and keys sit over them. Under it\n370:| Move | arrows; Home/End, PageUp/PageDown | D-pad or left stick |\n380:*More info* opens the game's own page: its stills as a gallery, its controls\n400:narrow it down:\n422:record, **F** favourites, **O** opens a game on its own page, **/** writes a\n428:copies it as a Markdown table.\n443:own section. Date and order selections can be shared as links.\n464:Every game can be played with up to three friends, each on their own device,\n467:no way for players to message each other; they talk in an app of their own.\n477:- **Parallel**: everyone plays their own copy, from the same\n491:awards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\n495:why, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n500:not a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n518:each with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\n560:  game in a folder of its own, so it can change nothing else and two jobs\n564:  on its own compared with the original.\n582:**Up next** holds the host's own picks: *Pick the games* opens every game the\n595:in the lobby before the first round or between rounds: by mode (Parallel,\n603:next game, how to win it and its controls (or the game shows its own card, in\n604:its own style), and lifts on its ready prompt:\n606:(the host sets the card's countdown in the lobby: 10, 20 or 30 seconds). The table: one point for every\n613:- The host signs in with Google, picks a name, and gets an invite link,\n621:- **Relay only** in the lobby sends all of a player's connections through the\n691:Known limits:\n692:- Games run on the hub's own origin, so a game could read the signed-in\n....\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>Star Arena — AI Hub session example (shared world)</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"A minimal shared-world game for the AI Hub Session Protocol: every copy moves its own player, the host runs the stars and the scores, first to ten wins.\">\n<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#1b1530\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Tech demo\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"2-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"2\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"First to ten stars wins\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move: arrows, WASD or a stick