{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JbT9PhTGE5D9RhDtETT8d3","type":"tool_result","content":"`node scripts/explore-browser.mjs`. Pass a deployed site URL to check production.\n\n## Playing together: AI Hub sessions\n\nEvery game can be played with up to three friends, each on their own device,\nthrough an **AI Hub session**: the hub deals the games (Random Mode), keeps\nthe clock, carries the network traffic and keeps score. There is no chat and\nno way for players to message each other; they talk in an app of their own.\n\nWhat a game must do is the **AI Hub Session Protocol**, specified in\n[`lib/hub/README.md`](lib/hub/README.md) and served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. That page is the\nwhole interface: it is the one thing handed to whichever agent builds a game.\nThe page holds only the interface and the hub's facts: no example values,\nsuggested numbers or design advice, since an agent takes any of those as the\nway to build the game. A game declares one of three modes:\n\n- **Parallel**: everyone plays their own copy, from the same\n  start, on the same clock; the hub draws a live scoreboard from each copy's\n  progress. Every game with a score or a finish can do it, with no networking,\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n....\n489:In short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\n592:After the picks, **Random Mode** deals. The hub deals every game that declares `hub:session` and\n623:  the browser, as `localStorage['ai-hub:relay-only']`).\n624:- While no game in the library declares `hub:session`, the reference games\n734:own, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\n741:an override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n757:checks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n758:(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\n....\n/**\n * @file The one-file playground, `playground.html` at the root (ADR-0023 amendment 5, the owner's call of\n * 2026-11-02): the playground (labs/playground/) built as one self-contained HTML page, its script and styles inline\n * and nothing fetched, so it opens from disk (file://) in a WebGPU browser, sent as one file. It is how the engine is\n * distributed and how the owner tests it, in place of the Vercel deployment it replaces.\n *\n * `buildOneFile` runs Vite's build on labs/playground/index.html with vite.config.ts's alias (a bare `three` is\n * `three/webgpu`), every dynamic import folded into the one script (`inlineDynamicImports`) and every asset a data URL;\n * `inlineBuild` then puts the script and the stylesheet into the page. The build gives the same bytes on every machine\n * (no path or time goes in: tools/lib/oneFile.test.ts), so `node x html --check` rebuilds it and compares, and the\n * quick gate runs that check (tools/cmd/ci.ts): the committed file matches its sources at every merge.\n *\n * Invariants: the page fetches nothing (`externalRefs` is empty); a `</script` inside the script is written\n * `<\\/script`, which means the same in a string, a template, a regular expression or a comment, and a `<!--` in it, which\n * no such rewrite makes safe, fails the build. Only the playground is in it: its links to the engine's other pages\n * (the Stress Box, the labs index, the labs) are the dev server's from the file, each saying in plain words to run\n * npm run dev first (labs/playground/ui/pages.ts).\n *\n * @example\n * inlineBuild('<script type=\"module\" crossorigin src=\"./a.js\"></script>', { 'a.js': 'go()' }); // '<script type=\"module\">go()</script>'\n * ONE_FILE; // 'playground.html'\n * @see tools/lib/oneFile.test.ts\n * @see tools/cmd/html.ts\n */\nimport { join } from 'node:path';\nimport type { Rollup } from 'vite';\n\n/** The file, from the repository root. */\nexport const ONE_FILE = 'playground.html';\n\n/** The page it is built from. */\nexport const ONE_FILE_PAGE = 'labs/playground/index.html';\n\n/** The note the file opens its head with. */\nexport const ONE_FILE_NOTE = `<!-- ${ONE_FILE}: the my-3dge playground as one self-contained page (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge, MIT; it carries three.js, MIT, Rapier, Apache-2.0, and @stdlib functions, Apache-2.0: see lean/LICENSE). Open it in a browser with WebGPU. Written by node x html --write from ${ONE_FILE_PAGE}; never edit it by hand. -->`; // prettier-ignore\n\n/** A module script or stylesheet Vite's page loads from a file. */\nconst LOADED = /<script type=\"module\" crossorigin src=\"([^\"]+)\"><\\/script>|<link rel=\"stylesheet\" crossorigin href=\"([^\"]+)\">/g; // prettier-ignore\n\n/**\n * The page `html` with each script and stylesheet it loads put inline, from `files` (by file name; a reference\n * matches the file whose name it ends with), and `ONE_FILE_NOTE` after its charset. Throws, naming the file, when a\n * reference has no file or a script holds a `<!--`.\n */\nexport function inlineBuild(html: string, files: Readonly<Record<string, string>>): string {\n  const find = (ref: string): string => {\n    const name = Object.keys(files).find((file) => ref.endsWith(`/${file}`) || ref === file || ref === `./${file}`);\n    if (name === undefined) throw new Error(`${ref} is not in the build: the page loads a file Vite did not write`);\n    return files[name];\n  };\n  const inlined = html.replace(LOADED, (_tag, script?: string, style?: string) => {\n    if (style) return `<style>${find(style)}</style>`;\n    const code = find(script!).replaceAll('</script', '<\\\\/script');\n    if (code.includes('<!--')) {\n      throw new Error(`${script} holds \"<!--\", which an inline script cannot: write it apart ('<' + '!--') in the source`); // prettier-ignore\n    }\n    return `<script type=\"module\">${code}</script>`;\n  });\n  return inlined.replace('<meta charset=\"utf-8\" />', (charset) => `${charset}\\n    ${ONE_FILE_NOTE}`);\n}\n\n/** The `src` and `href` attributes of `html` that load something other than a data URL (none, in the one file). */\nexport function externalRefs(html: string): string[] {\n  const outside = html.replace(/<script type=\"module\">[\\s\\S]*?<\\/script>|<style>[\\s\\S]*?<\\/style>/g, '');\n  return [...outside.matchAll(/\\b(?:src|href)=\"([^\"]*)\"/g)].map((match) => match[1]).filter((ref) => !ref.startsWith('data:')); // prettier-ignore\n}\n\n/** Builds the one file from the repository at `root` and returns its text (about 7 MB; about 11 s). */\nexport async function buildOneFile(root: string): Promise<string> {\n  const { build } = await import('vite');\n  const result = await build({\n    root,\n    configFile: false,\n    logLevel: 'warn',\n    base: './',\n    resolve: { alias: [{ find: /^three$/, replacement: 'three/webgpu' }] },\n    build: {\n      write: false,\n      modulePreload: false,\n      cssCodeSplit: false,\n      assetsInlineLimit: () => true,\n      chunkSizeWarningLimit: Infinity,\n      rollupOptions: { input: { playground: join(root, ONE_FILE_PAGE) }, output: { inlineDynamicImports: true } },\n    },\n  });\n  const outputs = (Array.isArray(result) ? result : [result]) as Rollup.RollupOutput[];\n  const files: Record<string, string> = {};\n  let html: string | undefined;\n  for (const item of outputs.flatMap((output) => output.output)) {\n    const text = item.type === 'chunk' ? item.code : typeof item.source === 'string' ? item.source : new TextDecoder().decode(item.source); // prettier-ignore\n    if (item.fileName.endsWith('.html')) html = text;\n    else files[item.fileName] = text;\n  }\n  if (html === undefined) throw new Error(`Vite wrote no page from ${ONE_FILE_PAGE}: check that it exists`);\n  return inlineBuild(html, files);\n}\n....\n/**\n * @file Writes and checks the one-file playground, `playground.html` (tools/lib/oneFile.ts; ADR-0023 amendment 5): the\n * playground as one self-contained HTML page, the file the engine is distributed and tested as.\n *\n * - `--write` builds it and writes it when it differs; commit it with the change that made it differ.\n * - `--check` (the default) builds it and fails while the committed file differs (`HTML_STALE`, fixed by `--write`) or\n *   loads anything from outside itself (`HTML_EXTERNAL`). The quick gate runs it (tools/cmd/ci.ts).\n *\n * The build takes about 11 s and gives the same bytes every time. The report's metrics give the file's size in bytes.\n *\n * Usage: node x html [--check | --write]. Exit 0 when the file is current (or was written), 1 otherwise, 2 on a\n * usage error.\n *\n * @example\n * // node x html --write   → x html: ok: wrote playground.html (6,912,709 bytes)\n * // node x html --check   → x html: ok: playground.html current (6,912,709 bytes)\n * @see tools/cmd/html.test.ts\n */\nimport { existsSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { join } from 'node:path';\nimport { buildOneFile, externalRefs, ONE_FILE } from '../lib/oneFile';\nimport type { Finding } from '../lib/report';\nimport { UsageError, type Command, type CommandResult } from '../x';\n\n/** Builds the command; tests inject the build. */\nexport function createHtmlCommand(build: (root: string) => Promise<string> = buildOneFile): Command {\n  return {\n    usage: 'html [--check | --write]',\n    options: { check: { type: 'boolean' }, write: { type: 'boolean' } },\n    maxPositionals: 0,\n    async run({ values, root }): Promise<CommandResult> {\n      if (values.check && values.write) throw new UsageError('choose --check or --write, not both');\n      const html = await build(root);\n      const path = join(root, ONE_FILE);\n      const current = existsSync(path) && readFileSync(path, 'utf8') === html;\n      const failures: Finding[] = externalRefs(html).map((ref) => ({\n        id: 'HTML_EXTERNAL',\n        message: `the one file loads ${ref}, which a file opened from disk cannot reach: inline it (tools/lib/oneFile.ts) or import it from the page's code`, // prettier-ignore\n        file: ONE_FILE,\n      }));\n      const bytes = Buffer.byteLength(html);\n      const size = `${bytes.toLocaleString('en-US')} bytes`;\n      if (values.write) {\n        if (!current) writeFileSync(path, html);\n        return {\n          ok: failures.length === 0,\n          summary: current ? `${ONE_FILE} was already current (${size})` : `wrote ${ONE_FILE} (${size})`,\n          failures,\n          metrics: { bytes, written: current ? 0 : 1 },\n        };\n      }\n      if (!current) {\n        failures.push({\n          id: 'HTML_STALE',\n          message: `${ONE_FILE} ${existsSync(path) ? 'differs from a build of its sources' : 'is missing'}: run node x html --write and commit it`, // prettier-ignore\n          file: ONE_FILE,\n        });\n      }\n      return {\n        ok: failures.length === 0,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"f12183e7-8a88-487a-9398-be675d67b4ac","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:25:05.488Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"`node scripts/explore-browser.mjs`. Pass a deployed site URL to check production.\n\n## Playing together: AI Hub sessions\n\nEvery game can be played with up to three friends, each on their own device,\nthrough an **AI Hub session**: the hub deals the games (Random Mode), keeps\nthe clock, carries the network traffic and keeps score. There is no chat and\nno way for players to message each other; they talk in an app of their own.\n\nWhat a game must do is the **AI Hub Session Protocol**, specified in\n[`lib/hub/README.md`](lib/hub/README.md) and served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. That page is the\nwhole interface: it is the one thing handed to whichever agent builds a game.\nThe page holds only the interface and the hub's facts: no example values,\nsuggested numbers or design advice, since an agent takes any of those as the\nway to build the game. A game declares one of three modes:\n\n- **Parallel**: everyone plays their own copy, from the same\n  start, on the same clock; the hub draws a live scoreboard from each copy's\n  progress. Every game with a score or a finish can do it, with no networking,\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n....\n489:In short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\n592:After the picks, **Random Mode** deals. The hub deals every game that declares `hub:session` and\n623:  the browser, as `localStorage['ai-hub:relay-only']`).\n624:- While no game in the library declares `hub:session`, the reference games\n734:own, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\n741:an override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n757:checks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n758:(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\n....\n/**\n * @file The one-file playground, `playground.html` at the root (ADR-0023 amendment 5, the owner's call of\n * 2026-11-02): the playground (labs/playground/) built as one self-contained HTML page, its script and styles inline\n * and nothing fetched, so it opens from disk (file://) in a WebGPU browser, sent as one file. It is how the engine is\n