{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7vpaqnDTwSbtFACzC779W","type":"tool_result","content":"/**\n * @file The cutaway (PLAN.md WP 4.4): what keeps a level's walls and pillars from hiding the play. `createCutaway(\n * meshes)` gives every level mesh part whose recipe cuts (engine/gfx/level/recipes.ts, `cut`) a twin, an instanced\n * mesh of its cut-down shape under the level's root with its part's slots (`instanceSlots`, which says why), and\n * `update(pose, focus)` moves each piece between the part and its twin as the camera needs (`planCutaway` says how,\n * pure and unit-tested): a wall facing the camera is cut low, a pillar between the camera and the hero becomes a\n * stump. A part's instances are its uncut cells, in order, and its twin's its cut ones, so each costs its draw groups\n * only while it has instances (`drawCalls`). Nothing changes on the GPU but instance matrices, and the twins are\n * registered with the warm-up (`register`), so turning the camera builds no pipeline.\n *\n * The prototype cut only for its classic views and never for its 3D cameras; this asks the geometry instead, so an\n * orbit camera outside the hall cuts the walls in front of it and a chase camera inside cuts none.\n *\n * The occluder fade (WP 13.8, docs/PLAYGROUND.md §4): for a camera that may stand behind a wall (`update`'s `fade`:\n * the shooter's and the platformer's, engine/gfx/cameras/collide.ts), every block piece of any part, and every object\n * `objects` gives (the stage's rides: each mesh by its own box, none thinner than `FADE_THIN`), whose box a line from the\n * camera to the hero's head or chest (`FADE_AIMS`) crosses is taken out of the picture until the line clears\n * (`planFade`): a piece leaves its part's instances, a mesh hides. A piece the cutaway cut stays as cut; a part drawn as one mesh (a ramp, a floor) is never faded, its\n * box no measure of its shape. It hides rather than drawing a see-through twin, so it adds no material and no pipeline\n * (ADR-0025 amendment 8).\n *\n * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/40-cameras.js:166-181` (`placeCut`: walls by their outward direction\n * against the view's, `> .3`, none from above 85°; pillars on the lines from the camera to the hero's head and feet\n * and, below 60°, four points 40 units round him, the pillar's box widened by 4 units) and `10-hall.js:302-370`\n * (`placeWalls`, the cut twins, `placePillars`, `segBox`), in metres.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only, never read by the sim; pure decisions (equal poses give equal cuts and fades);\n * `update` writes instances only when a cut or a fade changes; `dispose` puts every piece back and removes the twins.\n *\n * @example\n * // const cutaway = createCutaway(meshes);\n * // cutaway.register(gfx.warmup);\n * // each frame: cutaway.update(camera.pose(), hero.position);\n * import { makePose } from './cameras/pose';\n * const cells = [{ centre: [0.5, 0, 9.5], size: [1, 1], out: [0, 1] }] as const; // a wall tile facing south\n * const pose = makePose({ position: [0.5, 8, 20], yaw: Math.PI, pitch: -0.5 }); // south of it, looking north\n * planCutaway([{ when: 'facing', bottom: 0, top: 3, cells }], pose, [0.5, 0, 5]); // [[true]]\n * @see engine/gfx/cutaway.test.ts\n * @see tests/e2e/hall.spec.ts\n */\nimport { Box3, InstancedMesh, Vector3, type Mesh, type Object3D } from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { poseForward, poseTarget, type CameraPose, type Vec3 } from './cameras/pose';\nimport type { LevelCell } from './level/cells';\nimport { meshDrawCalls, type LevelMeshes, type LevelMeshPart } from './level/meshes';\nimport type { CutWhen } from './level/recipes';\nimport { instanceSlots, placeInstances } from './level/shapes';\nimport type { Warmup } from './warmup';\n\n/** A wall is cut when the cosine between its outward direction and the way to the camera is above this. */\nexport const CUT_FACING = 0.3;\n/** Looking down steeper than this (rad, 85°), the camera sees over every wall: none is cut. */\nexport const CUT_STEEP = (85 * Math.PI) / 180;\n/** Looking down at least this steeply (rad, 60°), only the hero's head and feet are aimed at, not the floor round him. */\nexport const AIM_STEEP = (60 * Math.PI) / 180;\n/** How far round a blocking piece's box a line of sight still counts as through it (m): 4 prototype units. */\nexport const CUT_MARGIN = 0.25;\n/** The points aimed at (m): the head and the feet above the focus, and the floor this far round it. */\nexport const CUT_AIMS = Object.freeze({ head: 1.625, feet: 0.125, around: 2.5 });\n/** The points the fade keeps in sight above the focus (m), and how far inside a box a line must pass to fade it. */\nexport const FADE_AIMS = Object.freeze({ head: 1.5, chest: 1, inset: 0.05 });\n\n/** A ride part thinner than this, m (its geometry's least extent): a cable, a rail, a rung or a pole hides no one. */\nexport const FADE_THIN = 0.25;\n\nconst size = new Vector3();\n\n/** Whether `mesh` is thinner than `FADE_THIN` (see it): the fade leaves it, and the ride a player holds, in the picture. */\nfunction thin(mesh: Mesh): boolean {\n  if (!mesh.geometry.boundingBox) mesh.geometry.computeBoundingBox();\n  mesh.geometry.boundingBox!.getSize(size).multiply(mesh.scale);\n====\n/**\n * @file The look `look:torch-lit`: the torch-lit hall, the prototype's stress world (PLAN.md WP 7.8): dark but for the\n * torches on the braziers, each flickering on its own, the nearest casting shadows, and fog from 12 m past the hero. It\n * is the sky `sky:hall` over the scene's own style with no filter: the light pool lights all the hall's 30 torch\n * spots on a renderer with tiled lighting (engine/gfx/enhanced/lighting.ts), else the 12 nearest the hero\n * (engine/gfx/lights.ts), the light following him round the hall; the other box looks are this one with a style or a\n * filter preset (labs/box/README.md lists them).\n *\n * Invariants: data only, the kind's fields (`node x describe look`): the sky `sky:hall` (engine/gfx/sky.ts), which a\n * page that starts the engine shows (engine/app/look.ts), the scene's own style, and every filter it leaves out off;\n * levels 0 (off) or 8 and more, so dull colours keep their hue. A page draws it when its setting `post.look` names it;\n * switching to it from a look with the scene's own lights warms up first (the lights change the shader key), then\n * builds nothing.\n *\n * @example\n * look.id; // 'look:torch-lit'\n * look.sky; // 'sky:hall'\n * @see labs/box/looks/torch-lit.test.ts\n * @see tests/e2e/box-look.spec.ts\n */\nimport { defineLook } from '../../../engine';\n\n/** The look `look:torch-lit`: the torch-lit hall: the sky `sky:hall` over the scene's own style, no filter. */\nexport const look = defineLook('look:torch-lit', {\n  description:\n    'The torch-lit hall: dark but for the flickering torches near the hero, the nearest casting shadows; fog past him.',\n  sky: 'sky:hall',\n});\n===\n/**\n * @file The stage's ground (PLAN.md WP 2.7; its frame WP 13.15): `groundMesh(size, material, options)` is a plane 2 cm\n * under a level's floor, `y`, reaching `GROUND_MARGIN` past the level's area on every side, its UVs in metres, named\n * `stage/ground` and receiving shadows. Round a terrain (`hole`) it is a frame about the terrain's area instead, which\n * the terrain itself covers: a site's cut shows what stands under it (a cellar), and the terrain's dips under its\n * origin's height never show the plane through them.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only; one mesh, every face up; equal inputs make equal geometry; without a hole, three's\n * plane, as the stage has always drawn it.\n *\n * @example\n * import { MeshBasicMaterial } from 'three/webgpu';\n * const frame = groundMesh([32, 32], new MeshBasicMaterial(), { hole: true });\n * frame.geometry.getIndex()!.count / 6; // 8: the cells round the terrain's own\n * @see engine/gfx/stage.test.ts\n */\nimport { BufferGeometry, Float32BufferAttribute, Mesh, PlaneGeometry, type Material } from 'three/webgpu';\n\n/** How far the ground reaches beyond the level on every side, m: past what any view frames at zoom 0.5. */\nexport const GROUND_MARGIN = 60;\n\n/** Where the ground lies: its area's north-west corner (m), the floor it stands under (m), and a terrain's hole. */\nexport interface GroundOptions {\n  corner?: readonly [number, number];\n  y?: number;\n  hole?: boolean;\n}\n\n/** The stage's ground round an area of `size` (m, along x and z: see the file comment). */\nexport function groundMesh(size: readonly [number, number], material: Material, options: GroundOptions = {}): Mesh {\n  const { corner: [x0, z0] = [0, 0], y = 0, hole = false } = options;\n  const [width, depth] = size;\n  const geometry = hole ? frame(x0, z0, width, depth, y - 0.02) : plane(x0, z0, width, depth, y - 0.02);\n  const ground = new Mesh(geometry, material);\n  Object.assign(ground, { name: 'stage/ground', receiveShadow: true });\n  return ground;\n}\n\n/** The plane over the area and its margins at height `y`, UVs in metres. */\nfunction plane(x0: number, z0: number, width: number, depth: number, y: number): BufferGeometry {\n  const [w, d] = [width + 2 * GROUND_MARGIN, depth + 2 * GROUND_MARGIN];\n  const geometry = new PlaneGeometry(w, d).rotateX(-Math.PI / 2).translate(x0 + width / 2, y, z0 + depth / 2);\n  const uv = geometry.getAttribute('uv');\n  for (let i = 0; i < uv.count; i++) uv.setXY(i, uv.getX(i) * w, uv.getY(i) * d);\n  return geometry;\n}\n\n/** The margins alone, the eight cells round the area at height `y`, UVs in metres from the south-west as the plane's. */\nfunction frame(x0: number, z0: number, width: number, depth: number, y: number): BufferGeometry {\n  const xs = [x0 - GROUND_MARGIN, x0, x0 + width, x0 + width + GROUND_MARGIN];\n===\napi.ts\nblend.ts\nboost.ts\nchase.ts\ncodes.ts\ncollide.ts\ndefine.ts\neffects.ts\nfirst.ts\nfixed.ts\nfly.ts\nfollow.ts\norbit.ts\nplace.ts\npose.ts\npresets.ts\nrail.ts\nrig.ts\nshoulder.ts\ncamera.test.ts.tmpl\ncamera.ts.tmpl\ntemplate.json\n/**\n * @file Unit tests for the camera mode `cam3={{name}}` ({{id}}.ts): its code makes it and it gives the code back with\n * its numbers, a bad one refused naming the form; it follows the player as he runs, never moving faster than he does,\n * and a second after he stops it stands at its place beside him, looking at him; the camera rig blends into it from\n * another camera and ends on its picture.\n * @see {{id}}.ts\n */\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\nimport { createCamera, createCameraRig, poseTarget, readCameraCode } from '../../../engine';\nimport { {{name}}Camera } from './{{name}}';\n\n/** Where it stands when its code gives no numbers. */\nconst { distance, height } = {{name}}Camera().spec();\n\n/** `point` rounded to the centimetre. */\nconst cm = (point: readonly number[]) => point.map((v) => Math.round(v * 100) / 100 + 0);\n\ndescribe('cam3={{name}}', () => {\n  it('is made from its code and gives the code back with its numbers', () => {\n    expect(readCameraCode('cam3={{name}},6')).toMatchObject({ type: 'game', name: '{{name}}' });\n    const camera = createCamera('cam3={{name}},6');\n    expect([camera.type, camera.code()]).toEqual(['{{name}}', `cam3={{name}},6,${height}`]);\n    expect(() => createCamera('cam3={{name}},0')).toThrow(/cam3={{name}},<distance>,<height>/);\n  });\n\n  it('follows the player smoothly as he runs, and a second after he stops stands beside him, looking at him', () => {\n    const camera = createCamera('cam3={{name}}');\n    let last = camera.update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 1 / 60).position; // the first frame finds him\n    let fastest = 0;\n    for (let k = 1; k <= 120; k++) {\n      const at = camera.update({ follow: [0, 0, (5 * k) / 60] }, 1 / 60).position; // south at 5 m/s for 2 s\n      fastest = Math.max(fastest, Math.hypot(...at.map((v, i) => v - last[i])));\n      last = at;\n    }\n    expect(fastest).toBeLessThanOrEqual(5 / 60 + 1e-9); // never faster than he ran: no jump in the picture\n    for (let k = 0; k < 60; k++) camera.update({ follow: [0, 0, 10] }, 1 / 60);\n    const pose = camera.pose();\n    expect(cm(pose.position)).toEqual([distance, height, 10]);\n    expect(cm(poseTarget(pose))).toEqual([0, 1, 10]); // a metre over his feet\n  });\n\n  it('is blended into by the camera rig from another camera, and ends on its own picture', () => {\n    const own = createCamera('cam3={{name}}').update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 1 / 60).position;\n    const away = (at: readonly number[]) => Math.hypot(...at.map((v, i) => v - own[i]));\n    const rig = createCameraRig('cam=iso');\n    rig.update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 1 / 60);\n    rig.set('cam3={{name}}', { blend: true });\n    rig.update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 0.1);\n    expect(away(rig.pose().position)).toBeGreaterThan(0.5); // a tenth of a second in: between the two pictures\n    for (let k = 0; k < 60; k++) rig.update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 1 / 60);\n    expect(away(rig.pose().position)).toBeLessThan(0.01);\n    expect(rig.pose().projection).toBe('perspective');\n  });\n});\n/**\n * @file The camera mode `cam3={{name}}`: what it shows (replace this text; `x docs` puts the first sentence in INDEX).\n * It starts as a side-scroller's dolly: it stands `distance` metres east of the player and `height` metres up, looks\n * west and down at a point a metre over his feet, and follows him with a lag (a time constant of 0.15 real seconds),\n * so a jump or a fall moves the picture smoothly and a stop settles it. Its code is\n * `cam3={{name}}[,<distance>,<height>]` in metres, 8 and 2.5 when left out.\n *\n * Invariants: view state, never the sim's: pure steps over plain data, which `followCamera` wraps in a camera; a\n * checked pose each frame (`makePose`: metres and radians, +Y up, its yaw the heading the intents' `cam` takes); `dt`\n * is real seconds since the last frame, so it frames alike at any frame rate. `defineCamera` adds the mode when the\n * page imports this module; from then `?cam3={{name}}`, `__engine.camera.set('cam3={{name}}', { blend: true })` and\n * the camera rig's blends reach it, and its name is a word no engine camera has.\n *\n * @example\n * import { createCamera } from '../../../engine';\n * const camera = createCamera('cam3={{name}},6');","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"f16ea631-15ed-4c8b-8c3c-07231f1788d9","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:36:57.075Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/**\n * @file The cutaway (PLAN.md WP 4.4): what keeps a level's walls and pillars from hiding the play. `createCutaway(\n * meshes)` gives every level mesh part whose recipe cuts (engine/gfx/level/recipes.ts, `cut`) a twin, an instanced\n * mesh of its cut-down shape under the level's root with its part's slots (`instanceSlots`, which says why), and\n * `update(pose, focus)` moves each piece between the part and its twin as the camera needs (`planCutaway` says how,\n * pure and unit-tested): a wall facing the camera is cut low, a pillar between the camera and the hero becomes a\n * stump. A part's instances are its uncut cells, in order, and its twin's its cut ones, so each costs its draw groups\n * only while it has instances (`drawCalls`). Nothing changes on the GPU but instance matrices, and the twins are\n * registered with the warm-up (`register`), so turning the camera builds no pipeline.\n *\n * The prototype cut only for its classic views and never for its 3D cameras; this asks the geometry instead, so an\n * orbit camera outside the hall cuts the walls in front of it and a chase camera inside cuts none.\n *\n * The occluder fade (WP 13.8, docs/PLAYGROUND.md §4): for a camera that may stand behind a wall (`update`'s `fade`:\n * the shooter's and the platformer's, engine/gfx/cameras/collide.ts), every block piece of any part, and every object\n * `objects` gives (the stage's rides: each mesh by its own box, none thinner than `FADE_THIN`), whose box a line from the\n * camera to the hero's head or chest (`FADE_AIMS`) crosses is taken out of the picture until the line clears\n * (`planFade`): a piece leaves its part's instances, a mesh hides. A piece the cutaway cut stays as cut; a part drawn as one mesh (a ramp, a floor) is never faded, its\n * box no measure of its shape. It hides rather than drawing a see-through twin, so it adds no material and no pipeline\n * (ADR-0025 amendment 8).\n *\n * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/40-cameras.js:166-181` (`placeCut`: walls by their outward direction\n * against the view's, `> .3`, none from above 85°; pillars on the lines from the camera to the hero's head and feet\n * and, below 60°, four points 40 units round him, the pillar's box widened by 4 units) and `10-hall.js:302-370`\n * (`placeWalls`, the cut twins, `placePillars`, `segBox`), in metres.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only, never read by the sim; pure decisions (equal poses give equal cuts and fades);\n * `update` writes instances only when a cut or a fade changes; `dispose` puts every piece back and removes the twins.\n *\n * @example\n * // const cutaway = createCutaway(meshes);\n * // cutaway.register(gfx.warmup);\n * // each frame: cutaway.update(camera.pose(), hero.position);\n * import { makePose } from './cameras/pose';\n * const cells = [{ centre: [0.5, 0, 9.5], size: [1, 1], out: [0, 1] }] as const; // a wall tile facing south\n * const pose = makePose({ position: [0.5, 8, 20], yaw: Math.PI, pitch: -0.5 }); // south of it, looking north\n * planCutaway([{ when: 'facing', bottom: 0, top: 3, cells }], pose, [0.5, 0, 5]); // [[true]]\n * @see engine/gfx/cutaway.test.ts\n * @see tests/e2e/hall.spec.ts\n */\nimport { Box3, InstancedMesh, Vector3, type Mesh, type Object3D } from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { poseForward, poseTarget, type CameraPose, type Vec3 } from './cameras/pose';\nimport type { LevelCell } from './level/cells';\nimport { meshDrawCalls, type LevelMeshes, type LevelMeshPart } from './level/meshes';\nimport type { CutWhen } from './level/recipes';\nimport { instanceSlots, placeInstances } from './level/shapes';\nimport type { Warmup } from './warmup';\n\n/** A wall is cut when the cosine between its outward direction and the way to the camera is above this. */\nexport const CUT_FACING = 0.3;\n/** Looking down steeper than this (rad, 85°), the camera sees over every wall: none is cut. */\nexport const CUT_STEEP = (85 * Math.PI) / 180;\n/** Looking down at least this steeply (rad, 60°), only the hero's head and feet are aimed at, not the floor round him. */\nexport const AIM_STEEP = (60 * Math.PI) / 180;\n/** How far round a blocking piece's box a line of sight still counts as through it (m): 4 prototype units. */\nexport const CUT_MARGIN = 0.25;\n/** The points aimed at (m): the head and the feet above the focus, and the floor this far round it. */\nexport const CUT_AIMS = Object.freeze({ head: 1.625, feet: 0.125, around: 2.5 });\n/** The points the fade keeps in sight above the focus (m), and how far inside a box a line must pass to fade it. */\nexport const FADE_AIMS = Object.freeze({ head: 1.5, chest: 1, inset: 0.05 });\n\n/** A ride part thinner than this, m (its geometry's least extent): a cable, a rail, a rung or a pole hides no one. */\nexport const FADE_THIN = 0.25;\n\nconst size = new Vector3();\n\n/** Whether `mesh` is thinner than `FADE_THIN` (see it): the fade leaves it, and the ride a player holds, in the picture. */\nfunction thin(mesh: Mesh): boolean {\n  if (!mesh.geometry.boundingBox) mesh.geometry.computeBoundingBox();\n  mesh.geometry.boundingBox!.getSize(size).multiply(mesh.scale);\n====\n/**\n * @file The look `look:torch-lit`: the torch-lit hall, the prototype's stress world (PLAN.md WP 7.8): dark but for the\n * torches on the braziers, each flickering on its own, the nearest casting shadows, and fog from 12 m past the hero. It\n * is the sky `sky:hall` over the scene's own style with no filter: the light pool lights all the hall's 30 torch\n * spots on a renderer with tiled lighting (engine/gfx/enhanced/lighting.ts), else the 12 nearest the hero\n * (engine/gfx/lights.ts), the light following him round the hall; the other box looks are this one with a style or a\n * filter preset (labs/box/README.md lists them).\n *\n * Invariants: data only, the kind's fields (`node x describe look`): the sky `sky:hall` (engine/gfx/sky.ts), which a\n * page that starts the engine shows (engine/app/look.ts), the scene's own style, and every filter it leaves out off;\n * levels 0 (off) or 8 and more, so dull colours keep their hue. A page draws it when its setting `post.look` names it;\n * switching to it from a look with the scene's own lights warms up first (the lights change the shader key), then\n * builds nothing.\n *\n * @example\n * look.id; // 'look:torch-lit'\n * look.sky; // 'sky:hall'\n * @see labs/box/looks/torch-lit.test.ts\n * @see tests/e2e/box-look.spec.ts\n */\nimport { defineLook } from '../../../engine';\n\n/** The look `look:torch-lit`: the torch-lit hall: the sky `sky:hall` over the scene's own style, no filter. */\nexport const look = defineLook('look:torch-lit', {\n  description:\n    'The torch-lit hall: dark but for the flickering torches near the hero, the nearest casting shadows; fog past him.',\n  sky: 'sky:hall',\n});\n===\n/**\n * @file The stage's ground (PLAN.md WP 2.7; its frame WP 13.15): `groundMesh(size, material, options)` is a plane 2 cm\n * under a level's floor, `y`, reaching `GROUND_MARGIN` past the level's area on every side, its UVs in metres, named\n * `stage/ground` and receiving shadows. Round a terrain (`hole`) it is a frame about the terrain's area instead, which\n * the terrain itself covers: a site's cut shows what stands under it (a cellar), and the terrain's dips under its\n * origin's height never show the plane through them.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only; one mesh, every face up; equal inputs make equal geometry; without a hole, three's\n * plane, as the stage has always drawn it.\n *\n * @example\n * import { MeshBasicMaterial } from 'three/webgpu';\n * const frame = groundMesh([32, 32], new MeshBasicMaterial(), { hole: true });\n * frame.geometry.getIndex()!.count / 6; // 8: the cells round the terrain's own\n * @see engine/gfx/stage.test.ts\n */\nimport { BufferGeometry, Float32BufferAttribute, Mesh, PlaneGeometry, type Material } from 'three/webgpu';\n\n/** How far the ground reaches beyond the level on every side, m: past what any view frames at zoom 0.5. */\nexport const GROUND_MARGIN = 60;\n\n/** Where the ground lies: its area's north-west corner (m), the floor it stands under (m), and a terrain's hole. */\nexport interface GroundOptions {\n  corner?: readonly [number, number];\n  y?: number;\n  hole?: boolean;\n}\n\n/** The stage's ground round an area of `size` (m, along x and z: see the file comment). */\nexport function groundMesh(size: readonly [number, number], material: Material, options: GroundOptions = {}): Mesh {\n  const { corner: [x0, z0] = [0, 0], y = 0, hole = false } = options;\n  const [width, depth] = size;\n  const geometry = hole ? frame(x0, z0, width, depth, y - 0.02) : plane(x0, z0, width, depth, y - 0.02);\n  const ground = new Mesh(geometry, material);\n  Object.assign(ground, { name: 'stage/ground', receiveShadow: true });\n  return ground;\n}\n\n/** The plane over the area and its margins at height `y`, UVs in metres. */\nfunction plane(x0: number, z0: number, width: number, depth: number, y: number): BufferGeometry {\n  const [w, d] = [width + 2 * GROUND_MARGIN, depth + 2 * GROUND_MARGIN];\n  const geometry = new PlaneGeometry(w, d).rotateX(-Math.PI / 2).translate(x0 + width / 2, y, z0 + depth / 2);\n  const uv = geometry.getAttribute('uv');\n  for (let i = 0; i < uv.count; i++) uv.setXY(i, uv.getX(i) * w, uv.getY(i) * d);\n  return geometry;\n}\n\n/** The margins alone, the eight cells round the area at height `y`, UVs in metres from the south-west as the plane's. */\nfunction frame(x0: number, z0: number, width: number, depth: number, y: number): BufferGeometry {\n  const xs = [x0 - GROUND_MARGIN, x0, x0 + width, x0 + width + GROUND_MARGIN];\n===\napi.ts\nblend.ts\nboost.ts\nchase.ts\ncodes.ts\ncollide.ts\ndefine.ts\neffects.ts\nfirst.ts\nfixed.ts\nfly.ts\nfollow.ts\norbit.ts\nplace.ts\npose.ts\npresets.ts\nrail.ts\nrig.ts\nshoulder.ts\ncamera.test.ts.tmpl\ncamera.ts.tmpl\ntemplate.json\n/**\n * @file Unit tests for the camera mode `cam3={{name}}` ({{id}}.ts): its code makes it and it gives the code back with\n * its numbers, a bad one refused naming the form; it follows the player as he runs, never moving faster than he does,\n * and a second after he stops it stands at its place beside him, looking at him; the camera rig blends into it from\n * another camera and ends on its picture.\n * @see {{id}}.ts\n */\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\nimport { createCamera, createCameraRig, poseTarget, readCameraCode } from '../../../engine';\nimport { {{name}}Camera } from './{{name}}';\n\n/** Where it stands when its code gives no numbers. */\nconst { distance, height } = {{name}}Camera().spec();\n\n/** `point` rounded to the centimetre. */\nconst cm = (point: readonly number[]) => point.map((v) => Math.round(v * 100) / 100 + 0);\n\ndescribe('cam3={{name}}', () => {\n  it('is made from its code and gives the code back with its numbers', () => {\n    expect(readCameraCode('cam3={{name}},6')).toMatchObject({ type: 'game', name: '{{name}}' });\n    const camera = createCamera('cam3={{name}},6');\n    expect([camera.type, camera.code()]).toEqual(['{{name}}', `cam3={{name}},6,${height}`]);\n    expect(() => createCamera('cam3={{name}},0')).toThrow(/cam3={{name}},<distance>,<height>/);\n  });\n\n  it('follows the player smoothly as he runs, and a second after he stops stands beside him, looking at him', () => {\n    const camera = createCamera('cam3={{name}}');\n    let last = camera.update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 1 / 60).position; // the first frame finds him\n    let fastest = 0;\n    for (let k = 1; k <= 120; k++) {\n      const at = camera.update({ follow: [0, 0, (5 * k) / 60] }, 1 / 60).position; // south at 5 m/s for 2 s\n      fastest = Math.max(fastest, Math.hypot(...at.map((v, i) => v - last[i])));\n      last = at;\n    }\n    expect(fastest).toBeLessThanOrEqual(5 / 60 + 1e-9); // never faster than he ran: no jump in the picture\n    for (let k = 0; k < 60; k++) camera.update({ follow: [0, 0, 10] }, 1 / 60);\n    const pose = camera.pose();\n    expect(cm(pose.position)).toEqual([distance, height, 10]);\n    expect(cm(poseTarget(pose))).toEqual([0, 1, 10]); // a metre over his feet\n  });\n\n  it('is blended into by the camera rig from another camera, and ends on its own picture', () => {\n    const own = createCamera('cam3={{name}}').update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 1 / 60).position;\n    const away = (at: readonly number[]) => Math.hypot(...at.map((v, i) => v - own[i]));\n    const rig = createCameraRig('cam=iso');\n    rig.update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 1 / 60);\n    rig.set('cam3={{name}}', { blend: true });\n    rig.update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 0.1);\n    expect(away(rig.pose().position)).toBeGreaterThan(0.5); // a tenth of a second in: between the two pictures\n    for (let k = 0; k < 60; k++) rig.update({ follow: [0, 0, 0] }, 1 / 60);\n    expect(away(rig.pose().position)).toBeLessThan(0.01);\n    expect(rig.pose().projection).toBe('perspective');\n  });\n});\n/**\n * @file The camera mode `cam3={{name}}`: what it shows (replace this text; `x docs` puts the first sentence in INDEX).\n * It starts as a side-scroller's dolly: it stands `distance` metres east of the player and `height` metres up, looks\n * west and down at a point a metre over his feet, and follows him with a lag (a time constant of 0.15 real seconds),\n * so a jump or a fall moves the picture smoothly and a stop settles it. Its code is\n * `cam3={{name}}[,<distance>,<height>]` in metres, 8 and 2.5 when left out.\n *\n * Invariants: view state, never the sim's: pure steps over plain data, which `followCamera` wraps in a camera; a\n * checked pose each frame (`makePose`: metres and radians, +Y up, its yaw the heading the intents' `cam` takes); `dt`\n * is real seconds since the last frame, so it frames alike at any frame rate. `defineCamera` adds the mode when the\n * page imports this module; from then `?cam3={{name}}`, `__engine.camera.set('cam3={{name}}', { blend: true })` and\n * the camera rig's blends reach it, and its name is a word no engine camera has.\n *\n * @example\n * import { createCamera } from '../../../engine';\n * const camera = createCamera('cam3={{name}},6');","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01J7vpaqnDTwSbtFACzC779W","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
