{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011zTinKypGrv5PGhFBF8QSD","type":"tool_result","content":"/**\n * @file Resolution modes (PLAN.md WP 2.1): how many pixels the renderer draws for the canvas's size on screen, from\n * the view setting `gfx.resolution`. `pixels` draws the engine's own chunky pixels, at most 330 lines and a whole\n * number of screen pixels each (2 at 540 screen lines, 3 at 720, 4 at 1080), so the picture is crisp and cheap;\n * `balanced` draws half the screen's resolution; `full` all of it. Both reduced modes are shown with\n * `image-rendering: pixelated`, so the browser enlarges them without blurring. `auto`, the default (WP 13.30), draws\n * the mode the page's look asks for (its `resolution`, engine/gfx/post/looks.ts), given by `prefer(mode)`\n * (engine/app/look.ts), and `pixels` while the look names none; the other three hold whatever the look asks.\n *\n * `fitResolution` is the arithmetic, pure and tested in Node; `createResolution` applies it to a renderer and its\n * canvas: `update()` measures the canvas's CSS size and the device pixel ratio (capped at `MAX_PIXEL_RATIO`) and,\n * when the size, the ratio or the mode changed since the last call, sets the drawing buffer (`setPixelRatio(1)`, then\n * `setSize(width, height, false)`). The page sizes the canvas with CSS; this module never writes its CSS size. The\n * frame calls `update()` first (engine/gfx/renderer.ts), so a resize or a mode change takes effect on the next frame.\n *\n * The picture's glide (WP 13.24): `shift(x, y)` moves the whole picture by a fraction of a pixel (CSS `transform`), the\n * remainder a camera snapped to the pixel grid took off (engine/gfx/post/snap.ts `snapGrid`), so a snapped picture\n * still glides as the camera does; the CSS variable `--my3dge-pixel`, one drawn pixel's size in CSS pixels, lets the\n * page give its canvas a margin of two drawn pixels so the glide never shows an edge (labs/playground/index.html).\n *\n * Phones (WP 3.12, built, not tested): `cap(budget)` caps the buffer's pixels and the pixel ratio by the quality tier\n * (engine/gfx/quality.ts; `pixels` mode by a coarser whole scale), and `keepWidth` widens a portrait picture's\n * vertical field of view so the horizontal one is kept (`2·atan(tan(fov/2)/aspect)`, at most 150°).\n *\n * Invariants: the drawing buffer is at least 1 × 1; in `pixels` mode its height is at most `PIXEL_LINES`, and,\n * uncapped, at least 200 whenever the screen has that many lines and a whole-pixel scale allows it (all but 331 to\n * 399). Changing the size never builds a pipeline (measured, WP 2.1). The setting is a view setting: never in the hash.\n * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/50-frame.js:18-28` (`fit()`, its modes and numbers), and the caps and the\n * portrait lens from `shardfall:crates/pav_app/src/quality.rs:73-84` and `shardfall:crates/pav_view/src/camera.rs:168-173`.\n *\n * @example\n * fitResolution({ width: 1280, height: 720 }, 1, 'pixels'); // { width: 427, height: 240, scale: 3, … }\n * fitResolution({ width: 1280, height: 720 }, 1, 'balanced').width; // 640\n * fitResolution({ width: 1280, height: 720 }, 2, 'full').height; // 1440\n * // resolution.prefer('full'); // the look's mode, drawn while gfx.resolution is 'auto'\n * @see engine/gfx/resolution.test.ts\n */\nengine/gfx/post/filters.ts:150:/** Whether `chain` has every filter off, so it would leave the picture as the scene draws it (`look:clean`). */\nengine/gfx/post/chain.ts:16: * frame (`look:clean`) skips the chain's passes and draws the scene straight (`Post.plain`); the warm-up's frame draws\nengine/gfx/post/looks.ts:28: * Invariants: every look's levels are 0 or 8 and more (the kind's check); a missing id falls back to `look:clean`\nengine/gfx/post/looks.ts:170:  'look:clean': { description: 'No filter: the picture as the scene draws it, in its own style.' },\nengine/gfx/post/looks.ts:348:      fallback: 'look:clean',\n12: * what it asks of the whole picture: `resolution`, the mode drawn while `gfx.resolution` is `auto`\n13: * (engine/gfx/resolution.ts), and `textures`, every material's maps filtered `smooth` or `pixel`\n46:import { RESOLUTION_MODES } from '../resolution';\n130:      \"The look drawn instead while the camera is a perspective one (its variant: same style, sky, resolution and textures); '' keeps this.\",\n132:  resolution: {\n137:      \"The resolution mode it is drawn at while gfx.resolution is 'auto' (pixels, balanced or full); '' keeps the engine's pixels.\",\n240:    description: 'Low resolution on a curved tube with scanlines, glow and colour fringes.',\naa.ts\nchain.ts\ncombine.ts\ncomposite.ts\nfilterChecks.ts\nfilterFields.ts\nfilterFieldsMore.ts\nfilterSchema.ts\nfilterUniforms.ts\nfilters.ts\ngrid.ts\nlooks.ts\nmrt.ts\nparticles.ts\npresets.ts\npresetsMore.ts\nresolveLook.ts\nsettings.ts\nshaders\nshading.ts\nsnap.ts\n/**\n * @file The light pool (PLAN.md WP 7.1, I-17): a fixed set of point lights near the focus. A level holds many light\n * spots (the hall's 30 torches, engine/world/level); every lit shader would pay for each light in the scene, and\n * adding or removing one rebuilds every lit pipeline (r182's lights node keys shaders on the light list). So the pool\n * makes `size` point lights once (8 to 16; 12 by default), never adds or removes one, and on each frame's `update`\n * gives them to the light sources that matter most near the focus: the shaders never rebuild as the focus moves. On a\n * renderer with tiled lighting (`feature:tiledLighting`, engine/gfx/enhanced/lighting.ts) it may hold up to 64\n * (`tiled`), since a lit shader then pays for the lights of its screen tile alone.\n *\n * Choosing: a source's rank is its distance from the focus divided by its kind's `priority` (a light of priority 2\n * counts as if it were half as far), ties going to the earlier source; the `size` best take the slots in rank order,\n * so the first slots, which cast shadows within the budget (engine/gfx/shadows.ts), hold the most important lights.\n * Lights near the cut-off fade out over the last `FADE` of the rank (the rank of the first source left out), so a\n * source handed from one frame's choice to the next never pops. A slot with no source shines with intensity 0.\n *\n * Lamps (WP 13.23): `update` also ranks the frame's lamps, the props' lights that are on (engine/gfx/props.ts), each\n * with its own colour, intensity and reach; one standing on a level's spot of its kind is that spot's, lit once.\n *\n * Light kinds (registry kind `light`): what a level's light-spot tag means, as three.js's `PointLight` parameters\n * (`color`, `intensity` in candela, `distance`, `decay`) plus the pool's `priority` and how much it `flicker`s; the\n * spot tag `torch` is `light:torch`. Flicker is the prototype's torch flicker (`FLICKER`, about ±13% round 0.9), each\n * source at a phase drawn from the random stream `setSources` is given: a visual stream (`world.fxRng`), never\n * hashed, so lights never change play.\n *\n * Invariants: the pool's light count never changes, nor any light's `castShadow` except through the shadow budget;\n * equal sources, focus, time and stream give equal slots; nothing here reads or writes the sim.\n *\n * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/10-hall.js:435-466`: the fixed light set, `FLICKER`, the torch's colour,\n * reach and decay (`TORCH_COLOR`, `TORCH_REACH`, 0.5), its intensity (`LIGHT.torch`), and the shadow lights taking\n * the lit torches nearest the hero.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"e635ee3b-3656-438c-aa0c-27724ec14560","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:51:17.304Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/**\n * @file Resolution modes (PLAN.md WP 2.1): how many pixels the renderer draws for the canvas's size on screen, from\n * the view setting `gfx.resolution`. `pixels` draws the engine's own chunky pixels, at most 330 lines and a whole\n * number of screen pixels each (2 at 540 screen lines, 3 at 720, 4 at 1080), so the picture is crisp and cheap;\n * `balanced` draws half the screen's resolution; `full` all of it. Both reduced modes are shown with\n * `image-rendering: pixelated`, so the browser enlarges them without blurring. `auto`, the default (WP 13.30), draws\n * the mode the page's look asks for (its `resolution`, engine/gfx/post/looks.ts), given by `prefer(mode)`\n * (engine/app/look.ts), and `pixels` while the look names none; the other three hold whatever the look asks.\n *\n * `fitResolution` is the arithmetic, pure and tested in Node; `createResolution` applies it to a renderer and its\n * canvas: `update()` measures the canvas's CSS size and the device pixel ratio (capped at `MAX_PIXEL_RATIO`) and,\n * when the size, the ratio or the mode changed since the last call, sets the drawing buffer (`setPixelRatio(1)`, then\n * `setSize(width, height, false)`). The page sizes the canvas with CSS; this module never writes its CSS size. The\n * frame calls `update()` first (engine/gfx/renderer.ts), so a resize or a mode change takes effect on the next frame.\n *\n * The picture's glide (WP 13.24): `shift(x, y)` moves the whole picture by a fraction of a pixel (CSS `transform`), the\n * remainder a camera snapped to the pixel grid took off (engine/gfx/post/snap.ts `snapGrid`), so a snapped picture\n * still glides as the camera does; the CSS variable `--my3dge-pixel`, one drawn pixel's size in CSS pixels, lets the\n * page give its canvas a margin of two drawn pixels so the glide never shows an edge (labs/playground/index.html).\n *\n * Phones (WP 3.12, built, not tested): `cap(budget)` caps the buffer's pixels and the pixel ratio by the quality tier\n * (engine/gfx/quality.ts; `pixels` mode by a coarser whole scale), and `keepWidth` widens a portrait picture's\n * vertical field of view so the horizontal one is kept (`2·atan(tan(fov/2)/aspect)`, at most 150°).\n *\n * Invariants: the drawing buffer is