{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjBSepSsvHRuA47GP8kH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MTZv5tnxXeWFQaWBjda7jV","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat tools/templates/prop/*.ts.tmpl | sed -n 1,60p | cut -c1-170; awk 'NR==1 && /^\\/\\*\\*/{f=1} f{print} /\\*\\//{if(f){exit}}' engine/gfx/stageProps.ts | cut -c1-190; grep -n \"gfx.warmUp\" -r engine/gfx/warmup.ts | head -5 | cut -c1-190; awk 'NR==1 && /^\\/\\*\\*/{f=1} f{print} /\\*\\//{if(f){exit}}' engine/gfx/warmup.ts | cut -c1-190 | head -40","description":"Read the prop template, stage props and warm-up rules"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2732,"cache_read_input_tokens":375972,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2732},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"12bdf934-bde6-4079-be54-45d9719a656a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:52:41.348Z","request_id":"req_011CgjBSeUcrW11itek4G6ak","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01MTZv5tnxXeWFQaWBjda7jV":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/proto && cat tools/templates/prop/*.ts.tmpl | sed -n 1,60p | cut -c1-170; awk 'NR==1 && /^\\/\\*\\*/{f=1} f{print} /\\*\\//{if(f){exit}}' engine/gfx/stageProps.ts | cut -c1-190; grep -n \"gfx.warmUp\" -r engine/gfx/warmup.ts | head -5 | cut -c1-190; awk 'NR==1 && /^\\/\\*\\*/{f=1} f{print} /\\*\\//{if(f){exit}}' engine/gfx/warmup.ts | cut -c1-190 | head -40","description":"Read the prop template, stage props and warm-up rules"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01MTZv5tnxXeWFQaWBjda7jV":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/.research/proto"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MTZv5tnxXeWFQaWBjda7jV","type":"tool_result","content":"/**\n * @file Unit tests for the prop `prop:{{name}}` ({{id}}.ts): its parts build clean (no degenerate triangles,\n * consistent normals), stand on its base, and reach as high as its body, which stands on the base where it is placed.\n * Each part is measured where it stands in the prop (`partMatrix`: its position and rotation), so a part turned on\n * its side counts its turned height.\n * @see {{id}}.ts\n */\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\nimport { buildPropPart, inspectGeometry, partMatrix, propBody } from '../../../engine';\nimport { {{name}} } from './{{name}}';\n\ndescribe('prop:{{name}}', () => {\n  it('builds clean parts that stand on its base, as tall as its body', () => {\n    let top = 0;\n    for (const part of {{name}}.parts) {\n      const report = inspectGeometry(buildPropPart(part).applyMatrix4(partMatrix(part)));\n      expect([report.missing, report.degenerate, report.flipped, report.badNormals], part.name).toEqual([[], 0, 0, 0]);\n      expect(report.min[1], part.name).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(-1e-6);\n      top = Math.max(top, report.max[1]);\n    }\n    const body = propBody({ prop: {{name}}.id, at: [2, 0, 3], yaw: 0 });\n    [2, top / 2, 3].forEach((value, k) => expect(body?.position[k]).toBeCloseTo(value, 5));\n  });\n});\n/**\n * @file The prop `prop:{{name}}`: what it is (replace this text; `x docs` puts the first sentence in INDEX).\n * It starts as a small wooden chest, a box of planks under a lid, that slides and tumbles when it is hit.\n *\n * Invariants: its parts stand on its base (the origin, +Y up), in metres, built from geometry and materials by id\n * (`node x describe geometry`, `node x describe material`); its body's shape stands on the base too, as tall as the\n * parts; `node x qa geo tex --only prop:{{name}}` checks its parts and their textures.\n *\n * @example\n * {{name}}.body.type; // 'dynamic'\n * @see {{id}}.test.ts\n */\nimport { defineProp } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\n\n/** The prop `prop:{{name}}`: replace this sentence with what it is. */\nexport const {{name}} = defineProp('prop:{{name}}', {\n  description: 'A small wooden chest: a box of planks under a lid (replace).',\n  parts: [\n    {\n      name: 'box',\n      geometry: 'geometry:box',\n      params: { width: 0.6, height: 0.3, depth: 0.4 },\n      material: 'material:planks',\n    },\n    {\n      name: 'lid',\n      geometry: 'geometry:box',\n      params: { width: 0.62, height: 0.15, depth: 0.42 },\n      material: 'material:planks',\n      position: [0, 0.3, 0],\n      tint: '#c8a080',\n    },\n  ],\n  body: { type: 'dynamic', shape: 'box', size: [0.6, 0.45, 0.4] },\n  place: { spawn: '{{name}}' },\n  gallery: { camera: 'turntable', duration: 0, states: [] }, // node x gallery: four views round it\n/**\n * @file Props on the stage (PLAN.md WP 4.6 core proposal, ADR-0008 amendment 5; §6.4's view): every entity with the\n * component `sceneProp` (engine/sim/props.ts: a prop standing in a scene, its id and its pose) drawn as its prop\n * through the prop meshes (engine/gfx/props.ts: one instanced batch per prop part, so a hundred crates are one draw\n * call), where its pose is at the frame's alpha. The stage (engine/gfx/stage.ts) draws these instead of stand-ins.\n *\n * `capture(entities, dt)` after each sim step keeps each prop's last two poses (centre and turn) and returns the ids\n * it draws; `draw(alpha)` draws each `alpha` of the way from the one before to the last (the centre along the line,\n * the turn the shorter way round), at presentation time (the steps captured, `alpha` of a step on), on which the\n * flames dance; each prop is drawn under its entity id, so its flames keep their own phase, and on or off as its\n * `propLight` says (a lamp its switch turned off: WP 13.16); `hide(test, key)` leaves out those on a storey the cut\n * takes off (engine/gfx/storeyCut.ts), lights and all, and says when its key changed. `lights()` lists this frame's\n * light spots in world space (the brazier's torch, the lamps that are on), for the light pool (WP 7.1). `carry(guns)`\n * adds the guns in the actors' hands (engine/gfx/puppets/actors.ts `carried`, WP 13.20) to the draws after it, each as\n * its weapon's prop where its grip stands, never hidden by the cut (its actor is not).\n *\n * The prop meshes are made when the first prop arrives, with a batch for every registered prop, and added to the\n * stage's scene then: a stage with no prop has none, and its scene graph is the one it had before props. `register`\n * registers their batches with the warm-up and adds whether there are any to the shader key, so the frame after they\n * arrive warms up first instead of stalling.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only (doctrine: WebGPU only): it reads the components and writes nothing back; equal\n * captures and alphas give equal matrices; every object it makes is under the prop meshes' root.\n *\n * @example\n * // const props = createStageProps({ scene: stage.scene, materials: library });\n * // const drawn = props.capture(world.query('sceneProp'), world.dt); // the ids drawn as props\n * // props.draw(0.5); // halfway between the last two steps\n * @see engine/gfx/stageProps.test.ts\n */\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:28: * idea for a registry. What a page's warm-ups compile is the view setting `gfx.warmUp` (`WARMUP_SETTINGS`).\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:65: * Its view setting `gfx.warmUp` (ADR-0008 amendment 11): what a page's warm-ups compile. r182 builds a shader for each\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:74:  'gfx.warmUp': {\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:85:/** Whether `settings` ask a page's warm-ups to compile only what it has drawn (`gfx.warmUp: asked`). */\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:86:export const warmsAsked = (settings: { get(path: string): unknown }): boolean => settings.get('gfx.warmUp') === 'asked';\n/**\n * @file The warm-up registry (PLAN.md §8.7, I-18): every GPU pipeline is built before play needs it, so no frame\n * stalls on one. Pools and batches register the objects they will show (`register(id, { objects })`); `run(view)`\n * makes each drawable without showing it, compiles the scene with three.js's `compileAsync` one step per entry with\n * progress, then draws one warm frame through the view's own `draw`, which builds what `compileAsync` cannot reach\n * (the shadow, post and output passes). `key(scene)` is the scene's shader key: fog, environment, background, shadow\n * map, tone mapping, output colour space, the lights as r182's `LightsNode` keys them (id and `castShadow`), the\n * registered ids, and the parts other modules add (`keyPart`, the post filters of WP 7.4). The frame re-runs the\n * warm-up whenever the key changes (engine/gfx/renderer.ts), so toggling fog, shadows, a filter or a light rebuilds\n * at a moment of the engine's choosing, never in the middle of a frame.\n *\n * Measured on r182 (WP 2.1): `compileAsync` compiles for the render target set on the renderer, and `render()` draws\n * a scene into an internal half-float target whenever it converts colours for the screen, so a plain `compileAsync`\n * built 4 pipelines `render()` never used and missed 4 it did. The warm-up therefore compiles for each target the\n * frame really draws the scene into, its passes (`view.passes`): a post pass's `renderTarget`, set up by the pass's\n * `enter` (its MRT on), or, by default, what `render()` draws into: a 1 × 1 stand-in with the internal target's\n * attachments, which shares its render context. The warm frame catches the rest.\n *\n * Compile, never draw, what `compileAsync` reaches. Measured on SwiftShader (gate G6's review): a pipeline the warm\n * frame builds is built on its own, one after another, about 45 ms each, while `compileAsync` builds its pipelines\n * side by side, about 7 ms each; the Stress Box hall's warm-up through a post chain whose plain frames were left to\n * the warm frame built 155 of its pipelines there, and its warm frame took 6 to 7 s a camera.\n *\n * Drawable without showing: each registered object, and each hidden ancestor of it, is made visible, and the\n * topmost one made visible is scaled to nothing; an `InstancedMesh` with no instances gets one, at zero scale; every\n * object in the scene is drawn without frustum culling, so what is behind the camera is built too. All of it is put\n * back afterwards, in reverse order. Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/50-frame.js:29-47` (`warmUp()`) as the\n * idea for a registry. What a page's warm-ups compile is the view setting `gfx.warmUp` (`WARMUP_SETTINGS`).\n *\n * Invariants: one warm-up runs at a time (a second `run` returns the first's promise); entries and key parts are kept\n * in id order; the report counts the pipelines this warm-up built (counted at the device, engine/gfx/pipelines.ts).\n *\n * @example\n * // const warmup = createWarmup({ renderer, counter: countPipelines(device) });\n * // warmup.register('pool:sparks', { objects: () => [sparks.mesh] });\n * // const report = await warmup.run({ scene, camera }, { onProgress: ({ done, total }) => bar(done / total) });\n * @see tests/e2e/renderer.spec.ts\n * @see engine/gfx/warmup.test.ts\n */","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"845bf5a9-195a-4143-9f3f-189ba14bc055","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:52:41.393Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/**\n * @file Unit tests for the prop `prop:{{name}}` ({{id}}.ts): its parts build clean (no degenerate triangles,\n * consistent normals), stand on its base, and reach as high as its body, which stands on the base where it is placed.\n * Each part is measured where it stands in the prop (`partMatrix`: its position and rotation), so a part turned on\n * its side counts its turned height.\n * @see {{id}}.ts\n */\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\nimport { buildPropPart, inspectGeometry, partMatrix, propBody } from '../../../engine';\nimport { {{name}} } from './{{name}}';\n\ndescribe('prop:{{name}}', () => {\n  it('builds clean parts that stand on its base, as tall as its body', () => {\n    let top = 0;\n    for (const part of {{name}}.parts) {\n      const report = inspectGeometry(buildPropPart(part).applyMatrix4(partMatrix(part)));\n      expect([report.missing, report.degenerate, report.flipped, report.badNormals], part.name).toEqual([[], 0, 0, 0]);\n      expect(report.min[1], part.name).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(-1e-6);\n      top = Math.max(top, report.max[1]);\n    }\n    const body = propBody({ prop: {{name}}.id, at: [2, 0, 3], yaw: 0 });\n    [2, top / 2, 3].forEach((value, k) => expect(body?.position[k]).toBeCloseTo(value, 5));\n  });\n});\n/**\n * @file The prop `prop:{{name}}`: what it is (replace this text; `x docs` puts the first sentence in INDEX).\n * It starts as a small wooden chest, a box of planks under a lid, that slides and tumbles when it is hit.\n *\n * Invariants: its parts stand on its base (the origin, +Y up), in metres, built from geometry and materials by id\n * (`node x describe geometry`, `node x describe material`); its body's shape stands on the base too, as tall as the\n * parts; `node x qa geo tex --only prop:{{name}}` checks its parts and their textures.\n *\n * @example\n * {{name}}.body.type; // 'dynamic'\n * @see {{id}}.test.ts\n */\nimport { defineProp } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\n\n/** The prop `prop:{{name}}`: replace this sentence with what it is. */\nexport const {{name}} = defineProp('prop:{{name}}', {\n  description: 'A small wooden chest: a box of planks under a lid (replace).',\n  parts: [\n    {\n      name: 'box',\n      geometry: 'geometry:box',\n      params: { width: 0.6, height: 0.3, depth: 0.4 },\n      material: 'material:planks',\n    },\n    {\n      name: 'lid',\n      geometry: 'geometry:box',\n      params: { width: 0.62, height: 0.15, depth: 0.42 },\n      material: 'material:planks',\n      position: [0, 0.3, 0],\n      tint: '#c8a080',\n    },\n  ],\n  body: { type: 'dynamic', shape: 'box', size: [0.6, 0.45, 0.4] },\n  place: { spawn: '{{name}}' },\n  gallery: { camera: 'turntable', duration: 0, states: [] }, // node x gallery: four views round it\n/**\n * @file Props on the stage (PLAN.md WP 4.6 core proposal, ADR-0008 amendment 5; §6.4's view): every entity with the\n * component `sceneProp` (engine/sim/props.ts: a prop standing in a scene, its id and its pose) drawn as its prop\n * through the prop meshes (engine/gfx/props.ts: one instanced batch per prop part, so a hundred crates are one draw\n * call), where its pose is at the frame's alpha. The stage (engine/gfx/stage.ts) draws these instead of stand-ins.\n *\n * `capture(entities, dt)` after each sim step keeps each prop's last two poses (centre and turn) and returns the ids\n * it draws; `draw(alpha)` draws each `alpha` of the way from the one before to the last (the centre along the line,\n * the turn the shorter way round), at presentation time (the steps captured, `alpha` of a step on), on which the\n * flames dance; each prop is drawn under its entity id, so its flames keep their own phase, and on or off as its\n * `propLight` says (a lamp its switch turned off: WP 13.16); `hide(test, key)` leaves out those on a storey the cut\n * takes off (engine/gfx/storeyCut.ts), lights and all, and says when its key changed. `lights()` lists this frame's\n * light spots in world space (the brazier's torch, the lamps that are on), for the light pool (WP 7.1). `carry(guns)`\n * adds the guns in the actors' hands (engine/gfx/puppets/actors.ts `carried`, WP 13.20) to the draws after it, each as\n * its weapon's prop where its grip stands, never hidden by the cut (its actor is not).\n *\n * The prop meshes are made when the first prop arrives, with a batch for every registered prop, and added to the\n * stage's scene then: a stage with no prop has none, and its scene graph is the one it had before props. `register`\n * registers their batches with the warm-up and adds whether there are any to the shader key, so the frame after they\n * arrive warms up first instead of stalling.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only (doctrine: WebGPU only): it reads the components and writes nothing back; equal\n * captures and alphas give equal matrices; every object it makes is under the prop meshes' root.\n *\n * @example\n * // const props = createStageProps({ scene: stage.scene, materials: library });\n * // const drawn = props.capture(world.query('sceneProp'), world.dt); // the ids drawn as props\n * // props.draw(0.5); // halfway between the last two steps\n * @see engine/gfx/stageProps.test.ts\n */\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:28: * idea for a registry. What a page's warm-ups compile is the view setting `gfx.warmUp` (`WARMUP_SETTINGS`).\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:65: * Its view setting `gfx.warmUp` (ADR-0008 amendment 11): what a page's warm-ups compile. r182 builds a shader for each\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:74:  'gfx.warmUp': {\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:85:/** Whether `settings` ask a page's warm-ups to compile only what it has drawn (`gfx.warmUp: asked`). */\nengine/gfx/warmup.ts:86:export const warmsAsked = (settings: { get(path: string): unknown }): boolean => settings.get('gfx.warmUp') === 'asked';\n/**\n * @file The warm-up registry (PLAN.md §8.7, I-18): every GPU pipeline is built before play needs it, so no frame\n * stalls on one. Pools and batches register the objects they will show (`register(id, { objects })`); `run(view)`\n * makes each drawable without showing it, compiles the scene with three.js's `compileAsync` one step per entry with\n * progress, then draws one warm frame through the view's own `draw`, which builds what `compileAsync` cannot reach\n * (the shadow, post and output passes). `key(scene)` is the scene's shader key: fog, environment, background, shadow\n * map, tone mapping, output colour space, the lights as r182's `LightsNode` keys them (id and `castShadow`), the\n * registered ids, and the parts other modules add (`keyPart`, the post filters of WP 7.4). The frame re-runs the\n * warm-up whenever the key changes (engine/gfx/renderer.ts), so toggling fog, shadows, a filter or a light rebuilds\n * at a moment of the engine's choosing, never in the middle of a frame.\n *\n * Measured on r182 (WP 2.1): `compileAsync` compiles for the render target set on the renderer, and `render()` draws\n * a scene into an internal half-float target whenever it converts colours for the screen, so a plain `compileAsync`\n * built 4 pipelines `render()` never used and missed 4 it did. The warm-up therefore compiles for each target the\n * frame really draws the scene into, its passes (`view.passes`): a post pass's `renderTarget`, set up by the pass's\n * `enter` (its MRT on), or, by default, what `render()` draws into: a 1 × 1 stand-in with the internal target's\n * attachments, which shares its render context. The warm frame catches the rest.\n *\n * Compile, never draw, what `compileAsync` reaches. Measured on SwiftShader (gate G6's review): a pipeline the warm\n * frame builds is built on its own, one after another, about 45 ms each, while `compileAsync` builds its pipelines\n * side by side, about 7 ms each; the Stress Box hall's warm-up through a post chain whose plain frames were left to\n * the warm frame built 155 of its pipelines there, and its warm frame took 6 to 7 s a camera.\n *\n * Drawable without showing: each registered object, and each hidden ancestor of it, is made visible, and the\n * topmost one made visible is scaled to nothing; an `InstancedMesh` with no instances gets one, at zero scale; every\n * object in the scene is drawn without frustum culling, so what is behind the camera is built too. All of it is put\n * back afterwards, in reverse order. Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/50-frame.js:29-47` (`warmUp()`) as the\n * idea for a registry. What a page's warm-ups compile is the view setting `gfx.warmUp` (`WARMUP_SETTINGS`).\n *\n * Invariants: one warm-up runs at a time (a second `run` returns the first's promise); entries and key parts are kept\n * in id order; the report counts the pipelines this warm-up built (counted at the device, engine/gfx/pipelines.ts).\n *\n * @example\n * // const warmup = createWarmup({ renderer, counter: countPipelines(device) });\n * // warmup.register('pool:sparks', { objects: () => [sparks.mesh] });\n * // const report = await warmup.run({ scene, camera }, { onProgress: ({ done, total }) => bar(done / total) });\n * @see tests/e2e/renderer.spec.ts\n * @see engine/gfx/warmup.test.ts\n */","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MTZv5tnxXeWFQaWBjda7jV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
