{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjEw1QawRQqRktR3uDNf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NkYWkSo1zjZcWY6wa1sdUB","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":140},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2995,"cache_read_input_tokens":618503,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2995},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"55733e09-d921-461e-a056-b11f33bde64f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:37:35.239Z","request_id":"req_011CgjEw11GnxSnCGasFnWTt","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01NkYWkSo1zjZcWY6wa1sdUB":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":140}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NkYWkSo1zjZcWY6wa1sdUB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: an original gothic co-op action RPG, inspired by the source named in TASK.md\n2\t\n3\tYou are building this game from this file, alone. Read it once, start to finish, before your first command. Everything\n4\tmarked **Verified** was run on this computer against engine commit `e7a1243` and session client 1.4.0 with a throwaway\n5\tprototype, so trust it over your instincts. Where a number is given without \"verified\", it is a starting value: tune it.\n6\t\n7\tPaths are relative to your dev folder. TASK.md gives the names of your dev folder, your deliverable HTML and your\n8\texit-interview file; this plan says \"the dev folder\", \"the deliverable\" and \"the exit interview\".\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 0. Ground rules\n13\t\n14\t1. **Title.** Pick it the way TASK.md says, first, since your folder name needs it. It is a playful riff, so it never\n15\t   contains the source's name unchanged. It goes on the title screen, in `<title>` and on the About screen. Everything\n16\t   below is written so it fits any title; the title screen adds the subtitle *A Chronicle of the Long Vesper*.\n17\t2. **An original game, not a recreation.** Copy the genre and how it feels: its mechanics, its pacing, its operatic doom.\n18\t   Copy nothing else. World, characters, names, levels, art, UI look and music are all this plan's own.\n19\t   - Never use these or anything like them: Sanctuary, Tristram, Caldeum, Westmarch, Pandemonium, Horadrim, Nephalem, Cain,\n20\t     Leah, Adria, Tyrael, Imperius, Auriel, Azmodan, Belial, Malthael, Leoric, the Butcher, Prime Evil, Soulstone, Kanai,\n21\t     Paragon, Torment, Greater Rift. No class called Barbarian, Demon Hunter, Witch Doctor, Monk, Wizard, Crusader or\n22\t     Necromancer.\n23\t   - No twin red and blue orbs flanked by statues, no flaming-letter logo, no falling star on a cathedral, no recreated\n24\t     town or act, no quoted melody. Use no outside art, audio or text from the source, and do not go looking for any.\n25\t3. **Done means done.** No TODOs, placeholders, stubs or \"coming soon\". If time runs short, cut *quantity*, in this\n26\t   order, and never a system or a class: chunks per biome (not below 8), Reliquaries (not below 10), lore books (not below\n27\t   6), dialogue variety, Vigil Wardens (not below 1).\n28\t4. **Leave git alone.** The starter's AGENTS.md says \"commit as you go\", but your dev folder sits inside another\n29\t   repository. Don't commit, push, or `git init`.\n30\t5. **Share the machine** as TASK.md says: one or two browsers at a time, OS-picked ports on 127.0.0.1, stop only your own\n31\t   PIDs. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n32\t6. **Performance is out of scope.** Add all the detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows the scene wants. The two\n33\t   architecture calls in §3 that look like optimisation are there because the alternative makes every test take minutes\n34\t   and breaks real-time play, not to chase a frame rate.\n35\t7. **Research:** none is needed beyond this plan. Say so in your Source Information unless you do some.\n36\t\n37\t---\n38\t\n39\t## 1. The game\n40\t\n41\t**Genre and perspective:** an isometric hack-and-slash action RPG for 1 to 4 players, from a fixed three-quarter camera.\n42\t\n43\t**Pitch.** On the ninth night of the Long Vesper, the great bell Old Sorrow rang, though it has had no clapper for three\n44\thundred years, and the dead got up. You are one of the Unsung, mortals the High Choir above and the Undersong below both\n45\tforgot to count. The kindly Canoness of Evensong begs you to climb the Minster of Saint Oriel and silence the bell. You\n46\tfight your way up through crypt and drowned nave and you do it. Then the world goes quiet and you learn what the bell was\n47\tfor: for three hundred years it had been holding the Undersong down. The Canoness has waited forty years for someone to\n48\tcut that rope. Now you go down into the Undersong, tear out the heart of the First Voice, cast a new clapper, and ring\n49\tOld Sorrow with your own hands.\n50\t\n51\t**Pillars. Every decision serves one of these.**\n52\t\n53\t- **Crunch.** Every hit has weight: a stop, a flinch, sparks, a number, a sound. Kills fling bodies and burst scenery.\n54\t- **The horde and the fireworks.** Packs of 6 to 20, skills that clear a screen, combos of skill forms that look different.\n55\t- **Loot that sings.** Showers of drops; a Reliquary tolls a bell and rains ash so you hear it before you see it.\n56\t- **Operatic doom with a dry sense of humour.** Villains taunt you from the walls. A narrator mourns everything. The\n57\t  half-deaf bell-tuner was right all along and will not stop saying so.\n58\t- **Friends on a couch or across the hub.** Four players, one world, no lag-hiding tricks that desync.\n59\t\n60\t**The vocabulary.** Use these words everywhere: in code identifiers, UI and dialogue. They keep the game its own.\n61\t\n62\t| Genre idea | This game |\n63\t| --- | --- |\n64\t| The hero's kind | **the Unsung** |\n65\t| Heaven and its servants | **the High Choir**, **the Gilded** (hollow golden statues that walk) |\n66\t| Hell and its rulers | **the Undersong**, **the Voices**, **the Hollow** (its creatures) |\n67\t| Risen dead; the cult | **the Unburied**; **the Unrung** |\n68\t| Skill variants | **Inflections** (each skill has a base form and two more) |\n69\t| Rarities | **Plain** (bone white), **Hallowed** (pale blue), **Gilded** (gold), **Reliquary** (ember crimson, ash and a bell), **Vestment** (verdigris green, sets) |\n70\t| Elite packs | **Anointed** (a pack sharing affixes), **Exalted** (a named leader with minions) |\n71\t| Timed random dungeon | **Vigil**; its tiers are **Hours** 1 to 12; its fill bar is **the Candle**; its boss the **Warden of the Hour** |\n72\t| Post-cap progress | **Devotion** |\n73\t| Difficulties | **Vespers** (normal), **Compline** (hard), **Matins** (expert), **Anguish I to III** |\n74\t| Waypoint; town portal | **Bellpost**; **the Homeward Bell** |\n75\t| Health pickup; potion | **Wafers**; **Sacramental Wine** |\n76\t| Gems; stash | **Tuning Stones**; **the Poor Box** |\n77\t| Kill-streak bonus | **Carnage** |\n78\t| Treasure runner; cursed chest | **Tithe-Imp**; **Shriving Chest** |\n79\t| Money; salvage materials | **silver**; **Iron Filings, Silver Filings, Gilt, Ember Ash** |\n80\t| Damage types | **Physical, Ember, Rime, Blight, Toll** |\n81\t\n82\t**Everything a player must be able to see in this demo:** three classes; 18 skills in 54 forms; passives; 8 gear slots\n83\twith visible gear tiers; five rarities with sets and unique powers; sockets; salvage, crafting and affix rerolls; a town\n84\twith shops and a shared stash; a full story with a twist and an ending; five zones plus the town; 17 monster types; 8\n85\telite affixes; 4 bosses with phases; events, shrines, traps, destructibles, secret walls and lore books; six difficulties;\n86\tVigils with twelve Hours; Devotion; three save slots; couch co-op and hub co-op; settings with rebinding; a dev mode; an\n87\tattract mode; an About screen.\n88\t\n89\t---\n90\t\n91\t## 2. Setup on this machine (verified)\n92\t\n93\tRun in the empty dev folder:\n94\t\n95\t```sh\n96\tsrc=$(mktemp -d) && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge \"$src\" \\\n97\t  && node \"$src/tools/game/new-game.mjs\" . --name game && rm -rf \"$src\"\n98\tnpm ci            # 28 s. Do NOT run scripts/setup.sh: it downloads a Node and a Chrome into ~/.cache, neither needed here\n99\tnpm test          # the starter's 6 tests pass on this machine's Node 24.22.1\n100\texport CHROMIUM_PATH=\"$HOME/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-1243/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell\"\n101\tnode x shot labs/game   # numbers and a text thumbnail; add --png only when a script can't answer the question\n102\t```\n103\t\n104\tThen read `AGENTS.md` and `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` (once), and look things up in `docs/API.md`, `docs/ERRORS.md`, `node x\n105\tdescribe <kind>`, `node x help`. `labs/box/` is the engine's worked example of combat, crowds, props, looks and sound:\n106\tread `labs/box/scenes/fight*.ts`, `labs/box/cast/*.ts` and `labs/box/sounds/*`, copy the patterns, never import it.\n107\t\n108\t**Traps that cost me time. Don't rediscover them.**\n109\t\n110\t| Trap | Fix |\n111\t| --- | --- |\n112\t| This machine sets `TMPDIR` to a long path. Full Chrome dies with \"Socket path too long\"; `npx tsx` dies with `listen EINVAL`. | Point `CHROMIUM_PATH` at the **headless shell** above: `node x shot` works with no `/tmp` use. Write scripts as `.mjs` or Vitest tests. If you must use `tsx`, prefix that one command with `TMPDIR=/tmp`. |\n113\t| TASK.md's WebGPU flag `--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` stalls this engine's warm-up: not ready after 120 s in the headless shell, nor after 40 s in full Chrome. | In your own Playwright scripts launch with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Ready in about 12 s from `file://`, with the default headless shell. |\n114\t| Software rendering draws about 1 or 2 frames a second here with shadows on (611 ms a frame), far fewer with several copies open. | Never wait on frames. Step with `__engine.step(n)`, read state, and call `__engine.render(n)` only when you need a picture. In browser tests add `?quality=low&gfx.warmUp=asked`: verified on a large build, `quality=low` took draw calls from 4,848 to 384 and a frame from 611 ms to 92 ms, and `asked` took startup from 28 s to 13 s. |\n115\t| `node x shot --cam cam=45,...` fails. | Give the bare value: `--cam 45,42,1.5,1,1`. |\n116\t| `python3 -m http.server 0` buffers its \"port\" line when piped. | `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, then read the first line. |\n117\t| `npm run check` runs Prettier and ESLint over all of `labs/game`. | No `any`. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines (hard limit 600): split content tables by class or family. Keep vendored text as `.txt` so nothing reformats it. |\n118\t| `w.add(id, …)` right after a spawn throws `SIM_NO_ENTITY`: a spawn joins the world at the step's end. | Pass everything in the spawn: `spawnActor(w, { …, components: { foe: {} } })`, `spawnProp(w, placement, { components })`. |\n119\t| `npm run dev` binds port 5173, which another agent may hold. | You rarely need it: `node x shot` and the one-file build from `file://` cover the same ground. |\n120\t\n121\t---\n122\t\n123\t## 3. Architecture\n124\t\n125\t### 3.1 Folders\n126\t\n127\tOnly `labs/*/scenes/**` and `labs/*/cast/**` get the engine's determinism lint, so **all sim-side code lives under those\n128\ttwo folders**. Tests sit beside their modules.\n129\t\n130\t```\n131\tlabs/game/\n132\t  index.html            shell: hub metas, #view canvas, #ui root. No session client here (see §13)\n133\t  main.ts               boot order (§3.8)\n134\t  README.md             what the game is, the calls you made, and the designer guide (§3.11)\n135\t  scenes/               SIM-SIDE\n136\t    world.ts            defineScene('game:world'), setup, step\n137\t    settings.ts         balance settings (§3.10)\n138\t    seats.ts            seat commands, hero input, the player flag (§3.6)\n139\t    worldgen/           pure generator, zone table, chunk text per biome, hand-made maps\n140\t    zones.ts            streaming, transitions, Bellposts"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"b3cac746-6daa-472e-9d50-2f0105b4097a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:37:35.245Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: an original gothic co-op action RPG, inspired by the source named in TASK.md\n\nYou are building this game from this file, alone. Read it once, start to finish, before your first command. Everything\nmarked **Verified** was run on this computer against engine commit `e7a1243` and session client 1.4.0 with a throwaway\nprototype, so trust it over your instincts. Where a number is given without \"verified\", it is a starting value: tune it.\n\nPaths are relative to your dev folder. TASK.md gives the names of your dev folder, your deliverable HTML and your\nexit-interview file; this plan says \"the dev folder\", \"the deliverable\" and \"the exit interview\".\n\n---\n\n## 0. Ground rules\n\n1. **Title.** Pick it the way TASK.md says, first, since your folder name needs it. It is a playful riff, so it never\n   contains the source's name unchanged. It goes on the title screen, in `<title>` and on the About screen. Everything\n   below is written so it fits any title; the title screen adds the subtitle *A Chronicle of the Long Vesper*.\n2. **An original game, not a recreation.** Copy the genre and how it feels: its mechanics, its pacing, its operatic doom.\n   Copy nothing else. World, characters, names, levels, art, UI look and music are all this plan's own.\n   - Never use these or anything like them: Sanctuary, Tristram, Caldeum, Westmarch, Pandemonium, Horadrim, Nephalem, Cain,\n     Leah, Adria, Tyrael, Imperius, Auriel, Azmodan, Belial, Malthael, Leoric, the Butcher, Prime Evil, Soulstone, Kanai,\n     Paragon, Torment, Greater Rift. No class called Barbarian, Demon Hunter, Witch Doctor, Monk, Wizard, Crusader or\n     Necromancer.\n   - No twin red and blue orbs flanked by statues, no flaming-letter logo, no falling star on a cathedral, no recreated\n     town or act, no quoted melody. Use no outside art, audio or text from the source, and do not go looking for any.\n3. **Done means done.** No TODOs, placeholders, stubs or \"coming soon\". If time runs short, cut *quantity*, in this\n   order, and never a system or a class: chunks per biome (not below 8), Reliquaries (not below 10), lore books (not below\n   6), dialogue variety, Vigil Wardens (not below 1).\n4. **Leave git alone.** The starter's AGENTS.md says \"commit as you go\", but your dev folder sits inside another\n   repository. Don't commit, push, or `git init`.\n5. **Share the machine** as TASK.md says: one or two browsers at a time, OS-picked ports on 127.0.0.1, stop only your own\n   PIDs. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n6. **Performance is out of scope.** Add all the detail, enemies, effects, lights and shadows the scene wants. The two\n   architecture calls in §3 that look like optimisation are there because the alternative makes every test take minutes\n   and breaks real-time play, not to chase a frame rate.\n7. **Research:** none is needed beyond this plan. Say so in your Source Information unless you do some.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Genre and perspective:** an isometric hack-and-slash action RPG for 1 to 4 players, from a fixed three-quarter camera.\n\n**Pitch.** On the ninth night of the Long Vesper, the great bell Old Sorrow rang, though it has had no clapper for three\nhundred years, and the dead got up. You are one of the Unsung, mortals the High Choir above and the Undersong below both\nforgot to count. The kindly Canoness of Evensong begs you to climb the Minster of Saint Oriel and silence the bell. You\nfight your way up through crypt and drowned nave and you do it. Then the world goes quiet and you learn what the bell was\nfor: for three hundred years it had been holding the Undersong down. The Canoness has waited forty years for someone to\ncut that rope. Now you go down into the Undersong, tear out the heart of the First Voice, cast a new clapper, and ring\nOld Sorrow with your own hands.\n\n**Pillars. Every decision serves one of these.**\n\n- **Crunch.** Every hit has weight: a stop, a flinch, sparks, a number, a sound. Kills fling bodies and burst scenery.\n- **The horde and the fireworks.** Packs of 6 to 20, skills that clear a screen, combos of skill forms that look different.\n- **Loot that sings.** Showers of drops; a Reliquary tolls a bell and rains ash so you hear it before you see it.\n- **Operatic doom with a dry sense of humour.** Villains taunt you from the walls. A narrator mourns everything. The\n  half-deaf bell-tuner was right all along and will not stop saying so.\n- **Friends on a couch or across the hub.** Four players, one world, no lag-hiding tricks that desync.\n\n**The vocabulary.** Use these words everywhere: in code identifiers, UI and dialogue. They keep the game its own.\n\n| Genre idea | This game |\n| --- | --- |\n| The hero's kind | **the Unsung** |\n| Heaven and its servants | **the High Choir**, **the Gilded** (hollow golden statues that walk) |\n| Hell and its rulers | **the Undersong**, **the Voices**, **the Hollow** (its creatures) |\n| Risen dead; the cult | **the Unburied**; **the Unrung** |\n| Skill variants | **Inflections** (each skill has a base form and two more) |\n| Rarities | **Plain** (bone white), **Hallowed** (pale blue), **Gilded** (gold), **Reliquary** (ember crimson, ash and a bell), **Vestment** (verdigris green, sets) |\n| Elite packs | **Anointed** (a pack sharing affixes), **Exalted** (a named leader with minions) |\n| Timed random dungeon | **Vigil**; its tiers are **Hours** 1 to 12; its fill bar is **the Candle**; its boss the **Warden of the Hour** |\n| Post-cap progress | **Devotion** |\n| Difficulties | **Vespers** (normal), **Compline** (hard), **Matins** (expert), **Anguish I to III** |\n| Waypoint; town portal | **Bellpost**; **the Homeward Bell** |\n| Health pickup; potion | **Wafers**; **Sacramental Wine** |\n| Gems; stash | **Tuning Stones**; **the Poor Box** |\n| Kill-streak bonus | **Carnage** |\n| Treasure runner; cursed chest | **Tithe-Imp**; **Shriving Chest** |\n| Money; salvage materials | **silver**; **Iron Filings, Silver Filings, Gilt, Ember Ash** |\n| Damage types | **Physical, Ember, Rime, Blight, Toll** |\n\n**Everything a player must be able to see in this demo:** three classes; 18 skills in 54 forms; passives; 8 gear slots\nwith visible gear tiers; five rarities with sets and unique powers; sockets; salvage, crafting and affix rerolls; a town\nwith shops and a shared stash; a full story with a twist and an ending; five zones plus the town; 17 monster types; 8\nelite affixes; 4 bosses with phases; events, shrines, traps, destructibles, secret walls and lore books; six difficulties;\nVigils with twelve Hours; Devotion; three save slots; couch co-op and hub co-op; settings with rebinding; a dev mode; an\nattract mode; an About screen.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Setup on this machine (verified)\n\nRun in the empty dev folder:\n\n```sh\nsrc=$(mktemp -d) && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge \"$src\" \\\n  && node \"$src/tools/game/new-game.mjs\" . --name game && rm -rf \"$src\"\nnpm ci            # 28 s. Do NOT run scripts/setup.sh: it downloads a Node and a Chrome into ~/.cache, neither needed here\nnpm test          # the starter's 6 tests pass on this machine's Node 24.22.1\nexport CHROMIUM_PATH=\"$HOME/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-1243/chrome-headless-shell-linux64/chrome-headless-shell\"\nnode x shot labs/game   # numbers and a text thumbnail; add --png only when a script can't answer the question\n```\n\nThen read `AGENTS.md` and `docs/GAME-GUIDE.md` (once), and look things up in `docs/API.md`, `docs/ERRORS.md`, `node x\ndescribe <kind>`, `node x help`. `labs/box/` is the engine's worked example of combat, crowds, props, looks and sound:\nread `labs/box/scenes/fight*.ts`, `labs/box/cast/*.ts` and `labs/box/sounds/*`, copy the patterns, never import it.\n\n**Traps that cost me time. Don't rediscover them.**\n\n| Trap | Fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| This machine sets `TMPDIR` to a long path. Full Chrome dies with \"Socket path too long\"; `npx tsx` dies with `listen EINVAL`. | Point `CHROMIUM_PATH` at the **headless shell** above: `node x shot` works with no `/tmp` use. Write scripts as `.mjs` or Vitest tests. If you must use `tsx`, prefix that one command with `TMPDIR=/tmp`. |\n| TASK.md's WebGPU flag `--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader` stalls this engine's warm-up: not ready after 120 s in the headless shell, nor after 40 s in full Chrome. | In your own Playwright scripts launch with `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`. Ready in about 12 s from `file://`, with the default headless shell. |\n| Software rendering draws about 1 or 2 frames a second here with shadows on (611 ms a frame), far fewer with several copies open. | Never wait on frames. Step with `__engine.step(n)`, read state, and call `__engine.render(n)` only when you need a picture. In browser tests add `?quality=low&gfx.warmUp=asked`: verified on a large build, `quality=low` took draw calls from 4,848 to 384 and a frame from 611 ms to 92 ms, and `asked` took startup from 28 s to 13 s. |\n| `node x shot --cam cam=45,...` fails. | Give the bare value: `--cam 45,42,1.5,1,1`. |\n| `python3 -m http.server 0` buffers its \"port\" line when piped. | `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, then read the first line. |\n| `npm run check` runs Prettier and ESLint over all of `labs/game`. | No `any`. Every file opens with a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines (hard limit 600): split content tables by class or family. Keep vendored text as `.txt` so nothing reformats it. |\n| `w.add(id, …)` right after a spawn throws `SIM_NO_ENTITY`: a spawn joins the world at the step's end. | Pass everything in the spawn: `spawnActor(w, { …, components: { foe: {} } })`, `spawnProp(w, placement, { components })`. |\n| `npm run dev` binds port 5173, which another agent may hold. | You rarely need it: `node x shot` and the one-file build from `file://` cover the same ground. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture\n\n### 3.1 Folders\n\nOnly `labs/*/scenes/**` and `labs/*/cast/**` get the engine's determinism lint, so **all sim-side code lives under those\ntwo folders**. Tests sit beside their modules.\n\n```\nlabs/game/\n  index.html            shell: hub metas, #view canvas, #ui root. No session client here (see §13)\n  main.ts               boot order (§3.8)\n  README.md             what the game is, the calls you made, and the designer guide (§3.11)\n  scenes/               SIM-SIDE\n    world.ts            defineScene('game:world'), setup, step\n    settings.ts         balance settings (§3.10)\n    seats.ts            seat commands, hero input, the player flag (§3.6)\n    worldgen/           pure generator, zone table, chunk text per biome, hand-made maps\n    zones.ts            streaming, transitions, Bellposts","numLines":140,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1003}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NkYWkSo1zjZcWY6wa1sdUB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
