{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjEwHn5pU5td53S6bSis","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CVn3NREaRrwdFj58SzAaW5","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/PLAN.md","offset":175,"limit":145},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4872,"cache_read_input_tokens":621498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4872},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"747bc9d6-1406-4790-a0ce-4ec2513854db","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:37:41.908Z","request_id":"req_011CgjEwHHp4tsetxG8L4oeM","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01CVn3NREaRrwdFj58SzAaW5":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/PLAN.md","offset":175,"limit":145}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CVn3NREaRrwdFj58SzAaW5","type":"tool_result","content":"175\t- Verified on a 300 × 300 level: `compileLevel` 70 to 150 ms, `NavGrid` 300 ms, a flow field 0.5 ms, an A* path 0.6 ms.\n176\t\n177\t### 3.3 Floors from the level; walls and everything else as instanced props\n178\t\n179\t**Verified, and the most important number in this plan.** The same 300 × 300 world took **113 s to start** with walls as\n180\tlevel glyphs (each wall run is its own mesh: 1,767 objects, 45,552 draw calls in the warm-up) and **12 to 15 s** with\n181\twalls as props (one instanced batch per part: about 100 objects). Build it the second way.\n182\t\n183\t- Level glyphs: one **floor glyph per floor look** (`mesh: '<recipe>'`, recipe `{ shape: 'surface', materials: [...],\n184\t  castShadow: false, receiveShadow: true }`), each one welded mesh and one draw call. One **undrawn solid glyph** for walls\n185\t  and rock: `{ top: 3, solid: true, collider: 'box', nav: { walkable: false }, full: true }` and no `mesh`.\n186\t- Walls: a body-less prop per wall tile, spawned from the level's tiles with `spawnProp`. No physics body: the undrawn\n187\t  glyph is the collider.\n188\t- **The static cut.** The camera stands south-east and never turns. A wall tile with floor to its north or west would hide\n189\t  that floor, so it spawns as a **low** variant (0.45 m); every other wall is tall (3 m). This replaces the engine's\n190\t  cutaway, which only serves level meshes.\n191\t- **Hide the stage's ground.** The stage draws a `material:stone` plane under the level, which shows through anywhere with\n192\t  no floor. Spawn one body-less `prop:voidSlab` at y −0.012: a box 900 × 0.004 × 900 in a near-black material with `shadow:\n193\t  false`. (A black floor glyph on the rock tiles also works but costs about 400 objects.)\n194\t- Keep a 20-tile rock margin round the world, so no view reaches the slab's edge.\n195\t\n196\t### 3.4 Zone streaming, the green room, and looks\n197\t\n198\t- **Streaming.** Spawn a zone's wall and scenery props when the first hero enters it; despawn them when none is left in\n199\t  it. Its packs, chests and destructibles keep their state in a zone record. This keeps entities to 2,000 or 3,000. A\n200\t  loading fade hides the burst.\n201\t- **The green room.** A new body-and-build pair first drawn mid-play triggers a warm-up: verified, swapping one hero's\n202\t  `puppet.body` to an unseen pair took `warmUps` from 2 to 6. So `setup` spawns one idle actor of every body in a walled-off\n203\t  corner, before the first warm-up, and despawns them after play starts. Assert `__engine.stats().pipelinesAfterWarmUp ===\n204\t  0`, with `warmUps` stable, in the browser test.\n205\t- **Scale check, verified:** 120 extra props of 3 parts each and 20 extra custom bodies took startup from 12 s to 28 s in\n206\t  software rendering, with `pipelines` only 75 to 82 and `pipelinesAfterWarmUp` 0, so define as many props as you like.\n207\t  Set `shadow: false` on small parts: each casting part is one draw per shadow-map face, the reason that build drew 4,848\n208\t  draw calls a frame against 384 with shadows off.\n209\t- **A look per zone.** Each zone has its own `look:` and sun-less `sky:`. Verified: switching `post.look` between looks\n210\t  whose skies have no sun builds nothing (`warmUps` and `pipelines` unchanged). Switch at zone entry behind the fade. Never\n211\t  use a sky with a sun; that changes the shader key.\n212\t\n213\t### 3.5 Characters\n214\t\n215\t- **Heroes and most monsters are actors** with their own `defineBody`: `spawnActor(w, { at, character, player: false, anim:\n216\t  { build, weapon }, body: 'body:x', components: {...} })`. Verified: custom bodies work, and swapping `puppet.body` at run\n217\t  time works, so visible gear is real. Give each hero class three armour tiers and each weapon type a tier: `body:penitentT1`\n218\t  to `T3`, swapped from what is equipped.\n219\t- **Cost, verified in Node under load:** 50 chasing actors 10 ms a step, 100 actors 16 ms, 200 actors 34 ms. The sim must\n220\t  keep real time or lockstep and play both suffer, so the design number is **at most 60 awake actors**, a slider in dev mode.\n221\t  Packs wake at 16 m and sleep or despawn when no hero is within 40 m for 10 s.\n222\t- **Crowd rigs (`addAgent`, `crowdBody`) are much cheaper, but their looks are fixed:** a humanoid is always the neutral\n223\t  shirt-and-trousers body in some build, a blob is pink (with horns, ears or wings), a floater is a wisp, a quadruped is a\n224\t  brown dog. Use them for exactly what suits those looks: Evensong's townsfolk (humanoid), Gristlings (blob), Thuribles and\n225\t  Cinder Wisps (floater), Grave Hounds (quadruped). Swarms of these may run to hundreds.\n226\t- **Bosses and non-humanoids** are an actor plus rigid parts: body-less props whose `sceneProp.x/y/z/q` a system writes each\n227\t  step. Verified: a system-moved body-less prop draws correctly and needs no extra work. Use this for coffin lids, boss\n228\t  wings and arms, orbiting candles and chains, and for anything `PathChain` or `SwayChain` drives.\n229\t- **Speed.** Give every `createCharacter` a high top speed (12 m/s) and scale the `move` vector to the speed you want, so\n230\t  buffs and slows never mutate the character.\n231\t- **Lights.** Only **prop lights** reach the light pool; a light on an emitter did not, in my test. The hero's lantern is a\n232\t  body-less prop with a `light`, which a `rules` system keeps at the hero. Torches and sconces are props too, and the pool\n233\t  picks the nearest.\n234\t- **Projectiles.** Engine bolts (`PROJECTILE_FIELDS`) are always orange. Use them for Ember. For other elements, use your own\n235\t  component with a tinted glowing body-less prop and a `frame.emit` trail, stepping them with the same swept tests.\n236\t\n237\t### 3.6 Seats and hero input\n238\t\n239\tA **seat** is a player slot, 0 to 3. Every hero has a `hero` component with its `seat`. Each step, a system in the `intents`\n240\tphase builds one **command** per seat: `{ m: [x, z], a: [x, y, z], b: [...held], p: [...pressed], t: targetEntityId }`.\n241\t\n242\t```ts\n243\t// scenes/seats.ts, sim-side. Verified: camera follows, hashes match across copies.\n244\tfunction seatCommand(w, seat, intents) {\n245\t  const net = intents['game:seats'];                       // lockstep: every seat's stamped command\n246\t  if (net) return net[seat] ?? {};\n247\t  if (seat === w.settings.get('game.seat'))                // solo or couch player 1: the page's devices\n248\t    return { m: intents.move, a: intents.aim, b: intents.b, p: intents.p, t: intents['game:target'] };\n249\t  return intents['game:pads']?.[seat] ?? {};               // couch players 2 to 4\n250\t}\n251\t// In the intents phase, for every hero, on every copy:\n252\te.actor.player = false;                 // so the actors system reads actor.intents, the same on all copies\n253\te.actor.intents = { move: pathOrStick(e, cmd), b: dodge(cmd) };\n254\t// In the rules phase, after the actors moved:\n255\te.actor.player = e.hero.seat === w.settings.get('game.seat');   // so the view's camera follows this copy's own hero\n256\t```\n257\t\n258\tBecause of that flip, the sim never reads `player` while it decides anything, and a copy's `game.seat` can't change play.\n259\tThe engine's `addMelee`, `addWeapons` and `addInteraction` only serve `player` actors: don't use them on heroes. Write\n260\thero attacks and use on `Attack`, `Combo`, `coneHits`, `damage` and `reactTo`, as `labs/box/scenes/fight-hero.ts` does.\n261\t\n262\t`engine.hash()` covers that flag, so it differs between copies by design. Compare copies with your own `gameHash(world)`:\n263\tFNV over quantised positions, hit points, RNG draws and entity count, computed in the page from `frame.world`.\n264\t\n265\t### 3.7 Commands from menus\n266\t\n267\tMenus change the sim only through `applyCommand(w, seat, cmd)` in `scenes/commands.ts`: equip, buy, salvage, set skill form,\n268\tchoose passive, begin game with save data, travel, use Bellpost. All game state stays in components as plain data.\n269\t\n270\t- **Solo and couch:** `engine.inspector.actions.run('cmd', { seat, cmd })`, a scene dev action. It works while paused and\n271\t  is recorded. It captures the world first, which is fine for a menu press and wrong for per-frame use.\n272\t- **Lockstep:** the command goes into the seat's stamped stream (§12.3) and runs at its tick on every copy.\n273\t\n274\tWhile `modal()` holds the page, the engine's intents are empty, virtual axes included, which is why menus don't use them.\n275\t\n276\t### 3.8 Boot order and drivers\n277\t\n278\t```ts\n279\t// main.ts. Verified order.\n280\tconst params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\n281\tfor (const key of [...params.keys()]) if (key.startsWith('aihub')) params.delete(key);\n282\tconst hub = window.AIHub;                     // exists only in the built file; undefined under `npm run dev`\n283\tconst info = hub?.active ? await hub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;\n284\tconst seed = info ? info.seed >>> 0 : Number(params.get('seed') ?? freshSeed());\n285\tparams.set('seed', String(seed));\n286\t// set game.seat, game.seats, cam=45,42,1.5,1,1 and post.look on params; defineLevel from makeWorld(seed)\n287\tconst startEngine = async () => {\n288\t  engine = await createEngine({ canvas, scenes: ['game:world'], search: `?${params}`, actionMap: 'actionMap:game',\n289\t                                store, modal, onFrame });\n290\t  if (info) engine.inspector.pause();         // from here the lockstep driver alone steps the sim\n291\t};\n292\tif (info) {                                   // a session: card, ready and traffic first, the engine only at the start\n293\t  params.set('gfx.warmUp', 'asked');\n294\t  showCard(info); startLockstep(info); hub.ready();\n295\t  hub.on('start', () => void startEngine());\n296\t} else await startEngine();\n297\t```\n298\t\n299\t- **In a session, `hub.ready()` comes first and `createEngine` waits for `start`. Don't change this order.** Verified the hard\n300\t  way, in this sequence: the harness needs `ready` from every copy within 20 s and ends its default round about 13 s after,\n301\t  and all its copies share one main thread. Calling `ready` after the warm-up failed outright. Calling it before\n302\t  `createEngine` but warming up at once passed on a small build and failed at 4 players on a build with the game's real\n303\t  prop and body counts, because one copy's warm-up starved another's startup. Waiting for `start` passes.\n304\t- **The lockstep driver starts at once too and runs without the engine.** A copy still warming up keeps its stamped log,\n305\t  sends its heartbeat, and catches up by stepping when it is ready; `time-up` and `finish` need no engine at all. On a real\n306\t  GPU the warm-up is a moment, inside the 3 s countdown that follows `start`.\n307\t- **Solo and couch driver:** the engine's normal frame loop, with interpolation, hit-stop and time scale. Extra pads and the\n308\t  hovered target reach the sim through `input.virtual.axis('game:pads', ...)` and `axis('game:target', id)`.\n309\t- **Lockstep driver:** §12.3.\n310\t\n311\t### 3.9 Save, title and restart\n312\t\n313\t- The title screen is HTML over the live world. At boot, the local hero stands in the **Vigil Chapel** class-select stage.\n314\t- **Play** sends a `begin` command with the save's plain data, or the chosen class for a new game; the sim applies it and\n315\t  moves the hero to the start. **Quit to Title** saves and calls `inspector.seed(seed)`. Attract mode uses the same restart\n316\t  to exit.\n317\t- Saves go through `createStore({ prefix, version, migrate })`: three slots plus the shared Poor Box. The sim emits a\n318\t  `game:save` event at Bellposts, zone changes, quest steps and on quit; its payload holds the save; the page writes it.\n319\t- A session's client gives the game an empty storage, so sessions need no special save code."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"81b5f0ce-cea8-4806-a768-ae9343b149ac","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:37:41.915Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/PLAN.md","content":"- Verified on a 300 × 300 level: `compileLevel` 70 to 150 ms, `NavGrid` 300 ms, a flow field 0.5 ms, an A* path 0.6 ms.\n\n### 3.3 Floors from the level; walls and everything else as instanced props\n\n**Verified, and the most important number in this plan.** The same 300 × 300 world took **113 s to start** with walls as\nlevel glyphs (each wall run is its own mesh: 1,767 objects, 45,552 draw calls in the warm-up) and **12 to 15 s** with\nwalls as props (one instanced batch per part: about 100 objects). Build it the second way.\n\n- Level glyphs: one **floor glyph per floor look** (`mesh: '<recipe>'`, recipe `{ shape: 'surface', materials: [...],\n  castShadow: false, receiveShadow: true }`), each one welded mesh and one draw call. One **undrawn solid glyph** for walls\n  and rock: `{ top: 3, solid: true, collider: 'box', nav: { walkable: false }, full: true }` and no `mesh`.\n- Walls: a body-less prop per wall tile, spawned from the level's tiles with `spawnProp`. No physics body: the undrawn\n  glyph is the collider.\n- **The static cut.** The camera stands south-east and never turns. A wall tile with floor to its north or west would hide\n  that floor, so it spawns as a **low** variant (0.45 m); every other wall is tall (3 m). This replaces the engine's\n  cutaway, which only serves level meshes.\n- **Hide the stage's ground.** The stage draws a `material:stone` plane under the level, which shows through anywhere with\n  no floor. Spawn one body-less `prop:voidSlab` at y −0.012: a box 900 × 0.004 × 900 in a near-black material with `shadow:\n  false`. (A black floor glyph on the rock tiles also works but costs about 400 objects.)\n- Keep a 20-tile rock margin round the world, so no view reaches the slab's edge.\n\n### 3.4 Zone streaming, the green room, and looks\n\n- **Streaming.** Spawn a zone's wall and scenery props when the first hero enters it; despawn them when none is left in\n  it. Its packs, chests and destructibles keep their state in a zone record. This keeps entities to 2,000 or 3,000. A\n  loading fade hides the burst.\n- **The green room.** A new body-and-build pair first drawn mid-play triggers a warm-up: verified, swapping one hero's\n  `puppet.body` to an unseen pair took `warmUps` from 2 to 6. So `setup` spawns one idle actor of every body in a walled-off\n  corner, before the first warm-up, and despawns them after play starts. Assert `__engine.stats().pipelinesAfterWarmUp ===\n  0`, with `warmUps` stable, in the browser test.\n- **Scale check, verified:** 120 extra props of 3 parts each and 20 extra custom bodies took startup from 12 s to 28 s in\n  software rendering, with `pipelines` only 75 to 82 and `pipelinesAfterWarmUp` 0, so define as many props as you like.\n  Set `shadow: false` on small parts: each casting part is one draw per shadow-map face, the reason that build drew 4,848\n  draw calls a frame against 384 with shadows off.\n- **A look per zone.** Each zone has its own `look:` and sun-less `sky:`. Verified: switching `post.look` between looks\n  whose skies have no sun builds nothing (`warmUps` and `pipelines` unchanged). Switch at zone entry behind the fade. Never\n  use a sky with a sun; that changes the shader key.\n\n### 3.5 Characters\n\n- **Heroes and most monsters are actors** with their own `defineBody`: `spawnActor(w, { at, character, player: false, anim:\n  { build, weapon }, body: 'body:x', components: {...} })`. Verified: custom bodies work, and swapping `puppet.body` at run\n  time works, so visible gear is real. Give each hero class three armour tiers and each weapon type a tier: `body:penitentT1`\n  to `T3`, swapped from what is equipped.\n- **Cost, verified in Node under load:** 50 chasing actors 10 ms a step, 100 actors 16 ms, 200 actors 34 ms. The sim must\n  keep real time or lockstep and play both suffer, so the design number is **at most 60 awake actors**, a slider in dev mode.\n  Packs wake at 16 m and sleep or despawn when no hero is within 40 m for 10 s.\n- **Crowd rigs (`addAgent`, `crowdBody`) are much cheaper, but their looks are fixed:** a humanoid is always the neutral\n  shirt-and-trousers body in some build, a blob is pink (with horns, ears or wings), a floater is a wisp, a quadruped is a\n  brown dog. Use them for exactly what suits those looks: Evensong's townsfolk (humanoid), Gristlings (blob), Thuribles and\n  Cinder Wisps (floater), Grave Hounds (quadruped). Swarms of these may run to hundreds.\n- **Bosses and non-humanoids** are an actor plus rigid parts: body-less props whose `sceneProp.x/y/z/q` a system writes each\n  step. Verified: a system-moved body-less prop draws correctly and needs no extra work. Use this for coffin lids, boss\n  wings and arms, orbiting candles and chains, and for anything `PathChain` or `SwayChain` drives.\n- **Speed.** Give every `createCharacter` a high top speed (12 m/s) and scale the `move` vector to the speed you want, so\n  buffs and slows never mutate the character.\n- **Lights.** Only **prop lights** reach the light pool; a light on an emitter did not, in my test. The hero's lantern is a\n  body-less prop with a `light`, which a `rules` system keeps at the hero. Torches and sconces are props too, and the pool\n  picks the nearest.\n- **Projectiles.** Engine bolts (`PROJECTILE_FIELDS`) are always orange. Use them for Ember. For other elements, use your own\n  component with a tinted glowing body-less prop and a `frame.emit` trail, stepping them with the same swept tests.\n\n### 3.6 Seats and hero input\n\nA **seat** is a player slot, 0 to 3. Every hero has a `hero` component with its `seat`. Each step, a system in the `intents`\nphase builds one **command** per seat: `{ m: [x, z], a: [x, y, z], b: [...held], p: [...pressed], t: targetEntityId }`.\n\n```ts\n// scenes/seats.ts, sim-side. Verified: camera follows, hashes match across copies.\nfunction seatCommand(w, seat, intents) {\n  const net = intents['game:seats'];                       // lockstep: every seat's stamped command\n  if (net) return net[seat] ?? {};\n  if (seat === w.settings.get('game.seat'))                // solo or couch player 1: the page's devices\n    return { m: intents.move, a: intents.aim, b: intents.b, p: intents.p, t: intents['game:target'] };\n  return intents['game:pads']?.[seat] ?? {};               // couch players 2 to 4\n}\n// In the intents phase, for every hero, on every copy:\ne.actor.player = false;                 // so the actors system reads actor.intents, the same on all copies\ne.actor.intents = { move: pathOrStick(e, cmd), b: dodge(cmd) };\n// In the rules phase, after the actors moved:\ne.actor.player = e.hero.seat === w.settings.get('game.seat');   // so the view's camera follows this copy's own hero\n```\n\nBecause of that flip, the sim never reads `player` while it decides anything, and a copy's `game.seat` can't change play.\nThe engine's `addMelee`, `addWeapons` and `addInteraction` only serve `player` actors: don't use them on heroes. Write\nhero attacks and use on `Attack`, `Combo`, `coneHits`, `damage` and `reactTo`, as `labs/box/scenes/fight-hero.ts` does.\n\n`engine.hash()` covers that flag, so it differs between copies by design. Compare copies with your own `gameHash(world)`:\nFNV over quantised positions, hit points, RNG draws and entity count, computed in the page from `frame.world`.\n\n### 3.7 Commands from menus\n\nMenus change the sim only through `applyCommand(w, seat, cmd)` in `scenes/commands.ts`: equip, buy, salvage, set skill form,\nchoose passive, begin game with save data, travel, use Bellpost. All game state stays in components as plain data.\n\n- **Solo and couch:** `engine.inspector.actions.run('cmd', { seat, cmd })`, a scene dev action. It works while paused and\n  is recorded. It captures the world first, which is fine for a menu press and wrong for per-frame use.\n- **Lockstep:** the command goes into the seat's stamped stream (§12.3) and runs at its tick on every copy.\n\nWhile `modal()` holds the page, the engine's intents are empty, virtual axes included, which is why menus don't use them.\n\n### 3.8 Boot order and drivers\n\n```ts\n// main.ts. Verified order.\nconst params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\nfor (const key of [...params.keys()]) if (key.startsWith('aihub')) params.delete(key);\nconst hub = window.AIHub;                     // exists only in the built file; undefined under `npm run dev`\nconst info = hub?.active ? await hub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;\nconst seed = info ? info.seed >>> 0 : Number(params.get('seed') ?? freshSeed());\nparams.set('seed', String(seed));\n// set game.seat, game.seats, cam=45,42,1.5,1,1 and post.look on params; defineLevel from makeWorld(seed)\nconst startEngine = async () => {\n  engine = await createEngine({ canvas, scenes: ['game:world'], search: `?${params}`, actionMap: 'actionMap:game',\n                                store, modal, onFrame });\n  if (info) engine.inspector.pause();         // from here the lockstep driver alone steps the sim\n};\nif (info) {                                   // a session: card, ready and traffic first, the engine only at the start\n  params.set('gfx.warmUp', 'asked');\n  showCard(info); startLockstep(info); hub.ready();\n  hub.on('start', () => void startEngine());\n} else await startEngine();\n```\n\n- **In a session, `hub.ready()` comes first and `createEngine` waits for `start`. Don't change this order.** Verified the hard\n  way, in this sequence: the harness needs `ready` from every copy within 20 s and ends its default round about 13 s after,\n  and all its copies share one main thread. Calling `ready` after the warm-up failed outright. Calling it before\n  `createEngine` but warming up at once passed on a small build and failed at 4 players on a build with the game's real\n  prop and body counts, because one copy's warm-up starved another's startup. Waiting for `start` passes.\n- **The lockstep driver starts at once too and runs without the engine.** A copy still warming up keeps its stamped log,\n  sends its heartbeat, and catches up by stepping when it is ready; `time-up` and `finish` need no engine at all. On a real\n  GPU the warm-up is a moment, inside the 3 s countdown that follows `start`.\n- **Solo and couch driver:** the engine's normal frame loop, with interpolation, hit-stop and time scale. Extra pads and the\n  hovered target reach the sim through `input.virtual.axis('game:pads', ...)` and `axis('game:target', id)`.\n- **Lockstep driver:** §12.3.\n\n### 3.9 Save, title and restart\n\n- The title screen is HTML over the live world. At boot, the local hero stands in the **Vigil Chapel** class-select stage.\n- **Play** sends a `begin` command with the save's plain data, or the chosen class for a new game; the sim applies it and\n  moves the hero to the start. **Quit to Title** saves and calls `inspector.seed(seed)`. Attract mode uses the same restart\n  to exit.\n- Saves go through `createStore({ prefix, version, migrate })`: three slots plus the shared Poor Box. The sim emits a\n  `game:save` event at Bellposts, zone changes, quest steps and on quit; its payload holds the save; the page writes it.\n- A session's client gives the game an empty storage, so sessions need no special save code.","numLines":145,"startLine":175,"totalLines":1003}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CVn3NREaRrwdFj58SzAaW5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
