{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G3GBk5wBb27SwxLezZzqU7","type":"tool_result","content":"  to exit.\n- Saves go through `createStore({ prefix, version, migrate })`: three slots plus the shared Poor Box. The sim emits a\n  `game:save` event at Bellposts, zone changes, quest steps and on quit; its payload holds the save; the page writes it.\n- A session's client gives the game an empty storage, so sessions need no special save code.\n\n### 3.10 Balance settings\n\n`defineSettings` with `when: 'now'`, read at each use: `game.bal.heroDamage, heroLife, monsterLife, monsterDamage, moveSpeed,\nattackSpeed, cooldown, resourceGen, packDensity, eliteChance, dropRate, rarityBoost, silver, xp, hitStop, knockback,\nawakeCap`, all multipliers defaulting to 1 except `awakeCap` (60). Dev mode shows these as live sliders with a reset. In a\nsession they stay at defaults.\n\n### 12.3 Host-stamped lockstep\n\n**The sim is deterministic, so copies send inputs, not world state. Verified with the harness on its bad network (150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5% loss): two copies matched at every hash checkpoint across 2,339 ticks, and 1, 2, 3 and 4 players, host-leave and \n\nThis is the protocol's shared world: the host's copy decides everything, the others send what their player does and show the world the host's stamped log produces. All effects, ragdolls and sounds work on every copy with no extra code, and there is no snapshot\n\n```ts\n// net/lockstep.ts. Runs from setInterval(drive, 33) always, and from onFrame when frames come.\n// Software rendering starves requestAnimationFrame, and a hidden tab stops it: a frame-only driver froze a client at tick 0.\nfunction drive() {\n  const now = AIHub.time();\n  if (!Number.isFinite(now) || now < 0 || finished) return;\n  const live = engine?.ready;                                     // false while warming up: keep the log, step later\n  if (AIHub.isHost) {\n    const target = Math.floor(now * 60 / 1000);\n    stampLocalInputIfChanged();                                   // { t: tick, s: seat, c: command }, into pending and outbox\n    if (live) stepTo(target);\n    if (now - lastSend >= 66) { lastSend = now; AIHub.send({ t: 'b', to: tick, e: outbox.splice(0), h: maybeHash() }); }\n  } else {\n    sendLocalInputIfChanged();                                    // to the host, at most every 50 ms; a heartbeat every 250 ms\n    if (live) stepTo(stampedTo);\n  }\n}\nfunction stepTo(limit) {\n  let budget = 120;\n  while (tick < limit && budget-- > 0) {\n    while (pending.length && pending[0].t <= tick) applyEntry(pending.shift());\n    engine.inspector.step(1, { 'game:seats': seatsNow });          // clock paused: engine.inspector.pause() once ready\n    clearPresses(seatsNow);                                        // a press lasts one tick\n    tick++;\n    if (tick % 120 === 0 && frameWorld) hashes[tick] = gameHash(frameWorld);\n  }\n}\n// On the host, a 'c' message is stamped with t = tick and goes to pending and outbox. A 'b' message: pending.push(...e); stampedTo = to.\n```\n\n- **Budget:** 20 messages a second a copy, 40 in a burst, 16 KB each. The host sends 15 batches a second, reliable, in order. Commands are small: send held state only when it changes.\n- **Local feel.** A client's input returns after a round trip. On press, immediately show a click marker, skill tile flash and a UI sound; no prediction in the sim.\n- **Menus and cheats:** the command stream (§3.7). Dev mode is off.\n- **Desync check:** the host includes `gameHash` every 2 s; a client that disagrees records it in `window.__net.desyncs`, and tests assert zero. In production, it keeps playing; the host's result stands.\n- **Clock:** `inspector.pause()` once `createEngine` resolves: the engine's loop still samples input and draws, and the driver alone steps. Read input in `onFrame` (`input.strength`, `held`, `pressed`), once a frame, and accumulate. Aim from `screenRay` and `o\n- **Hidden tabs:** the timer keeps stepping (throttled, catching up in chunks). A host hidden for over 20 s voids the round; that is the hub's rule.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Packaging\n\nThe engine's one-file recipe aimed at your page. **Verified: 4 to 12 s, 6.7 MB, opens from `file://`.** Output goes to the deliverable beside the dev folder.\n\n```js\n// labs/game/tools/build-one-file.mjs  <path to the deliverable>\nimport { build } from 'vite';\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nconst root = resolve('.');\nconst result = await build({ root, configFile: false, logLevel: 'warn', base: './',","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d0401-1a79-4c2f-8a33-ba9239fdbfed","uuid":"173162f8-c7a2-4419-ac51-c6e47d155411","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:44:29.458Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  to exit.\n- Saves go through `createStore({ prefix, version, migrate })`: three slots plus the shared Poor Box. The sim emits a\n  `game:save` event at Bellposts, zone changes, quest steps and on quit; its payload holds the save; the page writes it.\n- A session's client gives the game an empty storage, so sessions need no special save code.\n\n### 3.10 Balance settings\n\n`defineSettings` with `when: 'now'`, read at each use: `game.bal.heroDamage, heroLife, monsterLife, monsterDamage, moveSpeed,\nattackSpeed, cooldown, resourceGen, packDensity, eliteChance, dropRate, rarityBoost, silver, xp, hitStop, knockback,\nawakeCap`, all multipliers defaulting to 1 except `awakeCap` (60). Dev mode shows these as live sliders with a reset. In a\nsession they stay at defaults.\n\n### 12.3 Host-stamped lockstep\n\n**The sim is deterministic, so copies send inputs, not world state. Verified with the harness on its bad network (150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5% loss): two copies matched at every hash checkpoint across 2,339 ticks, and 1, 2, 3 and 4 players, host-leave and \n\nThis is the protocol's shared world: the host's copy decides everything, the others send what their player does and show the world the host's stamped log produces. All effects, ragdolls and sounds work on every copy with no extra code, and there is no snapshot\n\n```ts\n// net/lockstep.ts. Runs from setInterval(drive, 33) always, and from onFrame when frames come.\n// Software rendering starves requestAnimationFrame, and a hidden tab stops it: a frame-only driver froze a client at tick 0.\nfunction drive() {\n  const now = AIHub.time();\n  if (!Number.isFinite(now) || now < 0 || finished) return;\n  const live = engine?.ready;                                     // false while warming up: keep the log, step later\n  if (AIHub.isHost) {\n    const target = Math.floor(now * 60 / 1000);\n    stampLocalInputIfChanged();                                   // { t: tick, s: seat, c: command }, into pending and outbox\n    if (live) stepTo(target);\n    if (now - lastSend >= 66) { lastSend = now; AIHub.send({ t: 'b', to: tick, e: outbox.splice(0), h: maybeHash() }); }\n  } else {\n    sendLocalInputIfChanged();                                    // to the host, at most every 50 ms; a heartbeat every 250 ms\n    if (live) stepTo(stampedTo);\n  }\n}\nfunction stepTo(limit) {\n  let budget = 120;\n  while (tick < limit && budget-- > 0) {\n    while (pending.length && pending[0].t <= tick) applyEntry(pending.shift());\n    engine.inspector.step(1, { 'game:seats': seatsNow });          // clock paused: engine.inspector.pause() once ready\n    clearPresses(seatsNow);                                        // a press lasts one tick\n    tick++;\n    if (tick % 120 === 0 && frameWorld) hashes[tick] = gameHash(frameWorld);\n  }\n}\n// On the host, a 'c' message is stamped with t = tick and goes to pending and outbox. A 'b' message: pending.push(...e); stampedTo = to.\n```\n\n- **Budget:** 20 messages a second a copy, 40 in a burst, 16 KB each. The host sends 15 batches a second, reliable, in order. Commands are small: send held state only when it changes.\n- **Local feel.** A client's input returns after a round trip. On press, immediately show a click marker, skill tile flash and a UI sound; no prediction in the sim.\n- **Menus and cheats:** the command stream (§3.7). Dev mode is off.\n- **Desync check:** the host includes `gameHash` every 2 s; a client that disagrees records it in `window.__net.desyncs`, and tests assert zero. In production, it keeps playing; the host's result stands.\n- **Clock:** `inspector.pause()` once `createEngine` resolves: the engine's loop still samples input and draws, and the driver alone steps. Read input in `onFrame` (`input.strength`, `held`, `pressed`), once a frame, and accumulate. Aim from `screenRay` and `o\n- **Hidden tabs:** the timer keeps stepping (throttled, catching up in chunks). A host hidden for over 20 s voids the round; that is the hub's rule.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Packaging\n\nThe engine's one-file recipe aimed at your page. **Verified: 4 to 12 s, 6.7 MB, opens from `file://`.** Output goes to the deliverable beside the dev folder.\n\n```js\n// labs/game/tools/build-one-file.mjs  <path to the deliverable>\nimport { build } from 'vite';\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nconst root = resolve('.');\nconst result = await build({ root, configFile: false, logLevel: 'warn', base: './',","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01G3GBk5wBb27SwxLezZzqU7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjFSnhRwryRYEBkqzgVq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LG5DXt9v8nerCZxzkuJoLS","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211947__x8ab6bc/work/planner-exit-interview.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"It gives away the real decisions (genre, camera, effort, research) and stays strict about what done means: attract mode at 10 seconds, dev mode with live sliders, the hub's automatic check, one quiet test command, exact reporting. That combination let me make firm design calls and still know what every builder has to deliver. The This computer section was also a real help: what is installed, and how to stand in for a controller, a touch device and a second player.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Three things. First, 'Follow the hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the hub's page says a game outside a session plays as it always would and defines no multiplayer there, so I could not tell whether standalone play needs multiplayer at all. I planned couch co-op to cover the stronger reading. Second, 'capture its world, characters, themes and tone' next to 'build an original game': I read that as copying mechanics and mood while inventing every name, character, place, tune and look, and wrote that rule into the plan. Third, the stage does not say whether a planner may run code. I built a throwaway prototype in a scratch folder and deleted it, because the engine's limits decide the architecture and I did not want to hand builders guesses.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Choosing an architecture for builders who cannot ask me anything, where three limits collide: the engine cannot switch scenes in a running page, the hub forbids reloads, and on this machine software rendering runs about one frame a second while the harness loads up to four copies on one main thread. My first answers looked right and failed when tested. Walls as level glyphs took 113 seconds to start, and a session start order that passed on a small build failed at four players on a bigger one. It took repeated prototype runs to reach an order that passes, and then keeping the plan complete without burying that order in detail.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Fix the environment notes first, since they cost me the most time and every builder will hit them. (1) The listed WebGPU flag --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader stalls this engine's warm-up: not ready after 120 s in the headless shell or after 40 s in full Chrome. The engine's own --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader is ready in about 12 s. (2) The run's long TMPDIR makes full Chrome die with 'Socket path too long' and npx tsx die with 'listen EINVAL'. Shorten it, or tell builders to set CHROMIUM_PATH to the headless shell. (3) Say that the engine's node x browser commands need CHROMIUM_PATH here, and that plain npm ci is enough, because scripts/setup.sh downloads a Node and a Chrome outside the run's folder. (4) Say to leave git alone, since the starter's AGENTS.md says to commit and the folder sits inside another repository. Beyond that: state what 'the game on its own' means for multiplayer; say whether a planner may prototype and how long a plan should be; and warn that the hub's harness shares one main thread between copies and wants ready within 20 s, which rules out the obvious startup order for a heavy WebGPU game.\"\n  }\n]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2109,"cache_read_input_tokens":652113,"cache_creation":{"e